      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <p>ODO UGAL PATENT SELF-RAISING FLOUR "IBUT TYLTRY.</p>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER,</p>
        <p>MY DEAR NELLIE.</p>
        <p>Now Easter has passed and we are getting on so far into the spring, the prominent question is head- gear, and the variety of styles to be seen is quite surprising. One is a very elegsat black hat, which has a transparent crown and brim of lace, trimmed in a scalloped design with harrow niches of black chiffon. The tipper side of the. brim and the crown are also trimmed with narrow ruches of chiffon and fancy straw plait. The brim is turned up on the left side with choice black ostrich tips bows of black ribbon velvet, and a beautiful paste buckle, in which are twisted knots of black ribbon velvet and chiffon.</p>
        <p>There is also a hat in the new " Gitana" shape of a charmingly simple design. There is practically no trimming on the hat itself, which is of fancy black straw, as it has only a large rosette of black tulle and a full cream-coloured osprey on the left side; but under the brim, which is somewhat of the toreador shape, there is a drapery of Irish point lace of a deep beige tint, and beneath this, on the left side, resting on the hair, is a wreath of shaded pink roses of the choicest quality. This hat is the perfection of taste, and it is one of the greatest novelties of the season. ___</p>
        <p>If you look in the fashionable papers you will see, especially in the Lady," quite a number of smart walking costumes. One has long sleeves, and tunic skirt in fawn-coloured serge, outlined with fur. Large buttons and stripes in silk and tinsel cord. Revers and underskirt in velvet. Bloupo in turquoise-blue satin or pongee silk. Cravat tie in white silk muslin. The short jacket of black or dark velvet with elaborate revers is being much worn, and those with revers of white satin, completely covered with tiny ruchings of black chiffon or gauze, are exceedingly dressy, especially when supplemented by quantities of soft lace or tulle. Ostrich feather boas are too becom- ing to be discarded without a struggle, but the cheap imitations have made them undeniably com- mon, and it remains to be seen if smart women will be content to share this fashion with the million.</p>
        <p>As to what you said regarding colours generally, what is now being worn is fawn and its kindred tints of antelope, beaver, biscuit, and almond, and the new capes, braided, appliqudd, and otherwise trimmed with white, are mostly in the paler colourings, and lined with white satin, fawn satin, or very pale-coloured glacd silk. Very elegant, too, are jackets in this same style, the basques short, and invariably curved or scalloped, and braided or stitched.</p>
        <p>Very fine afternoon and evening dresses are becoming in evidence now. Peau de soie has for some years been steadily gaining in popularity, and has now become a really fashionable fabric, and has almost taken the place of the corded variety known an poult de soie. In delicate evening tints and in cream and oyster-white It Is an ideal fabric, and the plain skirt is made in a fashion much affected by smart modistes, who modity Parisian styles to suit English tastes. Tao front and side-front gores are eloped straightly in the usual manner, but the back is cut with elongated flutes, which trail in deep Bolds, ahhough the top is perfectly tight and close.</p>
        <p>?:••-d1 1</p>
        <p>The skirt is lined with yellow miffs veiling, and there are three pinked-out frills of yellow glace inside the edge. Groups of lace motifs adorn the skirt, and have a very handsome effect. The silk bodiee is made with a tight back and full front, and over this f,OIEVC.4 a &amp;wave of guipure, edged with is narrow fbral inserion, which makes a perfect fininh. We, are now paying for those mild winter days. and beneath the tifl2,ri ate still -sleeping the early violets and bright jonquils. a'ders had already been given for the brighter costumes of light Liberty cloth, so soft and satiny. These may now repose at the dressendter's, awaiting the more genial weather. The same may be said of the lovely lace and chitin dresNes which are to he worn at the balls. Terre ie.a general t.ndeney towards an exaggerated comp!iettien of l'uncee and tunics over inordinately heti* and flAr. skirts. Some few people have escaped this contagion, maintaining persistently the nail:lonians Ikea of the female form.</p>
        <p>Your loving</p>
        <p>JENNIE.</p>
        <p>SAVOIMY OMELETTE.— Lle a batter with three yolks anti one wAiiv-56;:f with one tablespoonful of flour, which must be dry and free from lumps, and one large tablespoonial of water. Add to this a tablespoonful of minced pardley, one chopped shallot, and a little pepper, salt, sod grated nut. meg, with a dessertspoonful of grated lean ham. Put a piece of butter the size of a nutmeg into a very clean omelette pin, and when thoroughly heated pour in the batter. When the underside is set, and of a golden colour, take the pan from the stove and hold it in front of a bright fire until it rises at least a couple of inches; thou wrap it over once aad send to table quickly.</p>
