        <head>"WOMEN'S CHAT." –</head>
        <p>"WOMEN'S CHAT." -</p>
        <p>The Queen has always enjoyed a good game of cards, and frequently after dinner, when no guests are "coin- mended," indulges in whist, or the old-fashioned game of Patience. The Prince of Wales is agree: whit player and frequently wbiles away an hour playing Patience. It is believed on god authority, that playing cards were inyentld in India, and thence found their way through Italy and Spain' into Western Europe. The first positive mention of cards is in the works of Covelluzzo, a writer of the 15th century,who states that "the gatno of cards, which Comes from the country of the Sarcens, wag hrought into Viterbo (in Italy) in 1379." Another unmistakable ref,rence to them occurs in the accounts for the year 1392,0 f the to asurer of tee homehold of the French King Charles VI., where an entry appears of the payment of fifty-stx Parisian sots, to one Jaequemin Gringonneur, for three uacks of cards for the king's use. Only five years after this date the provost of Paris made an edict forbidding working people to play at cards and certain other games on wot k days. It is therefore We to aesume that the use of them became general in France about the end of. the 14th century. Within a very chart time of this, if net before, they also came into common use throuehout the greater part of Europe. If the custom of " calling" were abolished, what a let of time society women would have on their bands, that surely neght be very much better employed. There is little sincerity in the present mode. The average caller delight 3 in a series ot " outs" when on her round, and everyone found at home is mentally termed a bore and a nuisance. But as roaters stand there is no help for it. "Calling "is a stern duty to those women who would fain hold their own in sociity. It is a duty they owe to themselves, to their neighbours, to their hus- bands and to their children. The numter of fashioneble weddings arranged to take place this season is quite rernatk.ble. and the circula- of " gifts " will be coes dereble. If only same seetem could be followed in the eiving of wedding presents, what an endless amount of disappointment :tliirdicity, and fibbery would ho averted. Dv, joke abeute the bride who received thirteen butter dishes is too well-known to raise a laugh now, bot one cennot help thinking that the unlucky recipient must have been tempted to return the Rift together with a etrone, sxpre,eion of feeling on the iubjeet et butter dishes, rather than write the polite end utterly untrue note of thanks that custana de- manded.</p>
        <p>The discuvsion cencerring theap.,e at which girlashould marry,stiil prcceeds merrily in one of our leading maga- zines. Tfe in.j.)rity of wAters are in f4vour of non - haste. desming twenty-five to thirty the moit suitable age. " John Strange Winter" hlwsver, takes an ocip site view of the raitt.eroYd advoTates quiteearly Ste la of the opinion tht the sooner girl marries the better, pro%hird she is phyi,ically and merit $.11? grown up. But, how many wris are mentally grown up. a%., say, eighteen to twenty ? At eighteen, one can have had little txperier-ce of the world, ard a marriage made at that as is often the cause of mush misery to all con- cerned.</p>
        <p>_ In Portn7al, a boy elf fonttern is cnnsi•lend marriare- able,and a"woman"of twelve and in Switzerlancl,Gre,c and Spain, the same law obtains. In Russia and &amp;roily, a youth rsu:t refrain fr mr. • nteripg into matrimony, nntil he is eighteen years of age, and a woman sixteen. In Germany, eiehteen is the age fired, and in France and Belgium the man must be eighteen and the wnman fourterfu. In Turk.v, any boy and maiden will c.irr walk, and understand the religious service, are a:lowed to he united for life. . _. _ _</p>
        <p>„.. The fashionable way in which to wear a watch is to attach it to a long chain, and le: it hanv from :his. The public annexer will rejoice over the mode, and probably roan a constierahle harvest while it lasts. )wer msnufscturers bid fair to do sooll this season, as littla else r. an blo• my are emplord for the neweAt teques. and hats fashiorahle all bosst something in the way of flowers—more or less. It does not though, seem necessary to io 1 V 7 the dictates as nature as to colour, t,r these blossoms. Gown and black reiss are quite fashiona.hle wear, and I ha.:e seen tome deep purple rose s with lesys exactly the sime colour, used for head- gear. All hats drop downwards ever the face, and the effect is b,c^ming to a'mott everyone.</p>
        <p>-6 lite the try's-, fa.hionahle slkhis coming swim:sr will b. foulard. It is srmowliat difficolt to account for its cortineed nopularity ai it isnot a rich !coking fabric, and unl.Fs elabirately trimmed and perfectly cut has no more Pffect thin a colourel cotton. It is mnreoveronly suitable for q ate youig eirls or really m'ddl.-age matron'- wo,r. To a ic-mto past her first piuth it gives an elderly look and this ii cnt.ioly not desirahlo.</p>
        <p>A capital way in which tei eggqii a llow; : Cot a middle i•izNi onion in dice, fry in a PtAcrpan a pit .of butt.r a light brown, add a teaapoonful of in gar when &amp;In.. fhen bftr a dish spread the onion over it, brea:c the egg. 4 jilt) i and place the e.ish in tin ova,. Worm the ee ar • set strew thorn ove: with frit.d bnadcrumhs, and ervi ver y hot.</p>
