      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>'WARRINGTON' TOV7II COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>'WARRINGTON' TOV7II COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>A meeting of this Council was held on Tuesday even- log. The Mayor (Alderman Wright) presided, and there were also present : Alderman Fairclough (ex-Maror. ,hddermon Sutton, Ronerts, Councillors Shav Green, Reston, Smethursr,Bennet% Longton,.Tohn Evans. James Evans, L. Ellison, Monks, Jolley, Shillcock, Rogers, Peacock, Burton, John Roberts, J. Lee, Bairi, Tinnion, JOhll Croslield. F. R. Robeitt, A. Woods, Cannel', with Mr. J. Lyon Whittle (town clerk). Mr. Stanley Wilson (deputy town clerk), Mr. Fidrburst (borough treasurer), Mr.Deaa (borough engineer),Mr.Hadduck (gas engineer); . ?dr. T. Lorigilin (borough surveyor). The minures of ths previous meting and those of the Watch Committee were °scrod without comment. STREET IMPROVEMENT —THE LYME-STREET</p>
        <p>CORNER. AN "UTTERLY ILLOGICAL" COMMITTEE. Alderman SUTTON pr :posed the adoption of the Illinutes of the street Improvement and Lighting.Corn- ntittee, and mentioned that the property in Chrtmes% lard, Rank-theet, to be pulled down, was purchased for WU purpose. The Finance and Estate Committee had been requested by their Committee to pull down the Property at the corner of Lvme-street and Horsemerket- street for the purpose of widening th 3 thoroughfare. Ile understood that the Finance Committee could not eLe4InPIY with the rsquest of tee Street Improvement '"lnmittee' as they eould not afford to lose a rent of About £lOO which was derived from the property. COLIECiBOT LONGTON seconee i the motion. .`,Councilleni Semen:me Said be was surprised to hear tie reramks of the Chairman with reference to the egnance Committee notseeing their way to pull down the rePerty at the corner of Lyme-street. The matter had (men ned3r the notice of the Council ever since he had been a member of it. A necessity for a change existed, and he could not, I:.herefore, understand at all why the 142inmittee should object to pull down the property even 2 it were bringing in five times the amount of money it 140 w did. He supposed the committee were waiting until ...Mew Iseeple were killed in the street,and then the matter lionlrl be taken into cerrideration. He had On several accidents in the street. and had it not been for the tenants of the shops rushing outside and stopping run- ' ateav horses, accidents of a serious nature would have 2courred. lie felt warmly about the matter. Only a 'ew months ago the son of a member of the Town Coun- cil (Alderman Roberts) hadsa narrow escape from being killed. The other day Mr 'Hickman had a conversation with him about the condition of Hossemarket-street,end he said its present coodition was a disgrace to the town, and that if a deputation of ratepasers could be formed he would be glad to join it to see if something could be done by the Council, especially as a man had ha I a barrow escape frcee being killed there the other day. The street was contently bleched. The Finance and 'other ceramit'ees could spend thousands of pounds on be widening of Wilderspool and the Knutstord-reads. it was proposed to widen a street at the end of the \fleeGas Works, and yet they had a piece of property in T,l)feemarket-street which was bringing in only £lOO or ezeso,which was to be untouched. It was a disgrace to Thpown to allow a building to project about nine feet. lie- hoped that the matter would not be allowed to rest eirViere it was, but that every member of the Council who flew anything about it would veto dead against the ; inete, and have the place pollee down,if it cost .-2.1,000 A luding to some sotto voce remarks of the Deputy Mayor e apeeker said he was as independent of Mews. eg"r, eiton's as enrolee in the room. He had introduced enie matter betore he was a shareholder of Sqelton's &amp;limited. The Mayon: Quite right. Councillor BENNETT supported Councillor Smethuret'e . :IVAestion, and said that if teens was one nartisular :Pet which was a source of danger and public incen- veeience it was that particular spot, and if there was Zne place for the demolition of which arguments could _et easily addlicsd it was the property in question. Ho 'lid tot agree with the Finance Committee, and he thought they were utterly i'logical. Tee premises which *ere being taken down in Bridge-street brought in a eonsiderable revenue, but if they were to act on this PritleiPle there would be no public improvements at all. lie did not think there was a piece in town where they Could get such a substantial improvement for such a Paltry expense as at the corner of Lyme-street. The minutes were confirmed HEALTH COMMITTEE. TUBERCULOSIS. . DAIRIES, COWSHEDS, AND MILESHOPS. e at a meeting of the Heal, h Committee an order made eee? the Local Government Board was read, altering the suairies, Cowsheds, and Milkshops Order by including In the a xoression "disease "in that order in case of a "Vr such disease: of the udder as shall be certified by a veterinary surgeon to he tuberetslar. It was resolved that at the request of the Local Government Board, new r,Nolations with respect to dairies, cowsheds, and milk- be made. The committee have resolved thst the Member for the Borough be requested to support the fapvement now being made for a quinquennial census ftbstead of a decenniel one. At a meeting held on the ptia April,it was resolved that the Council be recomenenti- eed to adopt the Infectious Diseases (Notification) Act, 1889. I Alderman SUTTON ir oved the confirmation of the nntes and remarked-that the committe 3 had no desire unduly persecute or deal harshly with purveyors of '!eiilk, but it was considered desirable in the interests of fate publics that there should be a proper system of in- 'spew ion to ensure perfect cleanliness awl t) prevent the sPread of disease. The tuberculosis question was an im- portant one, and it had engaged the attention of the committee for some time. The report of Dr. Gelman gave the result of the conference in Manchester and tendon. On the question of the census it was hsped 13130 would be taken in future every five years inetrad of tvery ten years. It would be an advantage if an altera- tion could be made. Alderman ROBERTS seconded, and the motion was Carried. 841NITARY WORKS COMMITTEE—AN ECONOMICAL</p>
