        <p>TION OF MESSRS. CROSFIELD. VINDICATION OF THE RIVERS commirrEE's POSITION.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Rivera Committee a letter was read from the Menctorster Ship Canal Company intimat- ing that arrangements had been male between the Canal Company and Mr. Willisne Griffi.:ha, contractor, for cerryine out the whole f toe dreigine work requiree under the agreem eat of 1893 between the C3rparation and the Company. Alderman ROBERTS moved the adoption of the minutes. He said the estimate this year was practically double what it was last year, thoueh it was nos a big estimate. The increase was brought abeut by the fa .Ir, that the Council sanctioned recently the recommendation of the Committee to appoint a river inspector, whose du'ies he explained at the time. He thought it was desirable t 3 make a few comments upon the case heard a few days ago at the Daresbery Ss ions, in which Messrs. Cresfield and Sons were prosecuted by the Mersey Conservancy for certain alleged off maces. He thought the Rivers' Corn mio.tea had a just cause of com- plaint in the want of courtesy shown to them by the Mersey Conservancy Board, inasmuch as up to a few weeks ago the Rivers Committee believe d everything was being done to the setisfantion of the C onservat or, Admiral Naves, with whom they desired to work in per- fect amity, and that no offeoce such as that for which Mesnes. Cresfield's had been summoned was being com- mitted. To strengthen their hinds the Rivers C ornmittee had employed a man for some months past to make daily observations in read to the state of the tides and the operations of the various sewers which dischareel i ate the river, and cc no cecasion, with one very slight ex- ception, had anything in the nature of an offence been dice overed. They were therefore surprised and in some respec's annoyed when they receivei a cominunien.t,oe a few weeks ago from Admiral Nares informing them that he had taken legal proceedings against a firm who were alleged to have committed certain offeneee. roe com- mittee asked for the name of the firm and the nature and the extent of the allege I offences, in order that they might personally investigate teem by the examina- tion .1 teeir man's log bock, and in also other way.; but the only reply they received was to the effect that the case was already in the hands of the solicitors of the Conservancy.and could not now be dealt with in any other way. It must be blrne in mind that the sole cause of the possibilitye of this complaint hai been the con- struction of the Manchester Ship Cenal,because since the canal was constructed the volume and effect of fool waters and tid at waters had baton con- siderable reduced. Previous to the construc- tion of the Ship Canal all deposits were swept away by the operations to which he had just referred. There wee no doubt whatever that the Mersey Conser- vancy as Teem ded this pro,eeeution were in touch with the Manchester Ship Canal Compiny,whe were liabee for the dreading operations imposed upon them by Parea• merit, by which they were complied to maintain the navigable condition of the river, w hies tee abstraction of water, caused by the construction of the Ship Canal, would, it was anticipated, seriously endanger. This anticipaeioe had since proved t be only too correct. While stricqy within the r leeal rights in taking the case D tresbury instead of Warrington, it would have been fairer to all parties if it hal been tried by jestices who had a bettor acquaintance with the history of the condi- tions of the rimer for the past ten or twelve years than the Daresbury justices crafd piles bly have. The decision of the Daresbury jasticei was a grievous one, and con trary to the weight of evidence, inasmuch as it was clearly proved, that on the days Is pan which the alleged offences were said to have occurred euce a thing was absolutely iinpeasible.ae the pipe which was said to have conveyed the sludge from the !fine presses into the r;ver was at that time,and so had been f or months previously, entirely disconnected from the lime press es, and blocked up in such a way as to make the conveyance of lime through the pipe a physical and mvolia,eical imposihil- ity. elesers. Cresfields had been punish° most unjustiy for offences which were not before the Courteand which occurred prior to the dates alleged upon the summonses, and as in the committee's opinion they had done every- thing pessib'e by the erection of the moat complete filter plant, at a engt of.over £5,000, which was under com- petent supervision, to renler imp oibte such off onces as those for which they were fined at D ireebury, they deserved and ouseet to have received an acquittal. This plant was insprcted months ago by the Rivers Committee, who saw it workine and were satisfied with its thorough efficiency, and the logbook of the watch- man employed be the ceminittoe, which contained his daily observations for several months pat, hors out the statement he had merle. In this cane, at any r ots, while the Manchester Ship Canal Company had roc eyed at the hands of the Corporation the mast sympathetic and favourable consideration in reeard to their dredging obligations, they had net shown a similar seirit in deal- lee with a firm who had evinced by their large capital expenditure and in many other ways their desire to comply with the law and to avoid anything in the shape of miecondect as affectine the navigable condition of the river. The Rivers Coinmitt,e, while on the one hand anxious and determined to prevent any offence such as was alleged in the present case, were at the same time equally determined, so far as they could, to prevent anything in the shape of persecution of War- rington manufaceurers, whose rights aed privileges were of the greatest peesible importance to them and to , the borough al a whole. In conclusion, Alderm on Roberts said that an apeeal against the migistratea' de- cision was not likely to be made by the firm. • Councillor HRA.TON, in secendineesaid he could endorse a great deal that Alderman Reeerts hail sail. He was an old member Of the committee, and he knew the Com- mittee's ilea had been that as little nuisance as possible should exist in the river. They had bad the outlets watched, and else had had trap; with a view to decid- ing whether solid matter was salt into the river. The commitnes fuse been anxious to make the river what it should he.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR said that from what he had seen ho be- lieved that Messrs. Crosfield hai done all in their power to prevent any nuial.nce. Hs was not going to express any opinion unon the decision of the m3gi4-ates. Alderman SUTTON fttifi it was al ways a delicate and difficult thing. to criticize the decision of a bench of magistrates, bat after visiting Messrs.. Crosdeld's works and suing what they had done to prevent any nuisance and loarninfr what be did on that ecnalon, he was rather astonished at the decision the magistrates cams tn. The minutes were annroved. WATER COMMIT TEE.--THE NEW ASSE9SNIENT The Water Committee resolved 044 the tender of the Natinlal Telephone Company to connect the cottag, at Anclon with the telephone system, subject to the Coy. poration entering into an agreemlnt for seven years b ) accepted. Councillor Lee moved the confirmation of the minutes, and stated that the Assessmont Committee had threatened them with a serious increase of assessment, which the cammittee were determined to contest. Alderman ROBCRTS seconded, and the minutes were approved. ELECTRIC LIGHTING.</p>
