        <p>THE SITE FOR THE OLIVER CRONIRELL STATUE</p>
        <p>The General Purpose Commit"e, r?corntneaded that the offer of Mr. Joen Waite to sell the land in Museum- street, adjoining the County Court and beeind the Gymnasium and Parr Hall, ag a site f3r a Teehnicel Institute, be accepted. It having been reported that the title of ;Meer Cromwell, presented by Mr. F. Monks had arrived, and that the Town Hall, Park, and Baths Committee was unable to recommend a suitable site within the gardens under their control, the General Purposes Committee reselved that the statue be erected in front of the ad Academy at Bridge Foot. The centre of the base to be set in line of the exeting gate, woe the necessary alterations ;nade to the paliearling and the approach to the Academe. It was also resolved that a petition toe presented to Perliement in favour of the rat- ing of grouad values : and thae the si.ze for the Walker fountain in front of the Town Hall, as recetnneended be the Town Hall. Park and Baths Committee he approve 1. Councillor BENNETT said he wished to protest against two of the resolutions. Otte was that of the site for the Technical Insti-ute. Re did no: propose to say enyteing beyond recording his protest. Tnea with reeard to the site for the W,ilker fountain, he was afraid Ur-3r would do a good deal to spoil the appearance of their beautiful Town Hall. With respect to tee re iolutien regarding the Cromwell statue he proposed an amendment that it be erected in front of the Old Aea.cleme, and setinline with the curb. He thought the position chosen by the com- mittee was the worst they could have selected. Tee Warrington soreete was unanimoue in tleh opoosition to the proposal made by the Corporation, and he thoeget ! that society was entitled to s ono considerathn. It bad ! by a considerable expenditure very much improved the Corporation property and made the Academy a worehe memento of Old Warrington. fl. qeestioaed whether the Corporation were not exceeding their rights by erect- jug the statue on some portion of the garden in front • the Academy. He believed the Borough Surveyor was of opinion that the etattee coaled be erected wieh ut en- croaching upon the _garden of the Academy. Tao only arguments he had heard in leveler of the site suggested were that the statue would be protected by the railings, and it would not encroach on the roadway. He had sufficient , faith in the law Welding iestincts of Warrington people that the statue woul I not require the protection of the railings, and &amp;se sufficient fee h ha• the teamsmee and driver of vollicles that the 7 would ' net run into it. As to the encroachment he must say ,1 that he was glad tee committee had at last come retied to his opinion that the wiith of the road at Bridge Foot was toe narrow. But the particular occasion on whieh 1 they had shown their z ;al for wide streets was an un- happy one and he did not appreciate it. He still said that Bridge•street was too narroer is its wide-t part,and future generations would declare who were the bate and moles of the Council and who were unalele to see in broad 1 daylight. The zeal of the eommittee for a Wile street on the present occasion seemed to be ohjectless. Mr. Lenedin had prepared a pthleenliwnehioeellstsrh,?oewl wedontilliatnowth,bene foot if the statue wereerected itnh tee br frei rdeele wwiatsh widened, dae nsei nag, p ma the Rite he su v.:tested. The committee fled been un- reasoneb'e in the matter,beceuse tht mernbors ehsolutele declined to look at the plans. The site terms-steel had been decided in a spirit of petntence —see all minded netulance—and he did not think it had heel fairly con- sidered. If it had been consideree the members would 838 that the very moderate request he now mule had a ; good deal of sound sense aed wisdom in it. Alderman Rosettes eeconded the amendment. In do- • ing so he wished to dlssocia'e himself from the strong Language which Ceuncillor Bennett had used in regard to it. Re did net know whether he was present at the meeting referred to— Couneillor BENNET!' It was the Street Improvement Committee I refereed to. Alderman ROBRRTS : The resolution berore them was fro:n the General Purposes Committee. He was im- pressed very favourably indeed with the way the General Purposes Commi!tee considered the question of the statue, and he could not recall to his mind any mem- ber who expressed himself in such a way as to justify the very etr ono remark; of Councillor Bennett. Councillor BENNETT : I do h t blame the Genet -.1 Purposes Committee. Alderman ROBERTS It would have been better to distinguish between the two committees. After the little breeze, when the matter was sttled in the Coancli Chamber, be had been exceedingly gratified to notice the very careful manner in which every consideration had been given to the proposal as to a site, and he thought that if Councillor Bennett had edepted soother coarse of speech, he would have had a very good chance of persuading the Council in a spirit of sweet reasonable- ness to grant what he desired. It was decided by the committee that the Mayor aid some a'presentatives of the Warrington Society should go to the site and consi- der it on all points. , The MAYOR : I did all that. aiderrnan ROBERTS: I did not know von had met Mr. Line with a view to considering the question; If it is not vital to your views you might moet us by making the statue stand eut in the way saggested. The Meeron I cam only say that lam not going to take the respOlsihilitv on my shoulder's. If the Coined decides on a new position it must tak 3 th” responsibi- li ty._ _</p>
        <p>ilderm,n 113narrrs Is there any reasonable objection ? Th. 9 MAYOR :I WR,9 a rmsmab!e objliction raisli by Councillor Jamas Evans ; that of the obitruction of the street.</p>
        <p>Ald3rman ROIMRTS : I knnw his 'ability aq a hor-n- -man, and h 3 would ba the very last mu who ve,ts likely to damage the Ft it,io by runnine into it with a horse and trap. (Laughter.) I cannot see that then will be the sliehtest obstruction.</p>
        <p>Thf3 MAYOR : Pahang not at tp, nrPsent time, but if the bridge we widPnod there would he.</p>
        <p>Alderiiiau ROMMTS said there were many centres where the traffics was greater than at Warrinztnn Bridge and no such obstruc`ion obtained. He thought ttie proposal was a reasonable one, and one they ought to try and nioet. Councilor JAWS EYANs sai I tho difficulty with rwee.l3 to the traffic was not on the Aca4e,ny side of the exee'. bu.`, on the opposite side. It was very difficult for lurrhs to turn out of Mersey-street and go ov3r the Bridge.</p>
        <p>_ Couneillor. Elks:mei elid he hed seen the statu t which wa te be erected, and was of opinion that the site selected by the committee was the best. Re etative of Cromwell at Manchester divides the traffia more thau anything else. (Laughter.) The MAYOR: It is very useful in that position. (Laughtt,r.) Alderman ROBERTS 15 not the fi-st time he his divided traffic. (Laughter.) Councillor Hee.rote said if they had hal the maniere width of street at Market Gate it would have been a very good place to put the statue. (Laughter.) • Councillor TINNION thought it would guide the Coun- cil in their decisbe if the. Mayor woull give them 'us opinion as to the desirableness of the site suggeited. Alderman SUTTON said he cou'd not help remarking, and he hoped Councillor Beanett would etcuse him Er using the expreesion, that he was a very queer flab to deal with alai very difficult ta understand. (Laughter.) Hiev!e ars in some cases were so decided that he could not be pereuaged to give way under any oonlideration whilst in other similar cases he em entirely c3unter his own views and expressions. Re was altogether in- consistent. (Ileer, hear.) - Councillor BENNETT : IC is you who an inconsistent. (Bear, hear.) Ald nenan SUTTON (COnthrlinZ) said ne member of the Strew; Imnravement Comm'ttee was so persistent in crying ous for the widening of streets,but when it muted his purpole he came up tending and without any hesi- tation or diffidence, entirely upset all hi; pet schemes and sentiments. (Laughter.) At the present time he was advocating a street to be encroach d upon by a statae, whilst at ce:her times there ivia no man erh) had a Ereater or more decided objection to any street being interfered witn in that way. The Mame : You must respect his feelings. Alderman SUTTON : I don't think any nnint)er is jue- tid coming forward in sapo,or; of a certain thing and at another speaking against it. Couneiller IiPINNETT : That is jut what all the "neem• bees, of the Street Im