        <p>Councillor F. R. ROBERT 3 said the statue could easily be removed if it was found, after ereci in, to be in an undesirable position: He did not think it was worth while spending any more time on the matter. If the statue did encroach on the garden in front of the Academy, the Warrington Society could, if it chose, be disagreaable. The Corporation had no right to go into aeyone's garden and erect a statu,, so that he hoped if the resolution were passed that the members of the War- ring.ton Society would not get their back op. Councillor &amp;taw GRERN sail aft n. examining the plans prepared by Xr. Longdin, he was coovinced there would tie no obstruction if the statue were placed as Councillor Bennett suggeited. The MAYOR said he should have preferred to have hen left out of the discussion, He had no very etrong opinions on the matter, bat he thought every coneidr,r a- tion hal been given to the male: to; tne G~nral Fr poses Committie. He saw no reason, so far as he was personally concerned, to alter the decision arriv.,cl at. A vote was then taken but only four—Aldo:n:l%n Roberti. Councillors Benn-tt, F R RohArts, an.l Shaw Gram—voted for the amendment, and the minutes were then passed. THE BITING OF GROUND VALUE 3. LIVELY DISCUSSION.</p>
        <p>Alderman SUTTON said he was very mucti astonished when he came to look over the Council .r.ummonii and found that the Finance Oomtnittee hid req ivi=t!d the General Purpose] Commit'ee- to present a cotition to Ptrliament in favour of the ratinz of ground values. (Rear. hear.) The Giaeral Par,iosa COMmitto? at, po3,red to have done without further ceremony, ev-.ctly what tiev were told to do. Councilor &amp;taw GRIM : it ill not often they do so.</p>
        <p>Alderman SurrON said he felt the iinuortance of question so much that he had considered it very well over and made notes on the points whian no would en- deavour wi h their permission to exp'ain. First of all in r3.lard to such an linoartant miasure ort wlic a there were so many diffe,,nees of opinion am )1.01 cabia.t ministers and rnembirs of the House of Commons, the leas: that mmaabers of the Carporation.uould do was to mak?! tnemsetves fully acquainted with all detai s res- pectias: it before attem,)ting to pass any resolutions upo it. Before multi a reaohuion was bout forward, the notice conveniqg the Committee meting ought to have stated what was intended.</p>
        <p>Aiderman ROBBEITS : Why? • Alderman SUTTON eta ed that when the Finance Com- mittee preheated its report to the Council on the subj.n; not a word was speken of their intention t) proceed further in the matter, and he (Aldermen Sutton) ems to the conclusion that it was net their intention to do so. Red he kiown that the question was to be dealt with by the General Purpose; Corn nittee he should certainly have attended that meet- ing althoueh it might have been very inconvenient to do so. But he understeoe that the resolution was pegged with scarcely a word of comment, and he did not thiele they should deal with a matter of such importa-ice IS sa loom a way. It simply cams to this that whereas tee legislature of this country reearded the question to be of im nense imoottence requirine the trio t careful con- aid station, the Warrington Cerporetio a in its sup nor wisdom—(lauehter and hear, hear)—Wae able to settle the rn atter in le;s than five minute eand did nrt hesitate t) petition the G•rearem ne:: that ground landlords should he male to centribute to local taxation in proportion to the velue of 1411-1. (Hear hear.) Councillor Tinenote : It is very sensib!e. Alderman SarroN said public opinion was very much divilel upon the reticle of land values. Many ;valuable arguments had been advanced bti pro and eon, but what would apply in sem, towns would not apply in ethers. The Alderman proceeded to defend the action coc landowners in refusing t) let their land in c ;nein cases. If a lani- namo,. held hick land in order that a better class of property m;ght be erected it produced a bieher rental, and conseqeentiv more nets. So far as Warningtiu was coneerned the cpeetise affectol them very little bela,use there was ;Jilt much vacant land for building purpeses within the Borough, and it was theref ore no; necessary for them to in-eorfere. With marl to the point of rating ground values, apart frona the hardships which would cenetantly be preserVerl in cennettion with ineeert 'hie aid inee Age. tent methods of esiessine the or )p ee;ies. in any diffiul tine would he forthcoming. He we rid ask why ehnold they always be down on the ground landlords. (Hair, hear.)</p>
        <p>A Voice: That is the first time. (Hew*, hear.) Alderman Surrom : I think the lieeislature has dealt with them seriously enough. Why should not supeerters of inell texeti on go further to compel paymint of rates by the owner; of unoceeopied premisee whe peril ,ted in keeping, their Premises ernetv until they receive I exorbit- ant rents. (Hear, hear.) Why not go a steo further lei carnival all property owners who were making their 20, 50 and sometimes .70 oe 80 per cent contribute a higher Reel t) the rates? Whet was sauce for the go ice wee (MC for the gander. Many other argemeet; could be advaeced hut ke contended that the question was not a municipal one bet an imperial one, and that therefore they h I no right to interfere. He certainly thoueht that to pass a resolution on guts an imoertent question whithoist any inrima,tion hayine preyieusly bele given Wte very improper. Ele would propose an ernondment that the m'nutes be passed with the excep- tion of that relating to the rating of ground values. Councillor HEATON seconded the amendment. He said that at the Goneral Purposes Committee meeting when the rerolution was proposed, one half of the committee were ready to leave the room.end it was passed in a veil, hasty miner. He felt eer:ein that it was a matter re- qeirine more cln.i leroti on than it had had. C.inec'llor SAM, Greggle said a few words were due from the Finance Committ.e. The Borouele Treasurer and himself attended the imnortant conference of muni- cipal representatives to consider the advisability of find- ing new sewn.; of revenue for municipalities. The chief means of obtaining the new sources of revenue were considered ,to be from the enhanced value of land in Vail municipality owing to the works and improvements of the municipality. Important Fplechee were made by members of all shades om peliticsl opinion and the came to one unanimous resolution that an applieatien to Parliament should be made advocating the taxation of erceend values. Os returning hem% he and the Borough T,easnrer reported in proper words, withent betraying any bias, about what had happenee. nce then an application had been presented to the Finance Committee that they should petition in favour of the scheme. It veal put before the whole Council, viz . the General Purposes Committee. Alderman &amp;Tyros : Without any intimation of what was corning on. _ _</p>
        <p>Councillor SIIAW GREEg : lam not reipensihle and I am sure the Town Clerk is not. It is the d sty of mem- bers of the Cenncil fon-rain?: the General Purposes Com- mittee to keep themselva acquainted with what is going on. (near. hear.) I have no big whatever. It is a very important and serious qaestioe, and no aheu% which difficalties are like'y to arise. There are 21.0 Cor- poratiens which have sent petitions in favour of the proposition. Alderman ROBERTS said they -a Pre MI in the same boat as A.ld.rman Satton with regard to this business being brouz.ht forward. It was a nflection on the General Purposes Committee to say that the motion Was passed in a hurry. It was passed because everybody prelent was convinced of the reasonableness of the pro- posal It was a pi,•ce of utter winger.° to say that the muter was not understood. The principle of the thing was tinderitood. T.) so:ve the difficulties of detail and legMatioe necessary t) embody that principle was not what they were there for. They were there to urge principles of legislation, and it was for the Government to embody those principles in legislation. E gory town especially the grswing towns. incorporated agricul- tural land which immediately increased in value. If the owners were prepared to let it for huild- ing, purposes at agricultural prices there worfd be every- thing in favour of the ground landlord net hearing the municipal government's taxes. The moment it was wanted for building purposes it was sold at five or ten times its agricultu -al value. The man who Tat the becks and mortar upon it, and the municipality. contri- buted to the increased value of the land. Was it fair that they. sheuid put this money into the landlord', peck-,t and also bear the burden of the taocation al well? All man SUTTON: The same old cry! Alderman Roberts : I can u-idertand peonle c ming here and t tking the part of interested individuals.</p>
        <p>Alderman SUTTON; lam corning here on the behalf of nobody but myself and the Pudic. (iJa.ughter.) Alderman ROBERTS: I make no reflections. The principle of th, thing is this: land form,rlv agricultural land, is incorporatel withi i the municipal area and be- comes building land. It increases in value thro igh nothing that the original owner has done Was he to reap all the benefit alai pay nothing towvd I the taxa- tion of the own?; It was unfair and unjust. He had no hesitation in saying that there wait no land held back from builders because the land owner desired a c,rtain class of properly. It was held back until the value of the land had reached a figure which justified the owner in letting it. It was ridiculous to tell them. as business men, that such a case at thtt was ever on the cards. They knew to the 'contra,y. The Council would b wise in voting for the principb• con- tamed in the petition, which was supperted by Conser- vatives as well as Liberals. Counciller Lomnrom said they had had an illustration of feeling from Councillor Bennett renukol by Al lerman Ruben% hit he thought it hardly soun led well of the latter gentl .men to deliver an impacsiorred and bitter attack on landlords as h 3 had done just new. Alderman ROBCRTS : I did not do anythinz of the SOrt.</p>
        <p>Councillor LONGTON : That is the irnp"eision it made on my mind.</p>
        <p>Allvlnln ROBERTS: That shows what your mind is capable of.</p>
        <p>Councillor LoNarom : I did not ttlink Aldnrman Illbirts would 4ccu4n 1113 of mliciac, mralna.l attacki.</p>
        <p>Alderman Roswell : I have given a distinct dis- Clainl.r to what you ail% CotIOCillOT LONGT)N : I WIA Tint rafstring to pur Te• mark, razardinl. Aldqrman Sutton. Alderman R)BERTS : Councillor Loag`ol said I made an impassioned and bitter attack OR landlords. Cmnaillor LORGTON : , And tha landlord interest. Alderman ROBERTA : Well I disclaim it.</p>
        <p>Councillor Loxo-rox said be thought the QtiVitiol was a far reaching awl diffilut one. for a belly cf commercial mon such as tlie Warrington Town Cooacil to discuss and delide upon in a hurried manner. It might be very disastrous and unjust. He would never vote so long as he live I for anything he bslieved ta ba unjust and dishonest. H 3 believed taxation of ground rents would be a robbery of the widow and the f atherless in many in,tances. (A.ldertnau Sntton : Hoar, hear.) He thought they were going beyond their scone in dealing. with till question. It was an imperial question and one they might very properly have a special meeting to die. Coss.</p>
        <p>The MAYOB: lam in sympathy with a good deal Galloonlor Lrmeos has said.</p>
        <p>Councillor PLuairron said he would like to say a word or two upon this qaestion,which he had considered long before the General Purposes Committee had anything to do with it. Where he resided and where his works were situated, the land was agricultural land 35 years ago, and since then it had beea built upol. Tdo landlord had a security in the property for his gnund rent. There was not one landowner he knew in the bormgh who paid his fair share of the Poor Rate and Improvement Rate. This was the question of the Ilhour and the ques- tion of the future. If they studied the interests of the town they would try to get the land taxed its farr share. He did net see why a landownPr should go soot free and pay nothin4 towards the rates of the Barouh. He knew land which was once let at 10 a yard and now was lot at 61 a yard, and the landlord had done nohing to increase its value. fie thought they could fairly leave too landowners to look after their own interests without them baking after their interests far them.</p>
        <p>Councillor SMETHURST supported the amendment be- cause he felt it was unjust to force the resolution upon this council. rhey had a government trying to do the best for the vvorkingenen of the country. (" Oh ") It was not the landowner who got the benefit of the in creased value of the land but the builder who bought the land from the landowners and then put 3d. or 4d. on it. The landowner did not force anyone to go and take his land. This little chamber, he added, seemed to be solving all the questions for the Government. They had now discussed ground rents and pensions. They must be an intAigeut body. (Laughter.) Councillor BENNETT supported the motion on the rninuk.es. As a Lonservative he saw his way to support it. Conservatives did not exist for specifically looking after ilia interests of the landlords, but the interests of the community. It was not fair that the landlords shoul reap these enormous unearned increments with- out paying sometning in the shape of ransom to the community from whom benefits were derived. The general principle of the thing meet commend itself to anyene lo Aloe at it in a fair spirit. amocillor Jamns EVANS said arable land had been taken into the Borough which used to be taxable, but the landlord now paid no rates, as the land had ceased to be arable land;</p>
        <p>Councillor PEAcock said he could put his hand on a piece of land whin!' was taken som years ago for £6O a year, but which the owners now wanted 22,000 for and also thQ original ground rent, The land had become valuable through works in the neighbourhood, arid was being h!ld back for a higher price. He knew this be- cauie he had tried to get it. (laughter.) Allemln SUTTON, in reply, said he stould not have opposed Ih3 resolution had they all had an opportunity of dinussia,c: it in committee. If the Council would only agree to defer the matter until they could have an oopirt laity of fairly discussing it be should be sitistied. Nothing iu the nature of smuggling it through the com- mittee shonld take place.</p>
        <p>Con ;calor TINNION siid it Etzotned to him that if they had au adjournment they would not come to any other conclusion, but would all vote the sun() way. If it had not been discussed at the General Purposes Committee it solmed to him that it had had some lively discussion R o rnewhe re else. Re thought tin ground landlord should b 3 taxel, just the ss.rne as the man who put the bricks and monar on his ground. (lElear. hear.)</p>
        <p>A vote was then takea. For the ameniment theca votPrl : Alderman Suttos, C)uncillers Smethurst, Longton, R)gers, third. Heiton, John Rchert 1, and Les (8). ~. Azainst tne amendu.ent : Alderman R obarts, Calla• Ci 110•3 8,1497 Cirrern, P.umptoa, Bennatt, John Emu., Jarnus•Eqans, Tsnuion, V. It. itohmts, Peacock, and F Monk' (11.) MIXOI2, : Eight for and eleven against. Ttae ameadment is lost .</p>
        <p>Alderman Soma; : I hopa you are satisfied now that it is a party question. (Laughter.) Caos.3evativei can- not see to the end of their noses. (Renewed laughter.)</p>
        <p>itININCE COMMIME-VIOTORIA PARK.</p>
        <p>'rho min Aro of this cominitie, as published in las,: Saturday's " Ex minqr." were proposed for chnfircnati by Councillor SErAw GREEN, and seconded by the DEPUTY MAYO R.</p>
        <p>Councillor ELLISON asked what it was intenthd to d) with rea7d to ii,czor4 Park. Was it propoded to open the front portion_?</p>
        <p>The MAYOR: 0 1 Y. COUCleillOt ELLISON : When ? The Mayou : I waleave the committee to decide- C )111ClilOr SHAW Gasgrr replied that tne committee were n4t in a position yet to do what they would, they had net had the sanctioa of the Local ;Government Board to deal with the matter, but they had set apart a large tralt of land. Councillor ELLISON sail hn had been over the land. The widest part at it was 150 yards and it tapered down to 108 yards. At a very high tide it was flooded and would be useless for recreation purposes. He knew the place well, and he considered that the worst portion of the land had been set apart._</p>
        <p>Councillor &amp;raw GREEN : I can only say that it repre- Fontsa pic, of over three acres, and for that end of LitchForri that is enough at present. Councilto? BENNETT : I could not support the matter unless it was purely temporary. The MAYOR : It 13 purely temporary. (Hear,hea7.) The minutes were then ?,arried- TfIE BUDGET.</p>
        <p>COMPREIIENSIVE STA CENIENT BY GOUNCIEJLOR</p>
        <p>SHAW GREEN. THE RATES FOR THE COMING YEAR.</p>
        <p>The minutes of the Council contain the estimates ot expenditure and income for the year commencing the Ist April, 1899 The following is a summery of the esti- mates :—Borough rate, £13,287 63 10,1 • improvement rite, R 21,052 64 21 ; g Iry-Tat sewers rate, £1,884 124 41: total. £36 224 5,: 4d. In 1895 the amount was £33.970 lOs 61 ; 1396. £35 643 23 2d; 1897, £39,030 17s 2,1 ; aed 1898, £37,063 75 14. Coueoillor Saaw-Gtosen meved the passing of the resolutions standing in his name, namely : "That the Council hereby estimate that the ameunt of £13,287 64. 101. will, in addition to the Borough Fund, be required and sufficient for the purposes to which the Borough Fund is applicable, under the Municipal Cor- porations Act, 1882, for other wise by law, for the year to and 31st March, 1900. That the Council do hereby order a Borough Rate to be made in pursuance of the Munici- pal Corporations Act, 1882, in erder to raise the said amount. That ths ove:seere of the township of War- rington be and they are hereby ordered to pay te the Treasurer of the Borough, by two half yearly instal- ments, and on or before such dates as may be fixed by the Finance and Enate Committee, the contribution of the sum of £13,238 61. 10d., out of the Poor Rate made or to be made for the said township of Werrington, as provided by the Municipal Corporations Act. 1882. That the estimate for an improvement rate as resotrunended by the Finance and E tate Committee be confirmed, and that the Finance and Estate Committee make an im- provement rate accordingly at two shillings and and eightpence in the pound, to be collected by two half yearly initalments, and be authorised to affix the com- mon seal of the Corporation thereto. That the estimate of the general sewers rate as recommended by the Fin- ance and E ita.te Committee be confirmed, and that the Finance and Estate Committee do make a gen ral sewers rate accordingly at fourpence in the £, to be collected by two half-yearly instalments, and be authorised to affix the common seal of the Corporation thereto." Couneillor Shaw Green said: It is with much pleasure that I rise to present the badget of the C neoosation for the ensuing year, as we have so recently been some- what excited consequent upon the promotion of the new Bill with its proposed heavy capital expenditure. I b.lieve the presentation of this budget will he a real relief, for though I thall have to show that in many ways our expenditure has increased, the actual amounr, we are asking you to approve is on the whole £339 less than the stun yo a eanczimed last year. It will be very gratifying, too, because it shows a con-, tinuance of that material mattress to which we have now been accustomed for so many years. The Council will remember three or four years ago our anxiety to make the bur lan caused by the smallpox epidemic as light as possible. Well, we asked you then for as small a rate as we could do with,and also for two years we only asked for a twopeeny 83Wer3 rate, and in consecpeace our bank balances got on the wrong side. Lam pleased to say that during the year we have tessera fortunate enough to have had atm) :1; contineally a credit halence, and cens-quently there has been a savine of in crest, and I need not say we have felt the benefit of the change. We naturally look to our sources of revenue for signs of prosperity, and with sine exception they all show dechied improvement. Whether it is that the past summer was net a sufficiently warm one, or the Baths were not attractive, the revenue from that soerce did no; reach its estimate by £l6 4s. Tom other sources eat revenue are well up. The first to which I shall refer is Longford, which hes imoroveci by the sum of £4OO, and which we are informed c ,uld have done much more had the committee only had the material to sell. Then, the Markets, based as it was upon its re- cord of the previous year, gave us £l5O extra, and the Weights and Measures Department adds its quota be are increase of £44. We hardy like to speak of the Cemetery as a revenue-producing asset, yet it is, and this year it has yielded £B7 over its estimates. The most important and most satisfying item of all, hewever,is ...he improve- anent rate itself. The Council will remember that I stated at our Ise meetine, when we were going through the fieaacial clauses of the new Bill, that the annual in- crease in the improvement rate was equal to a penny in the pound lam pleased to say that the year that has' jest closed show ano sign of diminution, for, notwisk- standine a large amount of empty property numbering settle 590 houses, in addition to the increase we hoped far, it has given us another £43. The expenditure generally bears the same satisfactory character, and I hoped it would not he necessary to call attention to any committee. Really, however, we cannot qnite help it, if it is only to have the opportunity of showing how necessary it is for a committee to keep in mind that all the arrangements of the beroueh are based upon estimates for a year, and as no one knows what may have to be fac,cl in the follnwicg year, we cannot be too particular in see- ing that the expenditure is not exceeded without full and sufficient cause. The Town Hall, Park mad Baths, the Cemetery, and the General Purposes are all in the unfortunate position of having overspent their estimates. There is, hnwever, some palliation in the case of the General Purposes Oemnsittee, for it could not anticipate having to oppose the St. Helens Corporation in the House of Commons. which it did at a cos s of 1230, nor the extra sum of £l3O required to clear the expense of the Art Exhibition. It is surprising what apparently insignificant causes have an appreciable influence on the estimates.lt is not so generally known that a serious fall of snow will make all the difference in the expenditure of one committee, sufficient sometimes to turn the scale, but as we have • beers singularly free from such this past winter, the credit of the High- way Department is saved so much thereby. The Mar- kets Corismittee, anxious to help the farmers before re. peeing the pig merket,found if they did so a veterinary oureenn must be continually on the ground, and they 1 carried on the market under such conditions for some 'rime. Of cream when the results became known it was ound that not only hedlall the tolls disappeared, but hey hat carried with them some thirty pounds beside.</p>
