      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>BOLTON CHILDREN.</head>
        <p>THE BOLTON CHILDREN.</p>
        <p>Here is a case where t wine, a boy and a g;rl,r:ero both greatly benefited by Scott's Emulsion. The b had rickets and his sicter cieemed to be run down in health, Concerning th.m the father says :- 5, Springfi .Id Avenue. Middleton-street, October 7ch, 1898. _</p>
        <p>" Gentlemen,—ln March last I began giving Scott's Emulsion to my Fon (twin), who was two years and four months old afid was very delicate, showing no signs c:f walking. He could not even sit up. The dector said he had rickets. Scvtt's Emulsion seemed to do him good from the first. Ho has now taken several bottles and is very much stronger and heavier,and can run all over the house. During tho summer his twin sister was rua down, and a few doses of Scott's Emulsion speedily put her right. I havi not given either of them any for a few weeks, but as the boy has a very bad cough I got him a bottle, and the first two doses seemed to ease him. I have recommended Seett's Enulsirmn Ca several friends, and all twve greatly bnnefited by it, "I nmlin, yrairs faithfully. "(Signed) J. W. BOLTON*"</p>
        <p>One of the sources that has helped US tor the present Budget is the estimate of the Nigiatseil and &amp;evening Department: Owing to the extension of the borough the committee made proeision for scaveoging, collecting the pails, &amp;e., in the aide I area, but it has worked. on so well with the rest of the borough, that there is a sav- ing in that department which has addei to the credit balance, and enabled the coinmittte to submit a reduc- tion of those items for the pre3. zo year. I do not pro pose to wade through the various estimates of the different committees. A few wer is generally will suf- fice, for I have ne doubt so enthusiastic a Council as ours will have given oven such uninteresting details as these some portio a of their attentien. Is the Watch Cernmittee the main increase is in clothing an I aceountrements, bat as this is tha year for overcoats and capes the increase is readily accountea for. Tier =Mon of the two items in the General Purposes Com- mittee (on page 4) soeak for themselves and account tar the reduction. The Gyeenasium is keeping up its repu- tether' and again asks for mere: The first item on page 7 for salaries is&amp; transfer, and will be repaid by another department, but it I did not say so seem adveeturoua spirit might ask why. In the Health Department, page 10, there is a now item, viz, £2O for interest on property on T swn Hilt purchased by that committee. Smas of us are disposed to ask for what purpose. Certeinly not. for an open space. Theneif not for that what was it for? In the Market Committee's estimate, page 12, will be found an item £420 hvtalment of princinal.This item was omitted last year because we hopei to have get permis- sion to capitalize and borrow, but such permission has I not been given, and to it must again be debitei to " revenue. Trie last item to which I would call atlentioe Is on nage 13 in the Finance Committee's estimates, viz., £1,420 interest on purchase money fin street improve- ments. This serious item of £l,OOO er:ra is iaterest on the money paid for property in various places other than Briage-street, &amp;t.. for the pur- pose of street improvements, the permission to borrow which has not yet been sanctioned. It will be seen by a reference and c otnea.risen of the Pens in the summery , of results on page 14 of the [Wee-, that thistyeae we are asking the Coueeil to sanction an expenhiure of £36.224 53. 4d. This, as I have already said, is actaally £339 is. 9.1. less than the sem we asked for last year. " It is matter for congratiVation, but it is not because our expenditure is any less. On the contrary, our expen- diture on all the three heads is greater, bat we have the I the benefit of the credit balances of which I spoke. The actual amount of expenditure is, as stated in the summary, £33,224, but there " is one item in the total estimate of the improvement rate account on page 13, to which I must call your attention f )r a moment. When the estimate were draftei it was discovered that the three or four items to which! have cellei special attention would necessitate an increated demand upon the ratepayers if. we cold not secure help. The committee was anxious, if possible to avoid calling upon the Gas Department, seems their expenditure on the new retort house and the recent re- duction ia the pries of gas. bet on our appeal to them they rrenerouily undertook to p-ovide a sum no evceed- ing £2,000 it circumstances necessitated such a c ill,and , so the lest item on the err dit side of pare 13, £3,823 has th's particular £2,000 in it, and as will be sego, reduc the actual expenditure te £21,252 6i 2.1. This vary welcome action has enabld the Committee to submit the resolution which I shall shortly propose, and thereby avoid any increase. I treat; I have not wearied the Council, but before submitting the motions wltich stand in my name, I snould like to add a few gsneral words. I have referred to the amenne of the improvement rate. The large sum of £21,291. was collected with only £9 , 23 11 of atrears.and tee borough treesurer,in his note to me,says—" Collected without haviute recourse to legal procee,lioes.” A rate at 2; 81 in the £ pro In-e 1 (as ' just statai) last year eE21,234, and we therefore, ask you to sanction the Finance Coennittee levying a rate at that amount fer the forthcoming year. For the borough rate we ask you to order the overseers to pay over to the Finance Committee the sum of £13,287 63 103, and then further, that we levy a general sewers rate at 41 ire ;he £. Seme people, I keo yr, have an idea that War- rington is heavily rated. For their benefit let me say that compared with other borough it is not at all highly rated. I have in my hand a list of 73 boroughs, an] Warrington st Ind% 43rd. The hiehest has a rate or 83 9d in the L,11.1d the lowest. 34 81. I spoke a little while ago of the materiel increase of the Borough. find that the ratable vtlus which . has jest been Pr(' snnted is this year £217,612 aeainst £205,302 oa.rs weed more. The gross indebte 'es of tee Berousti as £588,176, or a little over .£9 per head of the popula- tion. As this item will be meterially affected by the expenditure under the new 8111.1 Ahead like to re-assure any of the more timid among us. We have very con- siderable values in our revenue producing properties gas. water, market ten% ce netery—whic 1, if taken from our cross indebtedness, would leave very little for the Town Hall and Park, Longford,pelice court, street hnorovemente, sewae works. depots, &amp;c., See. I have in my hind a list of 52 boroughs with their capital indebtedness, and thougu many of these have I.ryn estates, yet the cl,!,bt per heal ralees from £2 to £27. Warringtnn, as you will see, comes out well in compari- son, and if the expenditure provided for in the new Bill, be carried throwth as .forshadowiTi we shall be able to maintain a reputation for %vie° and eood government (Appeterse.) I have pleasure in aubrniteing the budget, and the motions and resolutions standing in my name. The DEPUTT M&amp;YOB seconded, and tho motioas were</p>
        <p>Yon cannot get away from no unprejudiced opinions of those who, aft,u. taking Scott's Emul- sion, are kind enouzli to write about their experi- ence with this preparation. Actual experience is better than theory, and ekperience cectaiuly teaches that Scott's Emulsion is the greatest known renv!dy for all those bodily ailments indicated by wasting of flesh or strength. The b )y who was rickety derived benefit from the hypophosphites of time and soda in Scott's Emul- sion, whies as-ists in making healthy bones, and the little girl was undoubtediy benefited by the cod- liver oil, which makes ft ,‘ll and strength. Scott's Emulsion is sweet to the taste, easy of digestion, and is the moat rational form of cod-liver oil. Any- body who tries Sctt,'s Emulsion will not doubt its superlative merit, both as food and medicine. You can obtain a sample of Scott's Emulsion by vending threepence to cover postage to Scott and Bowne, Limited, Manufkc'urring Chemists, 95, Great aiffron Uitl. Loodon, E.C., and mentioning this paper. All chemists sell Scott's Emulsion.</p>
