        <p>Cculcillor Sum GREEN observel that the overseen, were now appointed by the Council. Ttvr3 was no vestrr as in the old tinaas, and tie assistant overseer (Mr J. L Tunatall) had supplied him with the lc:Cowin in form ition.</p>
        <p>" Dar Sir,—The Borough call f ths current yeir will, I underqrand, amount to £13,267 6i 101. As against £13,546 19i 21 last year a reduction of £261 12i 4cl. the Uni,na call for the current half year is £7.224, as against £6,733, f r tin corresponding half of last year, an increas3 of 4483. Icasmu!h as a similar increase may be expecte i in the call fsr the secood half of the current year, the Overseers will tied h nec:lssary to es- timate f)r a net* increase of £7lO 7s 81. Aitticugh, rherPfor., the Overseers lave A n increased balance of £417 12s 9d as compared with the ead of the last finan- cial year, i will oby;oualr be impossin:e to reduce the rate. Oa the other hand, it does not a ope tr that any increase riil be nee ,ssary. for the enh toned ratable valuo should make up what would otnorwise be defi- cient."</p>
        <p>Ti3S MAYOR.—I think we are very much indebted to Councilhr SIANV Green for tne very clear tridcornpre- hensive statement he has laid before 113. It id also a. very Rdigfletnry statement. (H•ar. hear.) THE NEW ROID FRO S TOCKTON HEATH TO LA. rcaFoRD.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR now brought forward a motion " to COQ.. si.ler, and if thought advisable, to approve of the appli- cation of Mesirs. Greenall, Whicley &amp; Company, Limited, to stop up and divert the public footpath lead- ing from Willerspool Causeway, near the Saracen's Head, to Was-lane." e pad he thouzht they would hive noticed that a new road had been formed about 100 yards b.3lovr the " Siroceo's Head "on the Warring- ton aide. an . this road led to the point the old roa.ii passing Alderman Francomb's howe led to. It was 604. yards Ione; and 40 feet wide, and would reduce the dis- tanoe frcm Latcurord to Warrington in th-it diroction very consider‘bly. Ii WaS Mei3N.Gret!nAll's intention to form a macadamised road, until it was taken over by tha Corporation, when it would regairo to be paved as the ordinary streets were. Mr. Down gave them his assurance that at the ear... ii est opportunity the road would be conlnue I right through tr.) Was/?-lane. The Mayor th.,u,!ht it would b an extreme!y useful road. Aid linan SUTTON second it:l and was of the opinion that it would be :1 great and desirable improvement. Councillor Straw GREEN supported the motion. He thought the road vtLiald la; a great advantage here- after.</p>
        <p>Councillor ELLISON aneed with what had been raid with regard to the flew roa 1. as thought however that it would be a gm tt inconvenience to those who- wished to go to Stocicon Heath. It would ba further round by the new rod. Lie thought it woull be very little inconvenience for Mesqrs. Greenall to make a foo!.— pa!li along their now lion of railwav from the Bridge- water Canal bridge to near tho Swing BrAge over the Ship Canal. Alderenan Ronzars : When i it. intended to connect this road with tn' Wtliorpml r.)ad ? The MAYOR : I think it will b 3 done forthwith, and certainly ti:4,r, the other rind is closed.</p>
        <p>Alderin In ROBERTS : Itwill 131 m tde a thlrotulhly servi2e tb:e road IT:tote it is tiandtd user tu Hie Gnpor- ation</p>
        <p>The MAYOR: Oh yes Alderman ROBERTS : We are taking. over in perpetu— ity the maintsn,inee .-}f a road in lieu of one which is now only an occuration load anti involves us in no liability wha,eret.. _</p>
        <p>The N1.3..Y0h . We do not take over the road until it i 3 partnaaehtty plvNi with setts. They inaimain it SO 'aria as it is mactamis,d. They have t pave it at th own exnease.</p>
        <p>--Alllerrnall ROBERTS : WhAt rights shall we have over it thm ? Thu MAYOR :- All the rights of a footway. The Sante a 3 over the pr.9gent rrai. Al.lormAn Ronstits lan satisnAd. he in..tion vv,ls 'll,n p :ssed avl tho CUR-. 11 rove.</p>
