      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>EX A MIN E ,</head>
        <p>THE EX A MIN E ,</p>
        <p>APRIL 14th 1899.</p>
        <p>THE House of Commons Parliamentary Control has taken that action against of the Church. the Church which it formerly took with regard to the Army. It has reaffirmed its right of control. Whilst the decision is legally unimpeachable it is by no means acceptable to BOMB Churchmen, and it touches the whole con- troversy now raging between the extreme Ritua- lists and the rest of the Church of England. The ARCHBISHOP of York says he and the PRIMATE are seeking for some 4' temporary expedient " in the form of "a purely ecclesiastical tribunal." That is, they are going to supersede the Judicial Com- mittee of the Privy Council by pushing it. aside, and they are about to exercise the "spiritual au- tonomy" Mr. BALFOUR suggested as an excellent thing I The constitution of the Judicial Com- amittee was deliberately made by a Conservative Government to keep Ritualism and Romanism in check. Hence the parties which can be so described have never liked it. Their theory is that the Church is superior to, and inde- pendent of the State. That is, ecclesiasti- cally, an altogether Roman theory. It is not a Protestant theory; it has not even the warrant of the pre-Reformation period. The right of Parlia- mentary control was reasserted in the Public Worship Regulation Act, and because Some por- tion of that Act has been disliked the Archbishops are going to fashion a court of their own, to which Objectors to the other court can apply. The opera- tion is not illegal, but it is not strictly legal. The decisions of the Archbishops are not the law of the land. They are not nearly so much so as is a resolution of the House of Commons. The differ- ence between the two is that the latter goes back to the law as it is embodied in an Act of Parlia- ment, sanctioned by both Houses of Parliament, and approved by the Queen as the Head of the state and the Head of the Church; whereas the former is a subtle attempt to reconcile men in re- volt by some extra legal process,which is not bind- ing upon anybody. It is conceivable that practices allowed by the two Archbishops might still be condemned by the Judicial Committee. The set- ting up of a purely ecclesiastical authority was narrowly avoided on Tuesday night, and it almost Seemed as if subtle wits had been at work to corn- bit the House of Commons to an illegal position giving the Judicial Committee the "go by." Itappily the danger was discovered in time to pre- vent a revolutionary catastrophe. Had the house raerely affirmed that lawlessness in the Church was disobedience to the Bishops and the Prayer Book it would have stultified itself, abandoned its old Position of Parliamentary control, and have set lip the bishops against the law of the land. The lesson will not be lost upon the flippant persons Who accuse the Judicial Committee of being a dis- ereditxl authority, and it will have to be kept in view if we should hear anything more about the bill to amend the Ecclesiastical Courts so as to give a measure of spiritual autonomy to the High Church party. The need tor loyalty is obvious to all moderate men, but if Parliament is to be asked to give freedom to the Church it must only be by the just process of Disestablishment and Disen- dowment. We cannot permit an imperium *itiperio, with a duumvirate ot Archbishops.</p>
        <p>THE number of suicides connected Suicile and with the Dreyfus case is steadily Forgfiry. mounting up. With the suicide of COLONEL HENRY'S secretary, ADJUTANT LORIL- LIE; on having received a summons to go to Paris fr°re St. Quentin, to give evidence in the inquiry UoW proceeding, four persons have taken their lives rather than subjec; themselves to cross-ex- Italination and ignominy. It is evident that there 'lave been a great number of forgeries connected 'with the Dreyfu case,' roughly divisible into two grouPe—the first before, and the second after, the trial. The latter are probably more daring than tile former, because they were intended to hush up inquiry, and they had to put the guilt of the accused man beyond all doubt in order to satisfy tmlle doubts and scruples of Ministers and Generals. hey effectually did so for the time being,but now they are being examined, they seem to be much plausible than they were before. They may</p>
        <p>be brushed aside as forgeries, made to order, and intended to shield several high-placed mili- tary officials. The first series of papers included the list of papers known as the bordereau, and it was in two different kinds of handwriting. The assumption of General ZURLINDEN was that DREYFUS wrote part of the document with his right hand and part with his left. The corres- pondence between Major ESTERHASY'S writing and that of the bordereau is so close, and it is so differ- ent from that of DREYFUS, as shown by undoubted copies, that if it were a genuine document—which may be doubted—it coull not have been written by DREYFUS at all. Assuming it to have been a bogus document, there was evidently an attempt to so far imitate parts of the handwriting of 1 DREYFUS as to lead to his arrest and trial. This I seems probable, because ESTERECAZY had already established a reputation for his ability to get at documents other Powers wanted, and he was said to have been in the habit of selling them. There were other officers in the plot to sell,and they readily joined in the plot to ruin DREYFUS iu order to escape themselves. The motive for for gory was thus a very strong one, and it existed exactly in the quarter where the suicides have occurred. All the men implicated, however, have not committed suicide. They seldom do. But some of the guilty parties, who' know so much, prefer death to dis- closure, whilst the others brazen it out,and excuse themselves by sophisms, which do not deceive any- body but themselves. It is a curious moral we must draw from these dabblings in military in- trigue, and it is not complimentary to the French staff. The French officers seem to be as corrupt to- day as they were under the Empire,and even more eager to make money by selling national secrets. It is a pitiable spectacle, and we do not care to dwell upon it Unduly. ADJUTANT LORILLIER could have thrown some light upon several infamies. He has preferred to take his own life, and to say nothing grave about his late chief. It may have seemed to him a sacrifice for the honour of a friend; but it simply makes matters worse. COLONEL HENRY had a pistol placed on the table of his cell, and was told to consider the honour of the Army. Has some higher officer been pointing out to AD- JUTANT LORILLIER the same dramatic exit?</p>
        <p>IF public opinion has any weight at all, the shipwreck of the Stella will be the last disaster causei by racing or reckless navigation which has for its sole object an accurate '''time of arrival. Neither of these charges has been proved. It may be proved that in this special instance they are entirely unfounded. But the impression has exis- ted for many months that racing, if superficially forbidden, was officially winked at by the campet- ing companies, and this impression must have had in the past some foundation, slight or strong. Too much attention need not be paid to the state- ments emanating from excited survivors of the recent wreck. Many of those state- ments aught certainly not to have been made, save in a court, and under legal invita- tion. They condemn brave men wha can no longer be heard in defence,and it may be that they place an interpretation upon semi-jesting remarks which the utterer never intended to convey. Most of the passengers appear to confess that they hardly realise how they got into the boats at all. They are hardly likely to be trustworthy guides on questions connected with the navigation of the bhip at the time of the wreck. On the one hand, the public should, in the interests of justice, accept all such statements with caution. On the other, the competing companies should treat with seriousness the impression that racing has been practised, if not by the Stella then by other boats at other times. If they took mutual steps to make racing impossible,public confidence would speedily be restored.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Society is now in possession of the Academy, and it is to be hoped that the ad- vantages the society offers to young men of literary and intellectual tastes will be taken full advantage of. The inaugural reception by the President was a great success and full of promise for the future. It wab quite natural that there should be some rejoicing in having saved such an historic building as the:Academy from destruction. There is no doubt that in the eyes of some public men the site is an excellent one for a good busi- ness establishment, and from their utilitarian standpoint they fail to see why the Corporation should throw money away upon what they would be disposed to call mere sentiment. This opinion, however, we are glad to say has not been allowed to prevail, and the Academy will still be handed down to .future generations, reminding them of its brilliant past and be a worthy memorial of the men who have thrown a halo around the history of Warrington in the 18th century.</p>
        <p>The irony Of fate is constantly being exemplified. Last week, a vessel which was expected by its inventor to revolutionise marine navigation came "under the hammer" at Liverpool, and not a single bid was made for her. This was the Ernest Bazin roller steamer, about which so much was heard, written, and read a couple of years ago. The vessel cost £20,000 to construct, and the prin- ciple of her construction was that the motive power should bo eight huge steel turbines, driven by separate engines. She was expected to ride over the waves much after the fashion of a bicycle negotiating a hill, but these expectations wets not realised owing to something being wrong with either the waves or the boat. The Ernest Bazin floated and moved. She could do all that, but she was found to be impracticable as a means of making the journey across the weary waste of waters, any more com- fortable than it was under the old and existing methods. Alas! What bright dreams have melted in the air. After having had an enormous amount of time, labour, and money expended upon her, tha Ernest Bazin has come down to be- ing hawked about the ports of Great Britaid. As a steamer she does not tempt bidders, but as old iron she may yet have a future before her.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>WAR RINGTON.</head>
        <p>WAR RINGTON.</p>
        <p>ANCIENT ORDER OF FORESTERS.- WARRINGTON • DISTRICT.</p>
        <p>The quarterly meeting of this friendly soCiety was held on Saturday, at the Blue Lion Inu,M.arket- place. Bro. J. E. Troman, C.R., presided over a full attendance of delegates, viz', Bros. J. J. Bennett (Court 138), George Allen, jun. (240), T. Baden (330), J. Ratter (431), W. Orritt (461), T. 11)11ins (475), and Llewellyn Davies (240), secretary. The balance sheet showed a satisfactory state of affairs, the investments amounting to £1,594 176. 3d., and the Court contributions to £B6 10a. The expendi- ture included £5B for death allowances, the greatest loss b.ing sustained by Court 475, three members having died during the past quarter. It was unani- mously resolved that Bro.Tronian, represent this district at the High Cana meeting, to be held at Cardiff in August.</p>
        <p>CLERGY DAUGHTERS' SCHOOL -IMPORTANT PROPOSAL.</p>
        <p>An important meeting in connection with the above scnool was hold at the Enstitution on Mon- day afternoon to consider the following resolutions: ___46 To take on lease for a term of 50 years, The Oakland, Preston Brook, at the annual rent of £3OO, and ta make such alteration as may be neces- sary to fit it for the purposes of the school." "To accept the offer of the Training Calle2e authorities to take over certain premises of the Clergy Daugh- ters' School, paying a sum in c iso of £2,500, and such rent as may be agreed upon by Mr. John White for the Clergy Daughters' School, and Mr. Earnshaw for the Training College, or by their um- pire if they disagree." A long discussion took place and eventually both matters were postponed for further consideration.</p>
        <p>LINSEED COMPOUND" (Trade Mark for Coughs and Colds, of proven efficacy. 130. Sold hy Chemists —Advt</p>
        <p>OLDBURY'S CoCoa is absolutely pure, baln,g entintly tree from kola. malt. hops, alkali, or Any foreign admixture C‘ution The public should insist on havmg CADBURY'S —sold only in Packets and Tins—as other Comas are °flea substituted for the take of extra profit.—A44 1(4).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>EXAMINER, Fill DAY APRIL 14, 1899. RUNCC RN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, Fill DAY APRIL 14, 1899. RUNCC RN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>THE ALKALI LITIGiTION. The Appeal Court, on Thursday, gave judgment with 3osts in the case of the Castnet Kellner Co. against the Commercial Corp4ration. The court found for the latter with costs. RUNCORN ANGLER'S ASSOCIATION. On Wednesday, abut half-a-doz-n of the members of the Runcorn Fishing Club met a consignment of " Raiab3w Trout "from Wvesda!e, Lancashire, with a conveyanct. at the Runcorn Station, and with the utmost despstch got them into the club w itere, a feat accom- plished without a single lost. The waters are now, well- stocked with breme, perch, roach, carp and tench and sport next season should be of a highly satisfactory character.</p>
        <p>CAMDEN WE3LEYAN SUNDAY SCHOOL ANNIVERSARY. Special services were held in connection with Camden Wesleyan Church Sunday Schools anniversary, on Sari- day last, in St. Paul's Church, Runcorn, when the Rev R P Dowries (editor of "Great Thoughts,") preached eloquent sermons both morning and cvdning.—On Mon- day, Mr. Thwnes als delivered a lecture on "An Ideal Hero "to a crowded audience. The chair was occupied by Dr. Bowman, of Northwich.—The collections realised £46. COLLISION ON THE SHIP CANAL AT WESTON POINT- Oa Wednesday evening a collision occurred on the Ship Canal at Weston Point. As a steam tug was tow- ing a flat from Weston Mersey lock to the decks the steamship Maegie Warrington, bound from London to Manchester, came along and collided with the flat Emma, b3longing to Richard Abel,and so seriously dam- aged the craft that she had to be unaieliately beached. The steamer sustainel no damage. DEATH OF MR. HARRISON, OF WIDNES. Mr. W. Harrison, , headmaster of tie Farnworth Grammar School, Wines, died on Monday, after a brief illness, from pneumonia, contracted while holidaying at Salle, at Easter. He was 35 years of age, and leaves a widow and three children. His early demise is deeply regretted by a wide circle of friends. Possessed of high scholastic attainments, Mr. Harrison was also a p•pular member of the Widnes Cricket Club, and was captain in the Ist Lancashire Engineers Volunt-lers, Liverpool.</p>
        <p>ST. MICHAEL'S SUNDIY SCHOOL, RUNCIRN. A miscellaneous concert was given in the Primitive Methodist echo )Iroom, Greenway-road, Runcorn, last Tuesday evening, which was well attended (under the auspices of the above school). The following programme was submitted, and was well received :—Pianoforte solo, Miss B Johnson ; song, Rev E S Oliver; recitation " Eloratius " Mr Chantler ; song, "Thy sentinel am I" Mr Ernest Bennett; humorous sketch, Mr J J Jeffreys ; duet," Friendship," Miss Pattie Timmins and Mr E Bennett. Rhombero's Toy Symphony was Pleasingly performed by the following: —Misses Dsllie Cameron, Kirscie Forrester, Eva Gregory. Nellie Johnson, Nora Lea, Mabel Pope, Maggie Smith, assie Stokes, Chrissie Sutton, Pattie Timmins, Annie Warner, Tetsie Wiswall. Piano, Miss Crnsbv and Miss B Johnsoc ; lit violins, Mr W B Kay, Mr C Ditch fild, Mr F R awland ; 2nd violin, Mr C EL Tlmmins - 'cello, Mr W Simkin ; conductor, Mrs W B Kay; The Rev G S Oliver followed with a song. Mr E B.nnett gave "The Longshoreman," and after an inimitable humorous sketch by Mr J J Joffreys, the singing of "God save the Queen" concluied the proc?edings. The able and efficient accompanists were Mrs W B Kq• and Mr J J H Challinor. RUNCORN BRANCH OE' THE GIRLS' FRIENDLY SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>The Primitive Methodist Seheolroom, Greenway-road. was, on Friday evenicg last, the scene of a bright and pleasant reunion of the members of the Runcorn Branch of the Girls' Friendly Society. The proceedings took the form of a miscellaneous cencert, and was presided over by the Rev. Canon Maitlaiel Wood (vicar). The open- ing part of the programme was the rendition of G. F. Roots' floral cantata " the coming of the Flowers" by members of the Girls' Friendly Society, in an effective manner. Part It opened with a humorous recitation by Mr. W. Jones. The daet " Excelsior" followed and Messrs. E. W`orrall and T. Norman Johnson were loudly applauded for a very clever performance. "In friend- ships name" sung by Miss Coward was well re- ceived. The pianoforte duet "A country dance ' be Mrs. W. B. Kay and Miss Smith was encored. Mr. E. Worrell contributed the serg " Mona" effectively,whilst Mr. T. Norman Johnson sang the "Storm Fiend " per- fectly, arm ill being undeniable. Mr. W. Jones sub• muted a second humorous sketch in his inimitable style, and was vociferously encored. The efficient ac- companists were: Mesdames Timmins, and W. B. Kay. The singing of " God save the Queen " &amp;wed a happily spent evening. The room had been nicely decorated with palms and arium lilies. TEMPERANCE MEETING AT WIDNES. On Monday evening, a united temperance meeting was held in the Lacey-street schoolroom. There was a large audience. Owing to the indisposition of the Vicar (the Rev. C. E. Stocks, M.A..), Councillor Owens mccupied the chair, and was supported by Revs. R. E. Corlett, MA., M. H. Capman, and Councillor Townend, of Middleton. A choir of Band of Elope chil- dren, under the conductorship uf Mr. J. H. Harper was present. After the rendering of the chorus ** Hippy Homes," the Chairman called upon Councillor Tewnend to address the meeting. That gentleman said he was there ass life teetotaler. He was glad to think that at the time of his hivh, his father and mother had placed his name on a pledge card, and he had never been more determined to keep that pledge than he was at the pre- sent time. He thought their Elands of Hvs were doing good work in traiaiug, children to take pert in public meetings. He was not there as ere who had suffered in his home life by the drink, but he could not understand some men speaking as they did' with regari to the traffic, who hid suffered by the drink—Tee Rev. R. E. Corlett also addressed the ineeting.Ele said that he wished teeir Vicar (the Rev. C. E. Stocks) had been able to be present. but ho desired to give att -Terme to the sympathy the Rev. M. Stocks had for all who wer3 working in the greet cause. Several more choruses were sung by the choir, and a vote of thanks to the Chairman and speakers terminated the meeting. LAMENTABLE FATALITY TO A FORMER RUNCORN MAN.</p>
        <p>Oa Monday, Mr, T. E. Sampson, coroner for Liver- pool, held an inquest upon Vie body of Frank Fletcher, 16 years of age, railway shunter, who resided at 50, Albert Elward-road, Liverpool. The deceased was in the employ of the L. &amp; N..W. Railway Company, snd whilst at work last Friday evening beyond Edge Hill Station, he was knock srl down by a waggon, sustaining compound injuries to Loth thighs, which resulted in his death at the Royal Infirmary en the folowing day. While in the Institution and shortly before his death &amp;cooed said it was purely an accident. —The jury returned a verdict of " Accidental de ith."—The i. &amp; N.W. Railway Company were represented by their solicitor, Mr. F ant:la.—Oa Wedae ;day afternoon the funeral of the deceased took place at the Runcorn Cemetery. The remains which wore enclosed in a handsomo brass mounted oak coffin wi.u.s cacotte I from Liverpool ts Runcorn by a large number of the brakeatnen employed in thi diF- tric•., who were also accomp mite I by Mr. Staliceir, their manager,and the Edge Hill Traffic Band of which the de- ceased was a member.Arrivyd at Runcorn the coffin was conveyed to the griveside being proceeded by the band paying the" Dead March," and followed by I3UIII trous sorowing relatives, friends, and his fellow workera. At the cemetery the burial service was coalucted by the Rev. C. P. Bashfor%, 8.A., and at the conclusion of the impressive ceremony a Ixautiful wreath was deposited on the grave as "a token of respect from his fellow workers."—Young Fletcher was well-known in Runcorn, and for ISOM time was e:nployed by Mr. FoiliPs,Charch- street. He was formerly a member of tin Mill Brow Band ,which eveatually became the Volunteer Brass -Band and though not a member of that holy he has many friends in it iv vo will bitterly regret his less.---Somo short time ago is parents resided in Union-street, but have now removed to North wich. General sympa'hy iS expressed for them in their sad affi ction.</p>
        <p>A CRUEL FATIIErt AT RIINTCOIig. On Friday morning before M-sars. F. J. N navels (pre- siding) and John Ocaleston, James Owen was arr tigned charged with unlawfully running away fcom Ruecera on the 2nd April, whereby his two children aged respee- tively four years and twelve months, became chargeable to t es Runcorn Uaion.—Thomas Cooke,relieving offieer, said that it was a very bad case. Prisoner had been in reeuler employment since Christmas and Was eareiag 275. a week. Oat of that amount he cerviderod Bs. or 93. a week was sefficient to maintain his wife amid chil- dren, and to pay the rent as well. The women got into such a weak state that her uecle had taksa her to a doc- tor, and he said that it was not medicine she wental but food. She was being starved to death. Since then she had been taken to her brother't in Liverpeel, avhsre she was now being nursed and cared for, but so low had she got that it was questiolable whether she ever would get better. On A.pril Ist the prisener cane to Rancor a from Ellesmere Po:t, and instead of hieluiriag for his children, who had been left at a neighbeuee, he went to a public-hotre. where his children were brought to him. He then took them to a house in Speakmen-street, and left them there, saying that he would return in a quartrr of an hour for them, which he failed to do. On toe Sunday he was drinking at the public-house again, but he went away the same day, and made no attempt to arrange for his children,in conseq mice of which they mere taken to the workhouss. The prisoaer had some very respectable relatione in the town,and his own father had said that perhaps a little punishment would do him good. They had he'ped him until they were tired of helping him.—Upon oeing asked what off .r he would make, the prisoner said that he could not pay more than Ss. and nay for his !edging; am wall—rise Chairman: Five shillings for two children P—The prisoner sail that be would have to pay something for his wife and his lodgings.—Mr C )okei replying to the Bench, said that the prisonsee house had been lotked up by the landlord fr the rent. The Guardians desired to press the case as an ex ample to others. He had put them to an expense of about £2.—The Chairman said that it was a das- tardy thing for the prisoeer to leave his children as he had done. He would be sent to Knutsford Gaol for one month's hard labour.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN WESLEYAN CYCLE CLUB.</p>
        <p>Under the charge of Cant4in Bart Cooke the above club had its usual run on Wedneiday. Helsby was the destination. The attendance of members was not large but the coating was thoroughly enjoyed.</p>
        <p>CHURCH MISSI3NARY SOCIETY CENTENARY.</p>
        <p>The Centenary of the Church Missionary Society was commemorated at Runcorn. by the holding of special services in the Parish Church. on Wednesiay evening, at which the preacher was the RSV. H. B. Blogg, M.A., vicar of Frolsham. HALTON WESLEYAN GUILD.</p>
        <p>Mr. Allen Bangley.student of Bangor College, on Mon- day, gave a paper on "Moderate Drinking," at the Hal- ton Wesleyan Guild meeting. Mr. F. Hanky presided over a goal atte-tdance. In the after diseussixt Messrs. W. Rowe and W. Looker took a prominent part.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GIJA.RDIEsTS.</p>
        <p>The usual meeting oF. the above Boird was held at Dutton Workhouse, on Tuesday last. There were pre- sent :—Mes=rs. J Mosley Williams (chairman). Mr A R Norman, T R -,ynolds, Dr Gastaldi, with the Rural Dis- trict Councillors, and Mr. George F. Ashton (deputy clerk.)</p>
        <p>FINANCE COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>Mr. T. Wright submitted the fini.nce report :—Out- relief wag granted as follows :—Runcorn, £BO ; Frod- sham, £25; Great Bud worth, £22. The payin:nts in- cluded £276 2s to the Cheshire County Council for maintenance of lunatics for the quarter.—The reporo was unanimously adopted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>WEATHER.</head>
        <p>THE WEATHER.</p>
        <p>Phenomenal weather prevailed daring the week. The mornings have been wet dreary and bleak alternating with sunshine and blue sky, and the eveniags have generally been cold. Oa Tuesday hail fell. Wednesday was very unsettled, though the afternoon waa fine. On Thursday a slight thunderstorm passed over the district and rain fell heavily during the greater part of the day. So far the weather has been more like March weather than the proverbial "sunshine and light showers" of April. GALLANT RESCUE OF A BOY AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>About noon on Wednesday a boy unknown (and who disappeared after losing rferzued)fell into theißridgewater Canal mar Top Locks. Sevaral individuals triei to reach him but tailed, and Mr. Peter Daakin, foreman of the ' Ship Canal Comp iny's Works known as the Sprinch yard, coming up. though fully clothed, dashed into the canal and swam with the youngster ashore. It was a plucky rescue. Ths boy, who had appar3ntly suffered nothing la.‘sides a wettinz, immediately he was placel on terra firma took to his heels,and up to the present his identity is unknown.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>RUNCORN INTER-WORKS FOOTIALL COMPETITION,</head>
        <p>RUNCORN INTER-WORKS FOOTIALL COMPETITION,</p>
        <p>This c7mpettion which excites general interest has been further advanced during the week. On Saturday the Sprinch Yard tearnwas beAten by Wigga VVorks,after a spirited game by 3 points (try), to nil.—Monday night saw the Docks vanquished Wy the Old S3aoery, by a dropped goat to nil. On Wednesday evening Castner Kellner employees defeated Hazlehursts by 25 points to nil.—Owing to a protest, the match between the Shop Assistants and Higlafield Tannery was replayed with the result that the Shopmen romped home with 11. pints again; their opponents nil.—On the first occasion the Shopmen won by 9 points to nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>CHIMNEY BLOWN DOWN AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>A CHIMNEY BLOWN DOWN AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>On Friday night during the gale which prevailed, a chimney, 1121 t in height, on the works of the United Alkali Company at Weston, succumbed to the force of the elements and fell with a groat crash. It appoars that the workmen of Mr. Whitehead, steeplejack. Liver- pool, had been employed during the day in removing a portisn of the lower part of the chimney with a view to straightsniug it. The chimney is in an exposed situa- tion,and the gale,which swept over the district all night, brought the structure to the ground. The chimney fell on a piece of vacant land, and there was, happi y, not one about at the time. By working all night the works were attached to another chimney which, up to a few Tetrs ago, was said to b 3 the highest in the world, and work has been proceeding as usual.</p>
        <p>A LIVERPOOL CAPTAIN FIND FOR Sltt7GG GAT</p>
        <p>RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>Alden Cogley. master of tbe Steamship Vahan, of Liverpool, was at tne Runcorn P.)lice Court on Tuesday afternoen' charged w,th importing slbs. of cigars and 41bs. of tobacco, wichout paying duty on the same.— rhe defendant pleaded guilty.—From the evidence of Horace Rold, customs officer, it appeared he went down to the vessel in company win other officers, at 3p. in. on the previous day which was upon Voce vessel's arrival at Weston Point from Dieppe. In answer to the usu Li interrogations the defendant praduced a small tin of tobacco and a few cigars, saying they were all be had. Warless directed the other officer; to go through the captain's room, and on opening a locker Customs Officer T. E. Garner came across the articles praduced. The captain immediately admitted baying the articles on boarcrand hoped they would take a lenient view of the case.—Mr. James H. Hamilton,inspector of customs, asked as the defendant was the chief officer on the ship that treble value and duty be charged. He pressed for it as an exarnple.--,Defendant stated he was very sorry it had happened.— Elle Bench decided to mak., the defend- ant pay the double value and duty (£9 les 81), or in de- fault one rnonth's imprisonment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>DE&amp;F AND DUMB MAN'S &amp;RANGE DILEMIA.</head>
        <p>A DE&amp;F AND DUMB MAN'S &amp;RANGE DILEMIA.</p>
        <p>The Market Drayton police have, dtrieg the past few days, had to deal with a problem which they found very difficult of solution. They noticed a resp!ctably- dressed man aimlessly wandering about the town with a dilapidated pneumatic bicycle. The man proved to be deaf and dumb, and although confronted with other deaf and dumb plrsons, ceu'd not make himself no der- stood. A number of gentlemen in the town, both legal and professional, endeavoured to converse with him but failed in the attempt. The only assist ince the man could give the police was the sketeh of a bridge, which was ultimately recognised by Major Heber Percy (of Ho inet Ball) as a sketch of Runcorn Bridge, and this materially a.szisted in finding the man's vlatives. They discovered that his nariae is Samuel Wimpenny, of Kelsall. near Chester. He h,d been on a visit from Selsall to North- wich, to see a relative, and missed his way on the return journey. He met with further misfortune by losing the chain off his bicycle and dilapidating his tyre and had to spend one or two nights in the open air, as people were frightened whoa, by dumb motions, he asked for a bed. It appears that on a previous occasion he was unfortunate enough to wander a long way from home, and he then extricated himself from the dilemma by drawing a sketch of Runcorn Bridge.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>MERSEY biISSION TO SEAMEN.</head>
        <p>MERSEY biISSION TO SEAMEN.</p>
        <p>Oa Tuesday evening the weekly concert was given, in the Seamen's Institute, by the Widnes Borough M+o- strels. There was a good attendance, and a splendid programme was gone through, to the great delight of all present. The troupe, 17 in number, under the conduc- torship of Mr. S. Rawlins, rend ,red the following pro- gramme in an admirable rn inner. The cornermen attracted great attention by their unceasing wit, while the two sketches caused much merriment and laughter. Mr. Shaw presided, and at the close proposed a vo' e of thanks to the B orough Minstrels for their kindness in coming over to give the entertainment. ! This was carried unanimously with great applause Part I. Opening chorus, "Jingle Beth," Troupe ; comic song, "I'm one of tllo tcklish kind," Mr J Ryan; song, " Death of Nelson," Mr S Pops ; comic song,' Sop in de frying pan," Herr Khremann ; son, " Fighting lads of England," Master J Ratter ; comic son," Underneaf de old umbrella." Me G Oatram ; duet, "Larboard Watch," Messrs Pone and Rawlins ; comic song, "Mrs McCs,rthy's Part s';" Mr G R Jones ; comic song, "Razors in de air," Mr J Jones. Part 11. Gumorous sketch, "Bandy Andy," Andy, J Ryan, Mr Brown, S Rawlins sword o-xsrcise by Herr lincernann ; sketch, " Doctor's Shop," Metisrs Ryan, Rawlins, J ones and Edwards ; pianist, Mr J Hart; conductor, Mr S Pope ; interlocutor, Mr S /Walling. Tan STORY OF A " TEM.-POUND NOTE."</p>
        <p>Oa Wecineeday atternoon at the Runcorn FALCO oMce. before Mr. F J. Norman, J.P., an interesting case was heard in which John Clarke was charged under warrant with having on the llh of AD:if, at Rancorn, unlaw- fully by means of a trick stolen £3 10a ; the money of Arnold Uneworth.—From the evidence given it appeared that Arnold Una worth, is a seamen's outi- ter, having his place of business in Waterloo-road, Top Loeks, Runcorn. Oa the day in question the prisoner came to his place of busineas, and stated that recently he had been working for Mr. G-enn, panlor and decorator, in Church-street. He was about altering his occupation and going to sea. He had been to sea before, and required an outfit. Selecting articles to the value. of £8 10s, prisoner pulled a paper out of his pocket and told Mr. Unsworth it wis a "ten pound note," at the same time asking Mr. Unsworth if he could change it. Mr. [Jassy orth did so. Then there arose a question of a receipt stamp for the bll, and as there was none in the shop prisoner said that he would ge and fetch ona from the Bridgewater Arms (kept by Mr. Jones). He went, taking the " ten penal note" %vita him and also the £3 10i. change, but did not return.— Subsequently information was giveh to the police and as the result of telephoning all ever the countrV, news reached then that he Was being detained at Widnes from whence he was afterwards brought over to Runcorn by Police-Constable Gregory and Superinten- dent Pearson. As it was suspected prisoner was an old offender, a remand of 8 days was asked for and granted. —lt appears tut the paper exhibited by the pri=oner wa,a not a ten pound note.—A.akei by the magistrate why he did not ascertain it was a ten-pound nate prose- cutor stated "Things had been so slack. and he was s anxious to do builness he never doubted it."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>FATAL MNITAKR AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>A FATAL MNITAKR AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Oa Tuesday afternoon Mr. Brighouse (coroner) held an inquest at the Pollee S:ir,ion on the body of Annie Parker (55). a widow, who died at her residence, at 68. Priestley-street, on Sunday after- noon, under sad circums';ances. For some months the deceased, who was well known in the neigh- bourhood and much respectel,had acted in a strange .manner, causing her friends much anxiety. At tin minutes past two she was in the shop with her son. The latter ware, to this door, and turning round as he was about to leave, he SAW his mother drink- ing out of a bottle which, it was subsequently found, contained carbolic acid. Dr. Herd was at once sent for, the usual remedies being applied, but Sirs. Parker became unconscious and died at five n'clock.—Charles Parker, deceased's son, said that a bottle of carbolic abid was kept in a desk in the shop, and in a similar bottle in the same desk was whiskey,- which his mother usel to take. He believed that she took the poison under the impres- sion that it was the whiskey.—The Coroner said the jury were satisfied that deceased did not intend to lake away her life, ani returned a verdict ot "Death from misadventure."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>SYMPATHETIC SUGGESTION FROM THE LADIES.</head>
        <p>A SYMPATHETIC SUGGESTION FROM THE LADIES.</p>
        <p>The Ladies Committee reported teat on the stio inst they visited ths female waide aadsome of those devoted to male inmates, and found all in a highly satisfactory condition. The members of the Committee held a strong opinion that the aged and debilitated inmates shouid be allowed beef tea and strong nourishment more invariably than they believe to be the case at present. Tney had refrained from making this representation until obeeryationt and equities had convinced them that crnsideraeion of this questi on and some augmentation of present diet for inmates who were feeble wild weak and who had at the same time not contributed to their own misfortune (this the committee believed to be the case with some of the old people) was desirable. They were arse convinced that the Guardians wen? anxious to make every arrangement in their power through the kind help of the matron and all other offieials, for the relief of the weakness and emaciation which was so hard to endure and which a very meagre diet only perpetuated and could not relieve. A. R. Norman stated the matter had been before Vie committee and they decided to refer it to the Medical Officer for his observations. He rather thought teat the remarks of the Ladies Comeeittee referred to the special diets they had, beef tea, brandy and such things ; but it was left entirely to the Doctor to recommend thesaine. At the same tima,in fairness to the committee he wished to state that the committee made a strong point with regard to the treatment of two classes ef intratce—the aged and deserving and the children. The Chairman was also of opinion that the dealing out of the special articles of diet was entirely in the hands of the Doctor.</p>
        <p>Mr. Stokes asked whether that referred only to those inmates who were under medical tr-a,,ment ?</p>
        <p>The Master said the whale of the inmates were periodi- c Illy under the observation of the Doctor and he cauld ord, r any_of.th.m the special diets.</p>
        <p>_ . It was decided to submit the ladies' observatioas to, the Dortor, acd await his remarks.</p>
        <p>ODDFELLIWS AND THR GUARDIANS-THE " HANSEL RULE</p>
        <p>MODISPIIID.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Stokes said it would be remembered that at the last Board meeting he as chairman of the Relief Super- vision Committee had brought a little matter before the Board, about an old man Thomas Haelehurst, an inmate of ties house. Meet of them would have seen that there had ben certain correspondence in the papers regarding it. In that correspondence he was charged with having incorrectly stated the number of years Hafehtiret had been a member of the lodge of Oldfellowa. His autho- rity for making the statement was from the lips of the old man himself. As the committee were desirous of finding out the full partieulars of his case he was asked how many years he had been a menaber of the seciety and be replied he could not remember. He was then asked whether he could give an idea of the length of time? He then said he was a member before be was married, which he stated was 40 years ago. From what ho read it appeared that Haelehurat must have entered the lodge somewh-re about 33 year; ago. He was further charged with being incorrect in his description of the old man's removal to the house. The rule of the club distinctly stated that if a member beeeme an inmate of a Work- house or Asylum his benefit would cease to be paid. Therefore they wanted to know whethe: Heel-hunt had cerne into the house of his own accord P—Toe Court asked how it was Hezlehurst came C.) the House ?" He stated "I did not know I was confine here until they got me on the way." His (Nlr. Stokes') authority was therefore the old man hineself. He brought the subject before the Board by the direction of the committee. The new rule of the 0-idfellows came into force last July, and the committee thought it was of importance not only to the Guardians but to the public at large. It was a very serious step for any man to take, ad he should consider whether his benefit should cease as soon as he became an inmate of a Workhouse or Asylum. Mr. T. Reynolds expressed a wish to say a few words in substantiating Mr Stokes. It was quite true that the old man declared that he had been in the society for 40 years, and it was also true that he declared he had no knowledge whatever of his coming to the house until he was on the way. As Mr. Stokes had remarked there had been considerable correspondenee since the last Board day, and he was glad of it, because it seemed te have raised a certain amount of feeling that had been lying dormant, and 'perhaps ultimately good might coins o! it. Of course,in correspondence,people must have some- thing to fire their shots at, and he along with Mr.Stekis had been assailed. For his own part he should have allowed the thing to pass over, only it was quite true that he made, in error, a statement that he oneht not to have made. He contended that morally speaking,what- ever the law might be, that if the Guardians received sufficient money to keep anyone in the Workhouse those persona were not paupers. He had stated at the last meeting that the action of the society had p tuperise the man. At that time he had not have the sliehest idea that the man had already been receiving 3i. per week from the Guardians, otherwise he would not have made the statement. He sow apologised fully and wished to withdraw the statement. Any other remark he had made which harl been referred to in the correspondence he had no inclination or desire to withdraw from, and he stood be his statement that the rule Was an injustice. (Hear, hear.) Mr. T. Wright static' in respect to any cruelty to which the man had been subj acted it had been inflicted by the Guardians. He wished to state that when Rule- hurst was receieine 3i. a week out-relief he followed his trade as a shoemaker, and in that way earned several shillings per week. The moment the old man became ill and unable to follow his occupation and received 3a. from his club, the Beard of Guardians stopped his oat- relief, leaving him only 31. a week from the Club to live upeo, and at a time to ) where he needed better pro- visioes and more nourishment. Mr. Wright con- sidered the system oF stipping out-relief because a man received a few shilling from a Cleb shoeld be put an end to, and the man should be allowed the money addi- tionally-.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Meadowcroft complimen'ed Mr. T. Reynolds upon his manliness in making an apology. He objected to the word pauperism. The children had been removed from the House to an outside school to remove that taint, and there was also a disposition to remove any such stigma in sward to adults. That being the dis- position of the members great care should be taken in applying the term pauper or paoperism to kmates of the House who had been respectable, decent, hardworking people all their lives, as the men Hazielsurst had been. There should be some consideration for their feelings and for the feelings of their relatives. (Hear. Ivar.) There was much talk of old age pensions, and if there were to be such pensions, money would have to cone out of the rates, and they might just as well apply the term pau- per to the people who received the pensions. With regard to the correspondence, at the last meeting, ment;oned that the rule had been approved by the Registrar General ; and they should consider that these societies had to conduct their business 02 finaneial lines in order to maintain substantitl benefits for their mem- bers. He knew clubs that had gone down for want of such management. He uniorenood that the Guardians had applied for money from clubs when persons ha come into the Workhouse and the money had not hem paid, and yet there had been no complaint to the Beard about it. It did seem singular that this particular society should be singled out for especial strong and severe remarks, when others had been allowed to pass. Since the subject had been given publicity he had been told of one or two cas-s where pressure had losers exerted to obtain payment, though it had not bsen ob. tamed. Be had no doub; this society felt that hardship was b?ing dealt out to them as compared with the treat- ment of others. He did not thiek that if the real facts of the case bad been known any observation would hay been made, or the matter brought before the Board.</p>
        <p>Mr. Stokes clasidered Mr. Mladowcroft had given rise to a remark that w3uld Larry the insinuation that the Conn had singlei out that particular society and paaqed others over. He wished to refute that. Mr. Baxter a;ked whetner they were to understand fr3tra Mr. Fro-Manes report that other club had always paid. and that it was the first case of clubs refusing to pay? Mr. T. Reynolds : Iraq, that is so. Mr. Stokes • That was the impression taken lo; the m.misers of the committe.</p>
        <p>Mr. Frodsham alal plinted out several &amp;Alm that ;lad paid, and gave their names. Mr. Meadowcr3ft stated he had been given to under- stand that the clubs they were discusqing were not the only delinqoentelp a member of the Manchester [Laity Branch of Oddfellows.</p>
        <p>The Chairman thought the eirplanation all that could be desired. Mr Reynolds had honourably apologised on two points that seemed to be heart barning to the -6ociety. • The matter dropped. [We understand that the true facts of the case are that John Hazlehurst, when he became ill, received lOs a week from the Oldfelows.and as he gradually dropood into the 35 a week allowance the Goardians re-alllwed him the sum of 3s a week until he 13,came an inmate; and that the Guardians only disnntinued his cut-relief during the period he was in receipt of more than 3s a week from his club</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>THANKS TO THE CHAIRMAN.</head>
        <p>THANKS TO THE CHAIRMAN.</p>
        <p>The Chairman observed that they were all aware that was the haat meeting of that Boari. Twelve manthi ago they did him the honour of electing him to thi chair and he hoped he had filled it with satisfaction to them all. He fully appreciated the honour and nsponsibility nf the position they had honoured him witb. He thanked elm for their co-operati)n and ex oresaed a hope that uey would meet again. He &amp;so howd they would mast their Clerk again—(hear,bear)—and he took that oppor.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <p>tunity of thanking him for his kind co-operation. He vacated the chain thanking them all for their many in- dulgenc,sl.—On the proposition of Mr. C. E. Linaker, seconded by Mr. T. Wright a hearty vote of thanks was accordsd Mr. Williams for his past services.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN RURA.L DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The firtnightly meeting of this Council was held at Dutton Workhouse. Oti Tuesday, when there were pre- sent :—Mmsrs J Knowles (chtirman), J S^,okes, J Mc- Intyre, Adnairal Jones Parry, W Fletchir, T Darlington, G Mien, C E Liaaker, T Baster. C C., J B Cnadwick, T Wright, P Leather, T Lwis, E Bretnerton, T Antro- but, P Gleave, W Bradford, H falbotc. J.P., with Mr George F Ashton (deouty clerk), and Massre Diggle and Swinton (surveyors.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>MOIRE WATERCOURSE.</head>
        <p>A MOIRE WATERCOURSE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Francis Wiswali, of the Manchester Ship Canal Company, wrote under date March 29th, with reference to the Moss-lane watercourse, at Moore, stating they proposed cleaning out the ditch as requested. He also stated that if the ditch was piped by the owner, not less than 24 inch pipes would serve their purpose. In reply to the Chairman, Mr. Swinton (surveyor), said there were no pips put into the ditch at present, only preparations for doing s 3 weie going on. The matter was considered satisfactory.</p>
        <p>ILLNESS OF THE MEDICAL OFFICER AND SURVEYOR.</p>
        <p>Dr. Carruthers wro:e stating that he was attending Mr. Farrington, sanitary inipector, who WI3 conuned to bed, suffering from sciatica, and unable to attend to his duties.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Adams, wife of Dr. Adams, medical officer,wrote under date April 9:h :—" lam sorry to have t tell you that Dr. Adams had a serious accident on Friday last. The horse was startled by the swindle bar of the dog cart snapping, and bolted. Dr. Adams and the man were both thrown out, but, fortunately, no one was killed. Considerable dam age was done, and the trap is smashed to atoms and (pits bsyand repair. Dr. Adams has hurt his right knee rather badly, and his left arm was much bruised. fie asks me to say he will net be able to leave his bed for a week, bat hopes by dictation to complete the report in a day or two. A. considerable portion of it is already in duplicate manuscript." Admiral Parry asked whit the Council would do in the case of any diseases breaking out?</p>
        <p>It was poinied otr tat the doctor had a deputy, and with respect to Air.Farrinzton it was decided to wait till neat fortnight and se how he progressed. A general opinion was expressed wishing them b3th a speedy recovery.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>SUTTON PAROCIIIAL AFFAIRS.</head>
        <p>SUTTON PAROCIIIAL AFFAIRS.</p>
        <p>At a spleiaT meeting of the Sutton Parochial Committee held on the 30Lh March,Mr.C.E.Linaker presiding, it was resolved unanimously that from and after the Ist day of April, 1899 occupiers of all farm ;in the parish of Sutton, to which the public water supply is laid oi be charged for such supply eiclier by meter at the rate of 11. per 1,000 gallons of water usnd,or at the rats of 61. per celt per annum en the rateabl value of the house, and 2s. per head per annum for each horse or cow on tle premises for which the water is used.</p>
        <p>The minutes were pews I. THANKS TO THE RETIRING CHAIRMAN.</p>
        <p>Mr. C. E Llnakar proplied a vet of thanki to Mr. Knowles their retirinz C tairman, azi expteised a hope that they would be able to re-elect him. Mr. Bitter seconded and thank :d Mr Know!aq for the Patience and indulgence he had extended to them whilst he filled the chair. The vote was carried wits acclamation.</p>
        <p>Mr. Knowles, responding, said it had always been a pleasure for him to preside there. He took that oppor- tunity of thanking the Clerk (Mr. John Ashton) for his valuable assistance, and tin vic3-chairmin and the deputy clerk, for it was always easy to work har- moniously when surrounded by friends. (Applause,)</p>
        <p>FINANCE REPORT.</p>
        <p>The financial statement Arm unanimously a,dopted on the motion of Mr. T. Wiight, seconded by Mr. J. B. Chadwick. The payments included an item of £673 103. 10J. on acc-mat of the opposition of the L. &amp; N.-W. Railway Company in the matter of the Helsby Sewer- age.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN URBA.N iiisra-auf COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>THE ESTIMATES.</p>
        <p>A special meeting o. the Runcorn Urban District Council was held at the Town Hall, on Wednesday night. There were present :—Messrs Lightburn (in the chair), E unison, J Pritchard, W Handley, W Gatley, .T Horsetield, J Wilson, J Swee'enan, T Dean, George Hewitt, and D Biebrewn. with Mr F Lake (law clerk), Mr E Marshall (clerk), and Me Jetnes Wilding (surveyor.) Mr. Itnieen moved that the General District Rate be fixed at 3s 3d in the £, and that is 71d be collected in the first six months, and is 711 in the sec-ind, to meet the estimate of the Council up to the 31st March 1901 With respect to the assessment and levying of the General District Rate for the year ending March 31st, 1900, it was recommended "that the owner inatead of the occupier be rated under the provision of Section 21.1 of the Public Health Act, 1875, where the ratable value of any premises liable ta assessment does not exceed the sure of £lO and that he be assessed on the reduced estimate of of the net annual value of the pr3perty." The expenditure was estimated at £8,251 which with a deficiency of £782 made a total of £9.033 required for the twelve months. Tne estimated incem exclusive of- the rate am )unted to £1,620 10 s which left £7,413 103 to be provided for. He fe:t sure the public would be please.' to know there hal been no increase in the burden which they were asked to bear. The Council. the Committees, and the officiate of the Council were to be congratulate I upon their ecooomicel menegemeet during the past year. (Hear, hear.) They had all made up their minds at the outset of last year determined as far as possible to keep the committees to the amount of ex- penditure allocated to them, and also with a determina- tion to succeed in reducing tee adverse balance by £3OO. They had practically been euccessfel for they succeeded in reduclne the arneunt by £2B just £l.l short of their proposed figure. (Hear, bear.) Considering all circum- stances and that they had ht I s,-)lne extra sexpenditare their congratulations were justified, foe estimate of income and expenditure was practically the same as last year and there were no special items that called fur remark. They would be ,g'al to kno v,heweeer,that the rateable value had gradually increaeed,a.nd had been doing so for a number of years. During the present year id in the £ would produce something like £lB2, whilst last year £1.79 was the figure, and previous years even less than that. With regard to the future he hoped they would be able to reduce the deficiency to £469, which was what they were providing for, and he considered that to reduce what might be callel a steediag defici- ency having something like £503 in two mire was so:nething to be proud of. (Hear, hear.) Mr. J. Wilson seconded the resoletien, and endorsed Mr. Imison's remarks. He did not think .as business men they could decrease the rate any further. The Cheirnsaa observed that the index of the wslFare of the town was the amount of rate nrodueel by id in the L. lie was glad te see it on the increase, and felt certain that they had now passed the low water mark. (Hear, hear.) - _</p>
        <p>Tho reiolution wis then put and untnini)usly agreed to.</p>
        <p>DROWNED IN Tile; NIA.SCEIESTER SHIP CANAL. A GENEROLTS COMPINY.</p>
        <p>On Thursday morning Mr. J. E. Worsley, coroner, held an inquest on the body of Alfred Conner, age-I 32, of 48, Shaw-street, an emplove of the United Alk iii Works company, who lost his life on Tuesday morning by falling into the I‘l%nn..ster Ship Canal, whilst en- gaged in fiKing a plank preparatory to disnarging the fist "Elton Fold" on which he was engaged.—Mr. EL N. Linaker, solicitor, appeared for the widow, and Mr. F. A. Lake, solicitor. for the U sited Alkali Works Clm- pany and for the Incur ancs )mpany.--Atr. F. J. /sTlr- man, manager of the United Alkali (Wigg%) Works was al- o present..</p>
        <p>George Wainwright, York-street, forern an of the Wigs Works, produced a plan of the place where the accident happened, showing the two flits" Eton F " and" Annie" lying near tee wharf ready far the work of discharging 0 go on. There was only a space of about two feet betweee the two flats. It was dark but the usual mode of going to work was observed : a plank being fixed across the flat for the purpose of holding lights. From what he understood it was whilst this blank was being fix,cl that the man fell into the water. It was not the foreman's duty to fix the lights. The dis- chargers always did it themselves. James Carter, Hilton Brooke, cep`ain of the " Elton Fold" said it would be about 3 o'clock on Tuesday morn- ing when they were about to commence the work of dis- ehargieg. Deceased brought the lights and he took two of them down the ladder and deceased brought the nth-r. They would be fixing the plank when the deceasEd fell into the water. He did not see him fall, but was told that" he had fallen int) the water " by another man. There were two or three shafts on the deck,a,ud d,ceas'd might have stepped on one of those and faller.' overbe Deceased was perfectly sober and the accid4nt WAS not caused by any carelessness on his part. He recovered the deceased's body about twi hoursaf ervra-de. There were no marks neon it that he could tell of.—ln answir to the solicitors witness said he could only sugge-t that the man stumbled and fell into the water, for as soon as he had put down his end of the plank he heard that deceased had fallen into the water. Witness heard no splash or anything. The Coroner in summing up. complimented the wit- neves upon the straightforward way in which they had given evidence, and said it all pointei to a "purely ae.• cidental cause of death."—The jury concurred, and brought in a verd'c; aceordingly. Mr. Linaker on behalf of the widow wished to thank the managers of the Wiggs Works for their kindness and coasideration. He understood it was their intention t give her what compensation they could. • The Coroner ohserved that the company always acted (as he had known from their conduct in previous cases), in a generous spirit. They always acted well, and he was pleasel to testify to it.</p>
        <p>Lk-3 thanked them for th.fir remark, and ob- serve&amp; it was thought by the company it was the best way of expressing the condolence of the comp iny, with the widow. (Hear, hear.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>RUNCORN CHARACTER SKETCHES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN CHARACTER SKETCHES.</p>
        <p>BY " Q.E.F.D." No. 7.</p>
        <p>"The pegs of the world are ever renewed "is a truism w: ich each succeeding age verifies; and as the primrose of 50 years ag) resembled that of to- day, 80 does the " fussvman " of 50 years ago re, samble him of to-day. Yet, there is a distinctive- ness about our local fussy one, that makes us take much more interest in him than in his prototype. As with the primrose—he's of our own growing— we saw him bud, and watched his development keenly.</p>
        <p>If you would picture him externally, draw up before you the portly Pick wick,the beaming benev- olent face, and the spec's witball ; then toning with a sprinkling of side wiskers—(just to taste) —you have the subject of our sketch standing oat in bold relief. He is one to whom the town is greatly indebted for long and honourably has he served its interests upon prominent boiies, and in business he is most punctilious. His greeting is benevolently superior and at times verges upon condezicension yet jg ever accompanied with the predominating trait—"fuss." Strange it is, though none the less true, the fault we most see in others is our own. So with he of our remarks, a fussy man he abhors, and detests to be told he has a tendency to pro-. crastinate the despatch of public business, whilst following his hobby and delving into the "mint, aniseed and cinnamon," though omitting "the weightier matters of the law." Still be does so,and in a p-culiarly persistent manner. What would have become of our prehistoric forefathers, if they suddenly had been deprived of their "fussy man." Could the succeeding years have been covered with- out him? It is doubtful ! But, whether they of time gone could have existed without him? or whether we of time present would care to be with-, out him? The fact remains he is with us in the 19th Century, and stands ready to welcome us in the 20th, with just as much fuss as ever. He is not mean, neither, can he be said to be benevolent—he's just fussy that is all! His every action is pervaded with it; and ware it possible it might be seen bubbling out of his pockets. When he gets up in the morning there is a fuss, and when he retires at night there is another fuss. On the way to and from his office the jouraey can never be accom- plished without some one or oth-r being button-, holed and fussed into either a good or bad temper, though 'is oftener the latter. hould he preside at a meeting, and often he does So preside, the "bustle" presides also, and try how he will will find vent though not down on the programme. How- ever, though uur subject is 80 exceedingly fussy, many are the smart business transactions and the political intrigues he has bean engaged in with credit to himself, and honour to the town. He is known to both rich and poor tho Ugh neither class love him. In fact, the latter rather look upon him as an op- pressor,whilst the former are entirely indifferent as to his existence or demise, though he is of opinion that he is the axis upon which all the universe— but especially that part of it which affects Runcorn —revolves around. Intensely self-opinionated, our fussy one will fret and fume, and struggle for a point in debate on Board or Council, and whilst act- ing as dictator will suffer none to dictate with impunity.</p>
        <p>This Work in Runcorn has been useful in many respects, and though at times marred by austerity, a retrospect of some 30 years c3mpares favourably with contempsraries, tie is not at all popular, but he is decidedly well known. Hs cannot be said to be highly respected in the popular sense, though he is respected,and within his own business sphere his opinion is greatly valued. He is proud and of an unbending nature, even tempered, calculating, exacting, but treats religious matters with stoical indifference, going and coming to a place of wor- ship,as the vast majority of church-goers do,simply as a matter of course ; may be business.</p>
        <p>Usefulness, might be the object, Of men, constructed thus : Trying to bette, mike their land, Everything they take in hind, Ac!omplish with a fuss.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>RUNCORN NOTES,</head>
        <p>RUNCORN NOTES,</p>
        <p>ttY A COIMESPONDENT The Runoorn Free Church Council will, on Mon- day, hold its inaugural meeting at the St. Paul's Wesleyan Church when speeches will be delivered by the Rev. Robert Veitch, NE.A., Liverpool, and sir. Percy W. Bunting M.A., (editor of the " Contem- porary Review "j Loudon, and others. This is a step in the right direction and all members interes- ted in the Free Churches should attend and assist in making the Council a power for goad.</p>
        <p>The discussion at the Board of Guardians meet- ing respecting the refusal of theOddfellow's Club to pay 33. a week to the Guardians ot the Runcorn Union towards the maintenance of John flazlehurst has developed somewhat. Mr. Meadowcroft in stating that "had the Relief Supervision Committee bean conversant with the actual facts of the case they would not have brought it forward." covers the whole of the ground. From the statements made by the majority of the Guardians it is quite evident they do not yet grasp the situation. Mr. T. Reynolds apologised for his asper.3i.on upon the club though he still considered the rule an injustice. Mr. Stokes thought that if there had been any mistake and incorrect statement it arose out of mts-concegt- tion of the information of the c4llector (Mr. Frod- sham). Yet he found fault with Mr. Meadowcroft for practically telling him so I</p>
        <p>In a short time it will be the duty of the Board of Guardians and the District Council to appoint their chairmen. The retiring gentlemen are Mr. J. Mor- ley Williams (chairman of the Guardians), sad Air. Joseph Knovvles(chairman of the District Canned). From the general feeling manifested it is quits on the cards that the two gentlemen will be re-elected, though there is another gentleman mentioned as likely to be requested co stand for the chairmauship of the Guardians. A gentkman, tao, whose experi. erica in the management of the house must be ex-. tensive.</p>
        <p>Oa the 19Gh the UrbAn District Council will be called upon to elect its Coairtnan. Mr. Samuel Taylor is the gentleman who retires from the post, which he has occupied for the last two years. And his election a third time would be assured were be to consent to sit; but this Mr. Taylor is not de,:irous of doing. Then comes the question who will re- ceive the honour as there are three or four gentle- man on the Council whose long service deserve:re- cognition.</p>
        <p>The vice-chair has been !Mei by Mr. J. Vir Light- burn, who upon the occasions he has !nen called to occupy the premier position, ha-, always done so with considerable credit. T lousTh it does not ap- pear to be a precedent, to offer the chair to the re- tiring vice-chairman, when such a capable man iS concerned, lam inclined to think, it not only would be courteous, but it would even be politic GO offer the honour each successive year to the retiring viCe- chairman. •</p>
        <p>The General District Rate for Rucora Urban Dis- trict was fixed on Wed.neslay night at a special Council meeting at 3s. 31, in the X, which is the same as that of last year. It is gratifying ta ob- serve that the Council kept well within their esti- mate of last year. and ware successful in reducing the adverse balance by £289, notwithstanding the fact that several special items of expenditure cropped up. The total amount required by the Council is £9,033 of which sum £1,620 is estimated income, the balance .£7,413 being required from the rate. It is satisfactory to note that the rateable value of Runcorn is an the increase,which indicates. that the town has commenced upon an upward grade of prosperity. May it continua so.</p>
        <p>ACCIDENT AT EARLFSTOWN. An unfortunate accident resulting in serious loss to Mr. S. Walton, cab proprietor, happened in Fair- clough-street, Earlestown, on Tuesday afternoon. It appears Mr. Walton was executing a funeral Dr- dor, and one of his men was driving a mourning coach, to which were attached two Belgian horses, when the pole slipped oat of the tcv.ket, and being on an incline, there was nothing to stop the coach, which gained speed and forced the h9rses on. A cart was just in front, and into the back of this tile off hone was forced the cosch behind jamming it against the carr,and severely injuring it. When re- leased it was found to be paralyed in both hind legs and after being dragged into Efatton's yard,was killed. Tne coach was filled with mouratTs, who were naturally much alarmed.</p>
      </div>
