      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>THEFTS OF PIGEONS BY BOYS. On Mond ey at the Werrineton Biroußh Police Court, before Mr. Monks (in the chair) and other magistratts, John Dickinson (13), Richard Newton (12), Thome% Curry (10), and George Forester (9), were charged with stealing nine pigeons, value 93 from a cote belonging to Mr John Lunt, St. Jarneestreot, Latchford, between the 13th and Btn April.—Dickinson and Forster were else charged with stealing two pigeon, value is 61 between the 2nd and 3rd April, belonging to Mr. John Norbary, WilderePnol-read.—Diekinson, Curry. and Forster were further charged with stealing nine pigeone value 93 from Mr. Joseph Bridge's cote, Sixpenny-fields.—Defendants ;plop ded guilty.—The Chief Constable said Mr. Loot kept pigeons in a loft in Sr. Jamei-street, aril on Thursday last one of his emploser named Lewtnn saw the pigeons safe and the premiees seeure. On Saturday moraine he found that nine pigeons were missing,and he also found a number of young birds dead in the nests. Information was given to the police, and it was found that Dickin- son heel sold four pigeons to Mr. Clare, bird dealer, Flag-lane. and the other five to Mrs. Locker, 8, Mill- street. on Friday morning. Dickinson was taken to the Felice Station, and made a statement which led to the arrest ot the other defendants. Further inquiries fouod that Dickinson and Forster had stolen two birds from Mr. Norbury and sold them. Dickinson, Curry, and Forster also broke into Mrs. Bridge's cote and took nine pigeons. Mr. Clare gave 61. each for till birds, and Mrs. Lecker sd. each.—The Chairman stated that the magistrates did not want to make gaolbirds of defend- ants,but Dickinson would be kept in the cells three days and have twelve strokes with the birch. Carry and For- ster, six each, and be kept in the cells for a day. A CHECK TO FIIRIOUS DRIVING. At the Warrington Borough Police Court on Monday before Mr. F. Monks pre-iliag and other magistrates, George Hutchinson, a young man, described as a butcher's assis'ant, living at 17, Greenestreet, was charged with having on the third April furiously driven a horse and trap along the Wilderep lel-read, to the dan- ger of passengers.—John Henry Beclrett,l6 years of ace, said that at eleven o'clock on the morning in qu,stion he was near the Norton Arms Hotel when he saw the de- fendant in charge of a butcher's horse; he was driving furiously, and he saw him strike the animal three times. The horse had its neck stretched out and its head nearly touching the floor. It Vriq trotting very fast.—The Chief Coneteble (alluding to the pesition of the htrse): I never beard of a horse perfohning that gymnastic feat yet. (Laught,r)—Witeess added that there were many people on the road at the time. Toe defendant drove furiously over the hridge.—James Ferriman, a youth, living at Platt Bridge. Wigan, said he was on a biceele crossing the bridge when defendant's trete knocked him off the machine, which was damaged, and he veal also hurt.--Seven or eight witnesses were ca.lled.ell of whom declared that the cleferviart was driving furiously.— Sergeant Evans said that the defendant denied to him that he was driving furiously. Ho also denied havine knocked down the bicyclict —Hetchir yin said the horse went at a slow trot over the bridge. If he had ..reeeelee the boy he would have been killed stone deal."—Defen- dant called two witnesses,one of whom gave his evidencl in such an unsatisfactory rnennner that Councillor Shaw Green told him to "stand down."-4 vnuth named Thompeon said he was in the trap with the defendant and the la=t witness. The horse did not touch the machine.—The Chief Constable Rave Foe any other witness P—Defendant I have, but they have not " arrows," (Loud laughter.)—The Chief Constable said be had received many complaints about the furious driving of butchers' carts. Anyone going about :the town could see that it was really a great scandal.—The Chairman (Mr. Monks), said the Bench were cone, eatie- fied that the defendant had been guilty ot furious driv- ing, and he would he fined 40s. including coats. ALLEGED THEFT FROM A PADDYSZINT. At the Warrington Oleasional nowt on TWOIV. fore Mr. F. A. Frost, Michael McDermott alias Denis Donnesin, waa charred oe remand with steeling a quantity of Provisions (bread, butter. bacon, cheese and sugar), on Friday last from the paddeshant of M. Fred 'Weir, Higher Whitley, and with aFsiulting Mrs. Weir at the same time and plece.—No evidence was eiven, and the defendant was remanded to the Petty Sessions on the 25th of this month.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>MINOR CASES.</head>
        <p>MINOR CASES.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOROUCiff POLICE COURT. lidorovr —Before Mr. F. Monks (chairman). and 4essrs. Birehell and S. Wright and Council'ors Shaw Green and Smethuret. LICENSE TRANSFERSI,—Mr. Jenkins apnlied for the transfer of the license of the Feathers Hotel. Bridge-street, to Mr. J. Lyon Whittle, Town Clerk —The Chief Constable: Rven on the ground of character I think there is no obje,tion. (Laughter.)—The Chairman Is the license being held tem- porarily ?—Mr. Jenkins : That is so —Mr. Birohall : The place is not very big. It's only a shanty now.—The applica- tion was granted.—The following transfers werealso granted: 'Queen's Hotel, from Stephen Stirrup to William Cherry ; off- license 38. Pinner'a-brow, from Clara J. Stephen to Annie Evans; Gledstone Arms (beerhouse). from Walter • John Hewitt to Robert Eyes: Royal Oak Branch (beerhouse), from Robert Eves t) James Thomas Bite; Ring ','Bells. from William McElroy to William Walter Whittle, and off-licenee, Mill-lane, from William Allen to Mary Allen (h)s wife) AN OLD HAND AT THE GAME. are Ann McQueen, nf no settled abode, was charged with stealing from Mr R. V. Morton's pawnshop. Winwick-street, on Saturday night, a man's jecket value 12s. —Prisoner pleaded guilty. —The Chief Constable said nrisoner's proper name was Rylands, Shortly; after ten o'clock on Saturday night Thomas Hanson, assis- tant to Mr. Morton. saw prisoner go to one o' the vestibeles at the shop and steal a men's coat from the 'rack. She then ran away and he followed her. Prisoner ran down a court and Hanson lost sight of her. He then gave information to the police. Prisoner was an old hand and had srverel times been convicted. Twelve months ago she had had nine month's imprisonment far theft.—Prisoner was sent to gaol for one month. A finu.'s PILE MING. —MIMI Wilson, a girl about 15. was charged with stealing from the house of Mr. Alfred Perris. • Sankey-street, two gold dress rings, a gold brooch, pair of gold ear-rings, a gentleman's silver weteh and weird. and other jewellery, altogether of the value of £B.—The Chief Constable said that since the let January the prisoner had been in the habit of going two or three times each week to Mr. Ferris's house to clean and char. The jewellery men- tioned in tha charge w:s seen in the wardrobe, to which the girl would have access. On Saturday a number of the articles were missed, and information waa given to Superin- tendent Monaghan, who. let.- r in the evening, met prisoner in the Market and told her of his suspicions, but she indig- nantly denied the charge. Hnwever, the Superintendent pointed out the significant fact that she was wsa.ring one of the rings St the tispn. She t nen replied. " It is all I have taken." The Superintendent took her to the police station. where a search was made, and piwntieltets relatinr to the 'brooch, tce , were found in her possession. At her aunt's house (where she lived) another ta,ticle of jewellery was</p>
