      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <p>found. and ultinvitely she admitted the whole of the offence —The girl =seated to be taken to a home by Mr. Taylor. police court missionary. lihe was also bound over in the sum of £5 for nix months to come up for judgment if called upon.</p>
        <p>WEDNFSDAY.—Before 'Messrs. Geddes (chstrmln) and Mr. Birch&amp;ll.</p>
        <p>AitSnß 019 Es.—John Skelhorn was fine]. 5s and costs or seven days for being drunk and disorderly on Tuesday morn - ing.—qichael Mahon was mu'eted in a similar amount for being drunk in Winwick-street.—John Stanton was fined 10s and costs or 14 days Lir hsing drunk whilit riding a horse in Liverpool-road. the previous night —Defendant said he wa3 exercising the horse for Magian Bros. THURSDAY.—Bdfore Hr._ John Fatrelough (chairman) and</p>
        <p>Alrlormln Sutton</p>
        <p>DRUNK AND DVIORDERLY.—Margaret Kennedy, last from Liverpool, was charged with being drunk and disorderly at a quarter to one that morning. —Sergeant livens stated that the prisoner was sitting on a doorstep singing a song of an ob- scene nature. Bhe Was drunk and made use of Obscene lan- guage when arrested.- -The prisoner who on the occasion of her previous visit to Warrington was sentenced to seven days Imprisonment, was now cJminitted to prison for a like term,</p>
        <p>RUNCORN PETIT SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>MOND A.T.—Bef ore Messrs. A. Thomas (presiding) and John</p>
        <p>Ookleston</p>
        <p>LICENSING BUSlNElS.—Temporary authority • was pranted to James Tatlook. to sell at the. " Holyhead Harbour " High- street. :vitt also to John Marsh to sell at the" Oddfellows Arms " Wellington-street.</p>
        <p>THE " BLACK LIST."--Joseph Walker was summoned for being drunk and disorderly in Percival-lane, on the 13th of April.—Defendant pleaded guilty.—The case was proved by Police Constable W. Wylie. of Westnn.—Pinert 5s and costs or 14 days.—Mary Ann Atherton pleaded guilty to being drunr and disorderly in Heath-road. Weston, on the previous Saturday night.—Pollee Constable Wylie proved the case. It appeared that the woman had ben found at the corner of the road user to Church-row. in a helplessly drunken condition. The Constable after considerable tie:l2bl° broulOt her to Runcorn Pollee Station.—Defendant stated she was subject to fits, and it must have been a fit she had for previously she had been "teetotal" for some time.—The Clerk reminded her that she had pleaded guilty to the charge —Defendant : 0, I have nothing to say I—ln consideration of her not being up before the Bench since 1893. defendant was let off on payment of 5s inclusive of costs.—James Robinson was brought up in custody. charted with being drunk end diserderly In High- street on the 7th of March lest.—lnspector Hicks who proved the case stated that the defendant was most abusive with his language, and was interf -ring with almost everyone he met. —ln answer to the Bench defendant stated he was guilty. The reason he went away was to search for some work so as to raise sufficient money to pay a fine. He wos unsuccesAul and had given himself up that morning —lt was pointed out that losegoinz away the defendant ha, 1 increased the casts be 14s.—Defendant's sister appeared in court, and as the result of an eloquent appeal the Bench fine 4 him 6s and Hs costs. A.NOTHER CASE OF DRIVING IN THE DARE. —Thomas Law- son, of 2, Warburtoa's Court. Warrington, was Drought up charged with being drunk whilst in charge of a horse and °art, and also with driving same without a light on Saturday night, Bth April—Defendant admitted haying some drink, but not sa much that he did not know what he was about.— Polies-Constable Samuel Gregory dvosed to being on duty in Greenway-road on the Saturday night; about 11-30 he saw a cart being driven down the road furiously, and having no light attached. Running after it. he overtook it, and found that the defendant was also drunk. On taking him to the Police Station defendant threatened the offie.ar, saying he "had a mind to take his eves oet," using bad language at the same time. There was no lamp on the cart.—Prison-r hat no questions to ask and nothleg to say.—For being drunk, whilst in charge he was fined 103 and be Costs; and 53 inclu- sive of cests for being without a light.</p>
        <p>HG4 STELLING AT lILLToN. --Ernest Hayes (14), and Thomas Hayes (14), of Halton-road and Woods-row. were summoned by Mr Joseph Walker, Haliwood Farm, Halton, for stealing 37 eggs, veined at 4s. between the 23th Marla and the 4th of April Wt.—Both boys pl,aied guilty. Joseph Walker, farmer, stated that on the 28th of ',larch last he engaged the defeu tants to drop potatoes. Subsequently he got suspicious that someone was stealing his eggs. though he did not suspect the boys. Accidentally going one day Into the cart-house he noticed a jacket lying on the floor wish eggs in the pocket. As the result of euqui -lea he ascer- tained the jacket belonged to rnest laves. He then ques- tioned the boys and th began telling of one another, and it was then he found out that one was as bed as the other. He gave information to the police.—rolic —Omit +We Upton, stationed at Halton, said he received information of the thefts about 1 o'clock ore Tiles fay, the 4th inst. He went to the fares and searched th 3 boys there, finding seven eggs upon them (4 on one, and 3on the other). He produced 5. two having fallen and broken. The boys admitted taking the eggs on different days. They were ordered to receive 12 strokes of the birch.</p>
        <p>WINDOW GREASING AT RITICORN.--AblrahaM Ltlnt (14), Bdward Blaekinore (14), John 1330th (13), end William Para- worth (14), pleaded guilty to wilful +lamage to proeerty, on the 25th of Marcia. by breelcmg 12 panes of glass in the windows of a dwelling house, the preperty of Mr. John Rueben Peacock.—Ar. Peacock said he was a farmer. The property damaged was a cottage siteate on his farm at dieser Runcorn. On the date nettled about a dozen pa es had been broken. He did not see the offence committed, but gave infermetion to the police.—Edw tri Williams, aged 13, of 14. Ivy-street, said he saw the boys break the windows by throwing stones He told P .lice-constable Wylie Police-constable Wylie carroborated.—Eliackrnore stated he did not throw any stont.s.—The boys were discharged on payment of costs 53 each. Has. HENRY IlawKisre " SUICKIN3ES HER llllSisteru Henry Hawkins, captain of the coasting vessel " Truth Seeker" of Runcorn, was summoned on the information of his wife Mary Hawkins, of 24, Lugsdele-rold, Whines, in that she desired to obtain a separation order under the Married Woman's Alt, 1893.—N1r. H. W. Linaker appeared for applicant, and Mr. Me' cher. solicitor, Northwiels, was for the defenee.—Mr Linek-r said it W 43 an application for maintenance on the part of the wife It w.iuld appear that Hawkins had left his wife on the 17th of October last and since teen she had only received £2 towaedi her mainten- enee.—Complain ant cave evidence as to her hush old leaving her on the 17th of October. She received £2 from him on the 20th of October. Sto had been unible to obtain goo is at the local tradesmen's shops.lShe resided in Lei 'star Gar tens. There were no children of thsmarriege. About six or seven weeks ago she left, her hashancl'a house and went ii, live with her son at 24, Lngsdaie-road. Widnes ; she did that be- cause she could get no money. frier husband WAS in receipt of good pay and would get £5O from October to Christmas. if he made the r mud which he did.- cross- examined by Mr. Fletcher witness admitted that her husband had provided a good home. the bad been rece;viiig 103. a week from her husband up to a short time ago. With regard to the furniture of the house, she had sold some ot it. nut refused t 3 account for the money She had sold chairs, "what nets " and ornaments and had lived on part of the money, but it was not enough to keep her. Her husband had told her tó go to Ilewitt's for go sls but she had only beers able to get 3s, worth or so. Later she stated she got ss. 3d. worth. Complainant had up to a certain period accompanied her husband on his voyages. She denied at first that she was addicted to drink, but subsequently stated she used to get excited. She admitted hitting her husband on the head with a teapot.and that he would carry the mirk of It to his gray,. On the 3rd of June, they were in Thurso harbour 81so isi leave the vessel from the 3rd to the 7th but she WAS not drunk at that time. She stayed at a private house and her husband knew very well where she was staying it was quite close to the harbour. She WAS not drunk when she came abo int, the vessel. She had not I sratteeld the vessel In any way. I{,r husbend had struck her and site struck him w.th the te p She had struck him with other thing., and to had thrown things at her. At this point Mr. Pletcher subaaltte 1 a letter from the owners of the vets dated the 231 h of June which was as follows 'Captain Hawkins, I hereley give .you definite notice that your wife is not to be allowed on the vessel, as she has done wilful daittage on p OVlOrl4 occAstorm" Witness denied that she committed any damage. After further cross-examinatiou of the sari-e nature %dr. Pletc'ner addressed the Bench. He contended that ins making an order of that kind the Bench should be gulled by two essentials : First there should be wilful neglect and secondly,in addition to such n-gieet, the husband should have caused his wife to leave and live sepia. de' v from Istm.He thought they would draw their ow c mclissions from the demeanour and general conduct of the complainant whilst giving her &amp;eidetic&amp; in addition to her own adrnlselon that she we. addicted to drink. He contended th it his client had not deserted or left h.s wife. He was a seifarine* man and was even now only away upon his duty. He hut done all he possibly cow(' for his wife, sending her 103 weekly, but carat r, if he received no atsknowle 'gement of the receipt of the rnoley. he was jestiftscl in withholding it.— The case was dismissed._</p>
        <p>- A FAMILY " " AT RUNCIIIL-BTARTLING ALIA- GATIoNi.—James Garnett. 2 lell-smere-street, Runeore, was summoned on the information of his wife Mary Jane Garnett, for persistent cruelty te her thereby cuisine he: to live apart from him ort the 28th of March. 1899.__Hr. Knowles (Widnes) appeared for, complainant an I Mr. Josenh Burton (Runcorn) tor the defence. —ln opening the ease Mr. Knowles stated that the parties avers marled at Renearn Church thirty years ago last February Mr.. Garnett con- sidered her husband was always a bad tempered man. but after her daughter's death the husband had got some un- founded ideas to work upon an I grew suipicioaa of the son- in-law who by the way was aged 39, and ihoeght. that he carried on improperly with the meth er-in-law who was 43 years of age. Indeed that seemed to be at the hettem o tic difficulty between the parties. He (Mr. Knewles) hal doue all he (mild to try and get the parties reconciled one to the other. Ile started to make peace between the parties and had to put the question to the defendant. " Would he witedraw Van imputations he had made aphis:, his wife ?" tie was unabl- to to so. inste ‘d the husband had said "f know what I do know abet them." —still meaning that he knew semathing ab eat them He had brought the son Rai the grown-up girls as witnesses— painful as it must be to them—and he wo td prove to the Bench that the defendant hart been petty of persistent cruelty. The girls were aeei 18 ant 21 respectively ant slapt in the same room as defendant and his wire D !readmit he would prove, was in the habit of cursing his wife &amp;roe ie every night calling her all the nasty names he cou!cl and also threatening her They would tell the B-neh Ilea during tae last fortnight she remained in the house, she had not the least bit of comfort,and that it was utterly imroesible for the woman to live with him. The eldest son would state he had stayed at home lately for his mother's protection as he was afraid that his father would do his mother some harm. He submitted that .if he could establish the persistent use of abusive languege by the defen,lant be would be accomplish- ing sufficient for the purposes of the At.% of Parliament. On Saturday. March 25th, the defendant hail threatened to crush the life out of her. First he threw a hymn beak at her; which she three, beck. Then there was a seufll 3, in which his client got hold of a poker and struck him on the</p>
