        <p>Tag interviei; from which the following is abridged was I work continued up to the fall of the year, when symp- tirs,, published in the " D ,ily Telegraph," the other day. tma ot weakness and general debility appeared. At Mr. W. F. Regan,F.R.G.S.. is, of course, the well-known first I took little notice, and was not alarmed until I city and South African magnate. Described by the in- found a difficulty in walking straight when passing I terviewer as a hard worker, of sound judgment and through the streets. Indeed, sometimes I was forced to restless activity, the famous author of " Boer and IJit- catch hold of a railing or turn into the nearest office for !ander " was fouad in his offics at 41, Thradneedle- a few minutes. I have been forced as early as ten strett, I,7;adon, 8.0., loug after his &amp;riff; had o'clock in the morning to hail a hansom in Oheapside, - not being able to control my limbs, though there was left but a few hundred yards to travel in order to reach this office. This kind of thing continued throughout 1896, though I never missed one day from business, and laintimg fits were of frequent occurrence. I reco'lect on one oecasien—to me a notable day (April 24, 1896)—as a strange coincidence by one mail I received three let- ters, the first written by the greater; of English states- men, the late</p>
