indireetly helps to relieve a compleint, the least one can I worked on in despeiation. In order that I might do is to aclreowledge the fact, S 3 that others who are better forget my wretched state, I consulted similarly afflicted may know that within teoir reach is a medicine which, if used, may tiring relief, four doctors, all specialists and noted medical Up to 1895 I enjoyed perfect health. From men. By one I was told that if I did not give . up my work in three months I should be in my Feeruary to November of the year in question grave within six; another suggested that I I worked on an aivreee niruteen hours out of should go abroad permasiently ; whilst a third consoled the twenty-four each day. Many days, ranning into me with his opinion that nothing could be dune,and that weeks, I have worked night and day. In 1896 heavy</p>
        <p>nose, and in the preience of the children defendant had said he would throttle her. Under those eircemstinces he thought it would be impossible for the parties to again live together. Defendant hit for years been earning large wages —from about 21 to £3a week, but he had only given his wife about 26, to 30 a week, and he would submit that his client !lei by tier own 'radii itry—pr ncipelly by doing laundry work contri- buted to make toe house. Che man had spent the rest oe his money in betting and following race meetings. There Were two chi dren in respect to wham they were entitled to ask fo: ailowsece Jessie Mall 1(11) ant Edith (8), and the allow ance he would ask for was 105 a week. Or course if the mm got into a better position th an h e was at present, thee coal i again appeal to the court to increeso the order. and likewiie If the Man got into a worse position he could appeal for any abatement —Mere Jane Garnett. wife of tee defendant. stated that she lived with he hesbend at 23:Sties:aerie-street until the 28th of March last. She corroborated her solici- tor s statement, adding that she hard worked bard to mike her home one of the most respectable in Ituaeorn. Oder husband was A Lober man and a hardworking man. tdat the time lirditighter died her has wird promised th at her sea- in law should cone and live with them, and also his two chiidren. She had never had any iinproeer intercoerse with her son-in-law at any time. Her husbend carried on about Green (the son-in-taw) and oars night she remembered there was a nose in the hence ana her see, and son-in.lew both got up to 699 What It W is, and found ie to be caused by the win i fl toping the odeloth in the lobby. Her two grown up children slept in the same:ro sei as her,and they were present that Ague. She had never been into her son-in-law's bedroem and he had never been lute here. ebefendent but abused her with language, and was alw eye insinuating that he knew whet the " b ghest " .w le. He was 