      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>TUE NORTIIMIN UNION.</head>
        <p>TUE NORTIIMIN UNION.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON v. HULL KINGSTON ROVERS. Tale friendly match was played at Wilderspool on Saturday before 2,003 spectators. Kingston Rover; brought a vely weak team, only one of the regular players turning out, the rest being second team men. Warrington were also weakly represented in consequence of injuries, susamlions Mr. F. Turner actaa as re- feree, and Vie teams lined out as follows. fluL Kingston Ravers Ward, full back ; Ripton, 'Ruddiforth, Draper, and Waters, threequartera •, Coyne and Eagland, hatf backs.; Parkinson, Joianson, Fle!cher, Wilson,Gibba, Holstock, Goodie, and Wilcock (Warring- tin) forwards. ! Warrington: Boscow, full back; Campbell, Hscken- L hull, Ishirwood, and Mores, threequartera ; Bate and Wilcox, half back;; Bnardinin. Morrison, .Fairhurst, Eden, Sallow, Jolley, Swift, and Deakin, forwards. Fairhurst started for Warringtan against a very strong wind. Bate ant Wilcox being early on trio offensive l'ne latter punted up field, and Ward sent in touch. Warringtoo rushed the ball up from the punt out, and Eiackenhall ini=sed a chance of picking up when on the line. 87 dint of occasional marks a.nd punting with the wind, the Ravers reached th 3 other and, Ward making an ineffectual shat at goal from centra. Desultory play fadowed, and Warrington being penalised, Godwin kicked a goal for the Revers. Bate enlivened •the proceeimls by threading his way through the (lull backs in fine style, but he was unsupported and a fine opening went begging. The visitors became agressive and Goodin broke away bat threw the ball wildly when challenged by Moscow, and a certain try was wasted. A tough struggle on tha home line (mimed, but the Rovers' efforts were of a clumsy charalter and resulted in nothing being scored. At half time, Hull tel by a goal to nit. Gniit reratteci operatiens, and Fairhuret's kick be- ing chargad down, the game went alainst Warrington. Toe homa4ters soon rallad howaver and set about wip- ing off arrears. Bad kicking was a feititre for a time, but at last Bate, Isherwood, and H.ockenhull got in a fine pass rag run down the field and the last-named scored far out. Isherwood missed tha goal. Bate and El mkenhull made anatner promi,ing move which failed in toe hand; of Campbell, iloscow putting in some well- ' judged touch kicks and Warrington kept up tha attack, Monroe having hard luck in not scoring after a dodgy run. Wilcox threw to Bat'', and the latter passed nicely to latter moed wh ) dastpd in smartly near the posts, but could only hit the upright with his goal-kick. This was all the scoring, though Warrington had the best of matters. Waters porforme I well on the defence for the Rnvers.who retired beaten by 2 tries (6 points) to a goal (2 paints).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION CUP.</head>
        <p>NORTHERN UNION CUP.</p>
        <p>FOURTH ROUND. &amp;ILFORD v. HUDDERSFIELD.</p>
        <p>_ Played at baiford, in cold weather, before 15,000 people. In the first half Huddersfield had the beet of play. but met with a eblendid defence. Thomas scored for Salford, Phillips converting. Half-time—Salford, 1 goil 1 try ; Huddersfield nil. In the secand moiety play WAS even, but near tma finish Thorny; scored again for Iford, no goal resulting. Result—Salford, 1 goal 2 triel3; Huddersfield nil.</p>
        <p>HUNSLAT v. HULL. At Hunslet. bat)re 12 000 ripPetatnra. A. alfrithorpo</p>
        <p>soon kicked a penalty goal for Huns!et. Wright got a try, and Hannah dropped a goll. Hunslet be!ng3 points ahead at half-time. In the second half the amulet for- wards playea a great game, and Hull were defending most of the time. Gilhugs dropped a goal, and Hugnilet won a brilliant victory. Result, Hunslet, 3 goals 1 try (9 points) ; Hull, nil.</p>
      </div>
