      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>OLDHAM v. WIDNES.</head>
        <p>OLDHAM v. WIDNES.</p>
        <p>At Oldham, in sh )wary weather, before 12,000 specta 'trim Oi•lharn had most of tne play durintr the'first ha though playing against a strong wind. WdHams feared two tries and F.e,cher One, Sam Lees failing at goal. On resuming, Oldham pre d continuously, and Fletcher, Davies and William sc,red tries. Sam Ls kicking one goal. Wsult—Oldham, 1 goal 6 tries (20 points) ; Virienes, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>LEEDS PARISH CHURCH v. LEIGH.</head>
        <p>LEEDS PARISH CHURCH v. LEIGH.</p>
        <p>Having beaten Batley, the holders of the cup, Leigh werP ea!urally fawurites for this match at Leeds, and the 9,000 peoeto uresent sal them qualify for the semi- final round by 2 goals 2 tries to 1 goal 1 try. Fine weather favoured the game, which all through was fast and intereting. The visitors were very smart on the ball, and showed better combination. In the first half Molyneux kicked two goals for Leigh, and after change of ends Duriblvin and Coleman obtainei tries, while Mosley got in for the home side. The end of the game wen:: in favour of Leeds, but they could not over- come the visitori' dafence. An unfortunate accident happened, J. Coleman having his leg broken.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON_ AND DISTRICT LEAGUE,</p>
        <p>_ WARRINGTON v. MARY'S V. EARLESTOWN ROVERS.— Played on the cricket ground Arpley, on Saturday Earleetewn won the tosa and Hunter kicked off for Sc. Mary's against a strong wind. The ball was at once taken to the home team quarters, where after a bout of passing Aspinall was called upon to char. Play was transferred and Dilwnrth ran well along touch, after which he centred to Connor, who experienced hard lines, the ball travelling over the tar. Toe kick-out did not relive the pressure for Hunter getting possission made tracks for the gcal, but his effort was frustrated by the visiting backs. Play was sent to midfield where the bail was ktpt for some t!me. Oncl again did Connor take the ball down the field, bat his shot went wide. The goal kick took play to the Saints' quarters and the visitors got within shooting ranee. Aepieall cleared the first attempt; but the second was a little too quick for torn and in trying to clear he had the misfortune to send the ball into his own goal. Shortly before the interval Eariestown came away, and the Saints' custodion was applauded for saving whilst on his knees. At half-time thescore was Earlegtewn Rovers one goal ; Warrington St. Marys, nil. Resuming, the play was taken to the visitors'encl, and in the first few minutes St. Mary's were awarded a corner, but tothing came of it. During the next ten minutes the Warring- ton team had many ellanc,?s, but they were not taken. IThe game began to slacken and the play was of an unin- teresting nature, the Rovers rarely leaving their own , quarters. When they did get away the bad travelled the length of the field, and once Aspinall had to make a daring save in his own goal. Altlacugh chances were frequent for St. Mary's the shooting of their players was erratic and when the whistle went for the final the score was—Earlestown Rovers, one goal; Warrington St. Maty's</p>
        <p>WIGAN CUP.-FINAL. EARLESTOWN V. Sr. HELENS ItECREATION.</p>
        <p>During the week the topic uppermost in conversation amongst the followers at the s3cker game was the pros- pect of Earlestown in connection with the final match with St. Helens Recreation for the possession of the Wigan Cup. Whilst it was well known that the com- position of the Earlest own team would be the same as OD previous occasions, various rumours were afloat anent the Recs team, the members of which it was said were in strict training, whilst on the other hand it was said that the Recs. would only be able to turn out a reserve team. When the names were given, however, it was found that the only absentee from the regular eleven was Ellaby. A special train, filled with enthusiasts left Earlestown for Wigan, and another special conveyed 'the Recs.' supporters from St. Helens. The game was played on the Wigan County ground, at Springfield Park, on Saturday afternoon, before about 3,000 snecta- tors, who with very fe w excmtions hailed from Earlcs- town and St. Helens. There was an exceptionally strong wind blowing from end to end of the ground when the teams lined up as follows :</p>
        <p>Earlestown : Goal, Cnew ; full hacks, Heyes and Wilkinson ; half backs, Lee, Parr and Green ; forwards, Settle, Leach, Hempson. Smith, Harrison. St. Helens Rees: Gnat, Hoy ; backs. Monks, Wood- ward ; half backs, Mather, Preston, and Hunter; for- wards, Jackson, Davies, Baines, Jones, Humbell. Referee, Mr. T. Laithwaite. Wilkinson won the toren and elected to play with the wind and sun at his hack, and at 3-45, Baines set the leather in motion, and Wilkinin returned, and in con- sequence of the wind Sr. Helens had to immedi- ately act on the defensive. Several throws-In availed little and for a time Weodward and MOhl3S managed to stave off disaster. A neat kick by Wilkinson skimmed the bar,and St. Helens worked to centre: but soon again hostilitieS were at Hoy's charge. Smart work by the Etrlestown left wing gave Settle a. chance and he only just in is,4ed finding, the wir d blow- ing the ball back. Flowever,by short passing, lhe Glass. men gained ground, and Jon's getting off, threatened danger. Wilkinson stopped him, and kicking down the field, his side again assumed the aggressive. Davies dribbled down. Subsequently Eariestown forced a corner but it was unproductive, and try as they would the net could not be found. Smith was to the fere, hut the Rees , left wing worked effectively, and several times worked down to Chew's charge. Heyes and Wilkinsorehowever, proved a stumbling block, and although the eesuing ataees favoured the Earlestownites, their efforts were compLtely spoiled by the strong breeze. The ball was repeatedly over the line, but eventually some hot work in front of the St. Helens goal caused their supporters some anxiety. They certainly did well to prevent a score. Neat passing gave Parr an excellent opportunity of opening, but unfortunately the wind took the ball a couple of inches on the wrong side of the bar. The Recs. next paid a brief visit to the other end, but a rush down the field enabled Smith to get possession and send in a shot, which gave Hoy a handful. Further pressure on the St. Helens goal followed, but nothing tangible accrued. Lialf-time—Eirlesrown nil, St. Helens nil. On turning round, Earlestown had to face the wind, but they played a mu h better combined game than in the fist half. From the kick-off, they got away, and after a few exchanges, tbey made a dash for Boy's charge. Smith got possession and outstripping the backs got perilously near say, but at the suereme moment, when a goal seemed inevitable, he stumbled and Monks rushing in, removed the danger. Quickly the Rees were at the other end, but Flayes and Wi kineen cleared. Several shots were sent in from midfield, but the wind took the bell high beyond the croes bar. Chew had to save on one or two occasions, but they were not difficult shots, and in throwing one away, the wind intervened and a comer resulted. Tnis was well placed and the Earlestewn colours were in danger of beiog lowerei, but Lee nicked in and cleared. Hampsen got pessessioo and away the forward line rushed, Settle and Lech being conspicuous for the clever way they cleared all obstacles, and immediately they were clustering round Hey who had a lively time, The ball was seeti to rise and was just failing in und:r the bar when Hoy jumped up, and to save had to concede a comer, which was safely negotiated. From the goal kick the Rees got away, and for some time the game consisted of threws in and goat kiclis,but still Earlestown pressed, Ultimately Beines and Davies cot well down, and some blundering on the pin of the Earlestown backs let Davies in who shot hard along the ground at an angte and beat Chew about 10 minutes of time much to the delight of the St. Helens supporters. Eertestown tried hard to oinaliae, but were unable to do so, and the Rees were declared the winners by one goal to nil. The cup and medals were afterwards presented to the winners by the Mayor of Wigan who observed that in his opinion the best team,, on the day's play had lost.</p>
        <p>ONE SURPRISE Oi THE VICTORIAN</p>
