      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>WARRINGTON AND SECONDARY EDUCATION.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND SECONDARY EDUCATION.</p>
        <p>A SERIOUS POSITION.</p>
        <p>, ' Information has just reached us of a most im- portant character upon the above subject. Fur some time past it appears that the Association of Muai- cipal Corporations has had the subject under its most serious consideration and various attempts at a settlement have been made. In January last the sub-committee met and adopted the following scheme t—lst "That a central education authority ehould be established, with an education minister attached" ; 2nd" That a local education authority should be established in each educational area; for the purpose of dealing with secondary education including technical instruction"; 3rd "That the area of every administrative county and county borough should be the educational area. A proviso was however introduced that, although the administra- tive county—which term includes for this purpose a county borough—was to be accepted as the area for secondary education, provision should be made enabling the council o. any county or any boreugh, to, make a representation to the effect that it was desirable that an educational area other than that of the administrative county should be constituted and the claims of sum borough would be considered by the central autho- rity. This was held to mean that if any no/l- er/linty 'borough was desirous of becoming an edu- cational area it might apply to the central authority. This sop to the non-county boroughs may be valued at any price but the conduct of the County Council of Lancashire towards the non-county boroughs in the matter of Technical Instruction precludes the possibility of assuming that any assistance to a non-county borough seeking to help itself would be rendered; rather is it to be feared that every obstacle would be placed in its way. It is further suggested that the local eria3B- - authority be- appointed as follows e— a majority by the County Council or county borough— it there are school boards one third of the remainder by the boards—another third to be nominated by the education minister from the school boards and the remaining third to be co-optated by those already chosen. Where there are no school boards then the appoint- ment is to be as follows—a majority by the council of the county or borough ; one third of the re- mainder by the minister and the remainder ce- optated. This scheme, it will be at once seen, entirely shuts out all municipalities which are not county boroughs; and where there are no school boards in such nounicipalit:es, it will be further seen that such boroughs will have no voice what- ever in the selection of the education autherity. It is impossible to conceive a greater hardehip, be- cause there are many boroughs having a population larger tban some county boroughs, yet, because the latter happen to- be county boroughs, they are to , enjoy a privilege that their more populous non- county neighbours may riot have. The particulars of this echeme were torwarded to the Government and to the heads of the Education Department as the voice and desire of the municipal authorities of the kingdom and it was perceived that it must have , considerable weight in the discussion by the Government of this somewhat diffieult problem. When the scheme came on for consideration, by the Association at its annual meeting, not unnaturally, the non-county boroughs strenuously objected to what they considered as a deprivation of their natural right, and, from what we learn, it would seem that the question after being fully de- bated, and ably debated too, at a meeting of the Non-County Boroughs Aesociationot resolution was unanimously passed, that the non-county boroughs should stand out for equal treatment with the county boroughs. So strongly and acutely was this unfair treatment felt that at one time it seemed likely to be the rock upon which the very existence of the Association of Municipal Corporations might. split, and had it not been for the kindly feeling and strong desire entertained by the leaders ot the non- county boroughs a conflict would have been iuevit- able. Toe repre,entatives from Liverpool, at the meeting, where the matter came up for considera- tion, declared in the most arrogant way that, whether the notecourty boroughs liked it or not, they meant to go on with toe ecneme as:suggested. All parties agiate that secondary education is an absolute necessity of our time,and that toe carrying on of the euecational plans can only be satisfactorily worked by a local authority. What particular form teat local authority should be, cannot be settled by any off-hand resolution. It has been suggested that all le iroughs with over 20,0.20 inhabitants should be the limit, :but this would not suit siMe authorities who are now doing good work in technical instruction. It has also been suggested that the smaller boroughs should be grouped with the villages around them, and thus centres of fair sizs could be created, and -very economically worked. It is no secret that the Tech- nical Instructiota Committee of the County of Lan- cashire has been continually at loggerheads with the local authorities and that in the opinion of many local authorities its conduct end manage- ment still leaves much room for improve- ment. The publication of the proceeding's of the Liverpool City Council shows that their representatives at the annual meeting of the Asso- ciation of Municipal Corporations did but state the truth when they declared their intention of acting at once, for the whole matter came before their Council on Wednesday night. Now, let us see how Warrington will be affected by the propositions set tort-h. Werringtose as most know, is the largeet non-county borough, and although we are credibly informed that the representatives of War- rington spoke out strenuously against the proposal—it this scheme be adopted Wariingtou will be effectually shut out. Many times have our columns proved the existence of friction between the County Council and the Borough, and threats that Warrington should seek to become a County Borough have been muttered time • end time again, but now it isevident that some speedy action must take place. Warrington has no School Board (which to many is a most unfortunate circumstance), and therefore Warringtcn would have no repreeentative at all upon the local educational authority, unless it, shouid happen that the central educational au- thority acting under the proviso above referred to should be of -opinion that it is in the best interest of education that a special area should be constitute I for Warrington. The temper of the County Council, and the deposition ot the members, leaves no tope for the suggestion that they would welcome the creation of a special area for Warrington. If nothing is to be done in other ways therefore, Warriugton must help itself, and the only effectual way of helpirg itself is for Warrington to take immeutate steps to become a eetanty borough. We have made enquiry, an 1 eaves been informed that the great reason why Warrington has no; sought to step up among the greater boroughs is that of expense— indeed there seems to be a certain fearful looking for, and dread of, any increased expenditure, but it is impossible to have progression we hoot some expense. lt may. after , ell, be only a nightmare, and when the psition of Warringten hall be preeerly worked up, and the i nilebreenees of the county to the town fully made Out, it Steals to us thsre is not much reason to eel icipat e any 1 great incrsase of expenniture. War- riagton has now the reputation of being among the " torwarn movement " boroughs, and it certainly has shown no disposit'on to shirk its respoesibility in dealing with technical in arectiou. Toe ( nly complaint it has ie the complaint of bad to-atment at etc hands of the County Council. It,bas secured a plot of land for a T,cluiesl :4chool. ; it has put tot) the new Bill a burn of money tor the erection of -ucli a seer el ; it is cirryieg on succest- fully continuatioe echool-e-lueational classes, art work, and all that pet tains to teehnieal instructfoos and a grant, from toe rates has fo- sOme time been made, and tro are informed that this item 1-4 again appra ing in the estituetel for the forthcoming e ear—all of who Ii t oeclusively (roves that War- rington is not one what hi-hire-I in thi+ matter. It, therefore, Warrinetou, ie -I to maintain its position- -4 anXicali as it apparently is to do its duty--i!, nitiSi do more than talk or it will must probably tin-1 it- : t...elt n.erged in the larger authority of the county. L/NUM C4TtiaRTIOUM. PMI'S. digest ive, corm- hive, rod ngreettb!y aperioat. 9,1,1.. 130.0f %11 I C lit tr, ists.—Ativt.</p>
