      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>SABBATH D ESSCRATION,</head>
        <p>SABBATH D ESSCRATION,</p>
        <p>Sir,—Referring to the" Two big London dailies which have expressed their intention to include the Sabbath among their publishing days,' you wrote in at week's "Examiner" thus : "As a Christian people we try to 'keep the Sabbath holy.' but it will be practically im- possible for is to obey this divine behest if newspapers are to flood the cities, towns, and village.' every Lord's Day." lam at one with you in this view of Sunday newspapers, and am glad to see von making a stand against this new denarture of the dailies referred to. But after all, lkfr.Eiltor, our obedience to the Sabbath law at present is very far indeed from being what it should be, and the Publishing of two Sunday newspopers will only be an addition to the desecrations already in existence; How many thousands of railway men of all grades are compelled to handle an enormous traffic in goods and passengers on the Sabbath days et the peril of losing their situations for disobedience! And no one in the pulpit, or out of it, seems to regard this traffic in any other light than that of ordinary legitimate business. Then look at the street.; Of our towns and cities on Sundays. There we find cabs, omnibus's, and tramears runnine in all directions, as if the fourth Commandment had no ex- istence, and the poor drivers and their sorely oppressed horses required no rest at all. And perhaps the ugliest feature of the rail and caeriaze traffic on the Sabhath io the intense selfishness which promota and underline it all. Money, more money, h the incentive with the Rail way Directors, and pleasure, or what they call pleaenre. moves the crowls of men and women who love to travel on Sundays. They never 914,m to give a thouzlit to the fact that their pleasure is purchased nt the cost of toil and sufferince to multitudes of their fellow creatures and dumb animals.</p>
        <p>But even worse than all this is the opining of Pnblic Sous3B, on the Sibhith day. Bikers, butcher% groeers, tailors, droners, and othe.s who trod, in the necessaries of life find six davci a werk snffieient for their re"p-.seA, and when Satu-dav nivht comes their pl.ces of bu'inesi are shut until Monday morning. But the case is differ- entwilh the sellers of s'rina drink. Not cseent with at least sixteen hours a day for six day; of the week the publieias must wele aperooritte Feven or eight hours of the S hbath for their purpose s. When we take into acconnt the;r late hours on Saturdays and the cleaning of their places on Sandays it may be 'rely said that the alibi-a-5 kno"P no rest day at al!. Surely their love for" the trade." or for the money they de'ive from it "nest he very stron7 when they set aside the boon of rest which God commando and prefer the opera- tions of the her prim], and the tanronm. In cnnclusion. Mr. Editor, it s'ems to me that the general trend of the time we live in is to ignoee Gol altovether. This may seem a startline statement when we consider the vast army of ecelesiaetics we have. aerl the millions of money th*t are every year expended upon whst. is called "The preaching of the ecsnel." But I think my view is not far frsm the truth, and others might possibly be inclined tn enenrie it if the will only look et the religious .n 1 irreligisus worlds as they really are at present. Your truly, ION!.</p>
        <p>TOO MANY YOUNG WIDOWS,</p>
        <p>,•- About the middle of thn seventeenth century it was observed that young w'dows xver, unusually plentiful in Rome. It was also remarked that marriages in high life, known to be unhappy, were speedily dissolved by the illness ard death of the husband.</p>
        <p>_ Their curiosity being; roused by this fact, the clerzy proceeded to investizat. Presently thy, told the Pope what lion they were workinte mi, w:tti th. Fensati --nal deve'onm.nta likely to result from it. " G cm." said His Holiness ; " Chorth de4res to know why hus- bands die so fist wi•Pl wives do not."</p>
        <p>Then inquiries 1,1 to the discovery of a secret sociefy of young mat-ron. who met at the house of a re- puted sorceres for instruction in Vie. art of poisoning. A little latr a similar organisation WEIR found in Paris. Thus was the plethora oF young widows accounted for. And it was only after a hundred rr so of the culprits had suffered at the stake or on the aallowl that the epi- demic was cluckel. For some time afterwards, how- vor, young men showed a marked hesitation to marry.</p>
        <p>Now let me quote you what a famous chemist says:— The human holy," he deciares, " eveo in its normal state, is a cupboard of poisons. So low 2 as they remain shut up io their own cover or vessel they are harmless, but when let loose they damage and kill." Yes, and it is an uneornforlable fact to think of. The hunzan body is packcd with honze-nzade poisons , to say nothing of those that gt into it from the out,zide. Most of the dis,ases that figura prominently in the bills of mortaltr are caused be poisons manufacturfd in the body itself, anal not got rid of in time: The stomauh, the liver, the kidnoys,all help make them, or we in some way responsible for them.</p>
        <p>It may seem aa odd thing to say,but di;ease is suicide, without knowledge or intent,but a kind of self-slaughter just the samo, S3metimel rapid and somotimes &amp;ow ; it depends.</p>
        <p>Here i 3 a, man who says h 3 sufferei from kidney com- plaint from his you"h—about forty years. He had a deal of pain in the ilwer part of his back, and often could not bend his body f rrward at al4. The other symptoms of kidn9y trouble—he had them also.of coursl, in a dull, weary, tiring way. He had asthma, too; short, labourod trratbing, preventing him frees lying squarely down on his bed, till only perfet position to rest in. They propped him up with pillow; and he drew his breath as beit he could.</p>
        <p>His kidney secretion was ordinarily scanty, and of a red colour. In time he grew 5) w:Alr he could hardly walk. When ho did get about, h;s most helpful com- pany was a stout stick. His appetite lwas poor, and he had a deal of Pain at the chest ani *nisch. _ -</p>
        <p>fixtors he had, !Inv of them ; and to-day he would be glad to get back the mone7 be spent (so blindly) for drugs that did him no good.</p>
        <p>So matters went on with this man from yesr to year. He wa.4 never well ; alwaya ;weak; practically nseless ; most of the time in paiu—nbe kind of man who Plato Faii is:oi no advantage to the world at all, and ought to</p>
        <p>4, In the spring of 1893," so cencludes a letter rotting forth the foregoing Lteus, " I read:of a case like mine havin% been cured by Mother Seigel's Syrup, and I tried it. The first bottle did me good, and fa a few weeks I was vastly better. The pain in my back was easier, and I could eat heartily, with 1:1J distress to follow. GriduallY I got up my strength, so that Iltid aside my stick, and could work. This for a mart of my age, tvlio had been ill so long, I regard '4F. inwvellous. Yet Moti.er Syrnp did it. I am:perfectly willing to have my case pub:ished." (Signed) Moses Tovsy, Cefia-rJal, Den, via Cardiff, august 31. 1898.</p>
        <p>All kidney crs.,ase, whether rapid or slow in its action, is causd by a pAson in the bloo i, arising from bad digestion in the fir,t instance, and rendered more per- aistent and cunp:rx by a Orpi i liver, male, s'milition of food by the IP Kly cells, an imp,rfect action of the bowels and skin ; and in.leed of th kidneys thereielves. The preventive is good digestion (Mr. Tovry was a dyspeptic), and the throwing of waste stuffs °verb, ard as last as they form. Ti s state of thiags is promoted and insured by Mother Seigcl'a Syrup. By its power t) pulify, sweep out, and sweeten the system, it cured this chronic case. Trust it fit your own complaints, or the ills of your friends.</p>
        <p>Tbnss young Roman matrons rerno7ed their husbands with arsenic, as they would ra!s. They were surely wicked women—'l4n2,Lrous ladies to have for wives.</p>
        <p>But, as I hive said ,our own boy forms coisoni even worse, and it is wise to keep it well cleaned with Mother .Seigers Syrup. •</p>
