      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>EtIRLESTOWN &amp; NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EtIRLESTOWN &amp; NEWTON.</p>
        <p>ACCIDENT TO A LAD AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>On Monday morning a lad named Richard Jone 3, of Chandos-street, Earlestown, was injured whilst following his emplqyment as a turner at Messrs. T. and T. Vicars' Works. It seems that after the machinery had been set going the lads: are in the habit of catching hold of the belting as it goes round and this lad was at the same trick when his hand was carried round with the belting and his fingers getting into the machinery were badly crashed. He immediately fainted,and was conveyed on the ambulance to Dr.-Mouncey who attended to his injuries. BAPTIST CHURCH. EARLESTOWN. • On Monday evening a tea and concert in aid of the Church Funds was held in the Baptist school- room. About 50 sat down to an excellent tea pro- vided by the ladies of the congregation. After tea the room was cleared, and an excellent programme was gone through. Songs were rendered by Miss Daisy Mather and Miss Corlett and bleisrs. W. Limond and G. W. livdd. Mr. Robinson gave two recitations entitled " Betsy and I are out and how Betsy and I got in.'and"Rubinstein's piano playing." Mr. Isherwood gave selections on the Musical Glasses and Fairy Bells, and was encored. A violin solo was also given by Mr. J. El. Paplow. The accompanist was Mrs. Latimer, who performed her task efficiently. EXCITING ESCAPADE OF TWO EARLESTOWN CHILDREN.</p>
        <p>There was much excitement in Houghton-street Earlestown on Slonday afternoon over the escapade of two lads both only three and a half years old, who as the result shows narrowly escaped drowning. The youngsters are named William Grandy, and Jackie Errycott, both of whn3e parents reside in Houghton-street. The children went out to play about 3 o'clock and at a quarter past four a girl named Sarah Ord brought young Grundy home dripping wet. As soon as he got into the house he said Mamma, Jackie's in the water." Knowing that Jackie Errycott had gone out with him, this statement caused a commotion, and immediate en- quiries were made,and Errycott' s father later on was met carrying his child along the Mesties from the dir,ction of Newton. It aopi3ars the children had rambled as far as Newton Hall farm, and having a toy boat and seeing a pond,they decided to sail toe boat on the water. This they proceeded to do, but the pond being covered with a green growth the children must have thought it was grass and step- ping on they were sooa overhead. Grundy screamed and his cries attracted a servant girl at the farm who immediately ran and palled him out. On setting him on his feet. he immediately male off home, but cried out that " Jackie',4 ia tne water." Tnis alarmed the servant girl, and looking in the pond she discovered the face of " Jackie," but could not reach him. She shouted one of the farm hands who walked into the water and pulled the little fellow out. He was immediatalv taken into the farm and wrapped in blankets and a stimulant was given. On coming round the lad said his father worked at the Print works, and information was sent across, and the father came and took the lad home. The children had a very narrow euape, but they do not now seem any worse.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>AN EXTRAORDINARY AFFRA.Y.</p>
        <p>At the St. Helens Police Court on Monday, a series of cases of breaking the peace and assault were brought by Constables Littler and Adams.of Peasley Cross. A man named Mynock, of Appleton-s;reet, was charged with being drunk, and his son with breaking the peace. Con- stable Littler said that on Monday last he found My- nick in the middle of a crowd of about 1,000 persons. There was a great uproar. Mynock was knocked down by a man named Robby, and was dazad for about a quarter of an hour. The crowd was very hostile and would not bring him water when he was trying to bring him round. They said he had been to the priest's house, and thvatened him. Mynock, in defence, said he went to see the priest at Peasley Cross, and had some unplea- santneas. Be alleged that the priest accused him of not being marri'd to his wife, who was dead. He told him the name of the priest who married him. Mynock alleged that the priest struck his son over the head with a stick. He took the stick from the priest, and after- wards when ha went to return the stick spoke to the father about what had happened, and the crowd mobbed him, and ha was very badly treated. Several witnesses on the other side said that Mynock had a knife in his hand, and was going to stab the priest.—The bench dis- missed the case against Mynock and his eon. and bould their assailants, Robby and Kay, over to keep the peace.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>VESTRY MEETINGS.</head>
        <p>VESTRY MEETINGS.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN PARISH CHURCH.</p>
        <p>The Easter vestry in connection witn the narish of Runcorn was held in the National Schools on Tnurs- day evening last week. The Rev. Canon Maitland .Wood presided.—The Chairman laid before the vestry the accounts of the various charities.—The Vicar moved the following resolution :—" That this vrstry approves of the application for a faculty to enanle the vicar and wardens to open two new lancet windows in the chan- cel, for the purpose of giving light tiaere ; and to permit the substitution of marble and alabaster communions rails for the present ones at the expense of Miss E lith Barclay."—The resolution was carried unanimously.- -The Vicar nominated Mr. J E Meredith as his warden. Mr fmison proposed the re-election of Mr. John Littler as people's warden. The motion was carried unanimously.—Mr. Meredith nominated the falLwing sidesmen : Messrs. W Timming,J K Dig4es,John Yates, and J S Davies; and the meeting elected (by ballot) Messrs W T Crodoe, J H Hamilton. George Hewitt, and J Wilding.—The Vicar nominated Mr J Weston as his warden at St. Michael's Church. Mr A L'ghtburn was elected people's warden. Mr Weston nominated Messrs. J Sherlock, W Miles,and T Bowyer as sidemen, arni the vestry elected (by ballot) Messrs. W Cropper, T Clark, and C Harrison.—For Weston Church the following officers wers elected : Vicar's warden, Mr Frank Robin- son ; people's warden, Mr J Fl B selres ; warden's sides- men : Messrs. A G Guest, H S Bamber, G H Garner, and D Johns; elected by the vestry: Mears. J Carlson, T Ford, junior. R Padbury, ani J Farrell. This concluded the business of the vestry.</p>
        <p>Atthe congregational meating which immediately fo-'- lowed that of the vestry, the financial st itement of the Parish Church was presented and showed rec)ipts amouetin, to £39t 143 311 The alcounts of St. Micitacl's Church stiotvad an income of £l5l 2 I.The expenses (inctuding £9O as the chuicn's prapordon to clergy fund) amounted to £l7B 4% 7d., leaving a defizit of £27 23. fqi. At St. Joon tho E e4n Weston, the receipts for the year amoonted to £l3O 18,3. 411. There was a balance on the year's working ot 34. whlch was paid to the Clergy Fund.—Votes of thank; concluded the mooting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>NEWTON.LE-WILLOWS V. KI RKDALE.—Aft,n a bre; absence from Nursery Park,the Newton team turned ou.; on Saturday to play the return match under the auspices of the Leverpool and District Co:nbinati on. The New- tunians t.rned ont as advertisei, bit Kirkdale were minus roar of their b at men. reliab:e substitutes being, however, found to fill their places. A string wind was blowing from goal to goat and Houghton having won the toss, the homPsters had the advan'age of this in the first half. The npening stagea were all in favour of Nowto and they quickly made the Kirkdale defenc-, bustle about. Some tall kicking on the part of the visiting forwards transferred play and Craig getting possession. he dasheri off with the tall, tricking Morrissey and Houghton, but as he was ab3ut shoot. Asht•sn popped in and cleare 1 in toe nick of time. The Kirkdale playtrs see nod to livi n tin now and Craig * again got going. He passed the ball out to the riiht wing who passel back agaia to the centre-forward and this player shot well Ira, Horrocks just managing to scraps the b 11 out as P; was gointe through. Following Lois the homesters we it off in r tre style and Frankland receiving the ball he passed t Ells who in turn passed on to Butler and Vl's player made ne mistake and scored the first goal for Nowton after 20 minutes play. Horsed be this reverse the ,isitors forwards became dangerous, and the inside left was the mrans of obtaining a very lucky pqnalising goal. No more scoring was done up to the inrerval when tte score sto, d at one gra eacn.--rhe visitors were the first to 6110 W up on resuming, but they were , not allowed ti come wittd 1 sho tine ranee. T:.e New- toni4ns played tetter against toe wind than they did with it, and they were seen hovering round the K'rkdale goal, the outcome of a prolonged attack 1,, ing that Glynn scored tho second and winning point. Final : Newton, 2 ; Kirkdale, 1.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY, APRIL 14, 1899. WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, APRIL 14, 1899. WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>FINAL MEETING.</p>
        <p>The Board meeting on WAnevlay was the concluling meeting for the year 1898-9, and the completion of the Board's business was considered. Mr G Pilling presided, and thsre were ales present Revs J H Riddette, T R Matthews, Mrs Clay and Mrs Ed Asten, Mesas 8 Davies. A .1 Jolley, T B Malkin, E L Bent, J Billinge, P Cunliffe, T 11 Ashcroft, H Hibbert, J Clarke, H Thornton, R For- shaw, William Hmighton, A Jolley, A Brooks, J Ball, J Johnson, with Mr J C Suttln (clerk), Mr Ward (work- how() master), Mr Wright (superintendent Padg,ate Schools), and Mr Owen (architect). MR. A. J. JOLLEY SPEAKS IN PRAISE OF THE</p>
        <p>CLASSIFICA.TION SCHEME. The House Commlttee recommended that 120 tons of sleepers be purchased from the Warrington Slate Com• panv, Limited, at 17s. 91. per ton, as per tender. Mr. Mezley,insrentor of Poor Law Schools,visited the Work- house on the 11th of April, and had made the following report in the visitors' book: "I have inspected the school to-day. The children are well taught and make Road progress ;or the time they have been in the school. The schedule will give details, J. R. Mozloy. 'Extra subject?, reigious knowleig I, good, the children answered intell4,eently ; singing, very fair ; Kindergar- ten work, god; re.-Atation, good ; drill, good. The babies could tell games of walnuts oi alphab !I: card." The scholars at the Workhouse are instructed by Miss Ward.</p>
        <p>Me. A. J. joteme, in moving the adoption of the minutes; called the attention of members to the grati- fying circumstance that there was a steady decrease in the number of inmates whieh had followed on the in- troduction of improved classification. Hs did not for his colleagues claim that the improvement was entirely due to the classification, but he veaturei to say no one who knew anything of the working of the house would doubt that it had been a very important factor in pro- ducing that desirable reset:. Last week the number as compared with the corresponding week lest year showed a decrease of 65. The decrease in previous weeks were,29, 40, 24, 33 and so on, all showing a steady decrease since the improved classification was introduced. The introduc- tion of the scheme had been juitified by the result. The condition of the sick during the ye Sr now ending had been materially improved. Pauper nursing had been entirely done away with, and the cendition of the aged poor as well of the sick poor had been materially improved. All that had been done practically without any addition to the cost to the general body of the ratepayers. (Hear, hear.) If such a decrease as that could be m iintained there would be an absolute decrease in the cod. In ad- dition a considerable number of able-bodied people had been compelled to go out to elm their own living, and that was not only a finaecial gain to the ratepayers but a moral gain to society at large. He might claim for his colleagues on the House Committee that they had given a model lesson in administration, and emphasized the very imp ertant lesson which some members seemed unwilling to learn—that in the long run strictness of ad- ministration was both the kindest and cheapest policy. S) far as he could judge, tnerm had been no unkindness or unnecesGary hardships inflicted on any inmate. In vie'', of the very large expense encurred on the infi rnery the large expenditure which would have to be incurred, and the additional administra".ive expenditure which would have to be faced when the building was opmed, there was imperative need for the stric:est economy all round. The members of the House Committee might congratelate themselves on having given a model lesson in economy of working. Mr. ALFaen JOLLEY seconded, and the minutes were approve. APPOINTMENT OF A NEW CHIPLAIN.</p>
        <p>The CLERIC read a lett?.r from the Rev. E. R. Tar. buck, the new vicir of St. PAul'e, applyine: for the Position of chaplain to the workhous,, vacated by the Rev. H. V. Piot._</p>
        <p>TheCHAIRMAN ASICArI whe!her that WaS the! only applica- tion. Was thsre no rospinse to the Rev. J.R. Riddette's apnea!? (Laughter.) The CLERK : That is the only application. The CHAIRMAN Mrird the opp3incinent of Mr. Tar- buck, and referred to his suitability for the pssition. Mr. A. J. JOLLEY seconded, and the resolution was unanimously carried._ _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>INCREASE OF LUNACY.</head>
        <p>INCREASE OF LUNACY.</p>
        <p>Mr. SHEA.RER intimated to the Board that there had been no fewer thae five lunatics breught to the Work- house since Saturday morning. They had applied t 9 Rainhill Asylum for their ad nission, but the authorities were only able to renive one female inmate: She hal been taken, and it was quite p)ssible that the others vrmld have to be taken to the private asylum at Hay- dock. He would suggest that arrangements be made with D. Street, the superintendent, as to terms. The CLERK was instructed to °Take the best terms possible.</p>
        <p>THE NEW INFIRMARY.—FURTEIER PROPOSALS. A printed repert of minutes of meetings of the sub- committee and Infirmary Committee was submitted. At a meeting of the suh-oemmittee on the 15th utt it was decided not to proeeed with the obtainine of estimates for the economiser or boi era or to proceed .with the drawings for the bailer shed and engine house and chim- ney, inasmuch as the size of the boilers to be used would depend upon the putting down a plant for the electric lighting or the obtaining the electric light from the Cor- poration, in which latter case, the boilers would only have to be sufficiently powerful to deal with the hot water heating and hot water service and the laundry machinery. With respect to water for the noilers, the architects laid be ore the nob-committee an estimate from the Corporation of £95 for a new 3in main from Priestlev-street to supply water from Appleton, and it was decided to recommend the Guardians not to build a rain water tank for the storage of the rain water from the Infirmary buildings. At a further meeting on the 29th ult. the following estimates were op med:—Heating and hot water servic., qeward and Company. Lancaster. £2,130 ; Doreue, Griffiths and Company, Liverpool. £2llB ss; Killick arid Cochran, Liverperal, £1,694 ; Wilson and Company, Manchester, towel rails £l7B if required, £1,553 ; King, Limited, Liverpool, £1,408 10a. Toe architect; were requestsd to report to the sub-committee as to whether the contractors, sending in the three lowest eetimates, had complied with the requirements which the architects had issued, and to generally report en them. The tenders for electric lighting were as follea ti : —Drake and Gorham,London,22,9ss 16;; Edmundien's Limited. £2929 10s; Warrington Electric Lightine Corn- pant', £2,436 5;; Calvert and Company, Manchester, £2,419. The architects were requested to report to the sub-committee on tee eetim ates of Messrs Culvert and Company, and the Warrington Electric Lighting Com- pany. The estimate received from the Warrington Cor- poration for certain portions of the work, w 1.3 opened when it was found that for the wiring only, the esti- mate; stood as under :—Warrington Electric Company, £729 5s ; Iffarringten Corporation, £724 6;; Calvert and Company. Mancne3ter, £712 ; and the Corp° -atio offered to supply electric lignt at a charge not ex teediag 51 per unit. The tenders for hydrants and valves for tire extinguishing inside the Infirmary were as follows : Rose and Company. Winchester, £97; Messrs Merry- weather, London, £6B 183 81. Tne architects (lleerirs. William and Seger 0 rreo) re- ported as follows :—" As directed by you on the 29;h, we have carefully examined arei gone through the de- tails of the three estimates for heating and hot 'vat service referred to us, and have to report that we con- sider the estimate of Messrs. Killick and Cochrane, of Liverpool, £1,694, gives the most value for the money, and fulfils all the requirements of our soecificationt. They recommended its acceptance. With reeard to the pipes and fire hydrants for inside the building; the estimate of Messrs. Merry weather, £B3 18; 61, coeers all the requirements of the particulars we gave t' them and might he aecepted. Merryweather's velem- mend the addition of three sluice valve; to shut off the rising mains at the bottom of each, and we think they would be an advantage, the extra c.e,t would be £7 4.4. s'ith reeled to the estimate of the electric lighting we can say,the two we are asked to report upon have given the separate costs under ( ach of the nine heads we gave to them, so that teey include for tee lamps outside. We sheeld recommend that an e'eetrical engine be fited upon, and that the full details when obtained of the two lowest estimates be p it bee:ore him for a full repere.and that he be instruceee to give an estimate of the probable annual costs of working, and maintenance, and also the probable cost per unit of the output. 'raking the lowest estimate, the probable total cost for the electrical plant, cables and wiring with a proportion of the costs of boilers, economizer, she I, chimney and fixes will be £3,003. After going into all tee (details of the Contractor's estimates, the Engineer C)uld take tais total sum on which to cal- culate interest, maintenance and working expenses. Whsn this is done the committee can j odee whether it is wiser to put down the electric al plant for the exclu- sive use of the Guar IiAtIS or whether it is advisable to entertain the offer of the Corporation of Warrington to °tipple- it." The Anti-committee re :commended that the 'report of William and Segar Owen te accepted, and that subject to any necessary aperoval by Local Government Board the work he proceeded with. The commission to Messrs. Owen to be 5 per cent upon the outlay, such commission to include all imr3enal expenses and the fres to a competent electrical eneineee, such engineer to be selected by Messrs. W. and 9. Owen. It is ale° recommended that the tsnder of Messrs. Killick and ' Cochran amounting t £1,694 for heating and hot water service be accepted, subject however to aeproval of the ;</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <p>THE MOST NUTRITIOUS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
