      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>plans by the Lonal Government B3ard, and also that the NEW BOOKS MAGAZINES estima:es of Messrs. Merryweather for fire hydrants io- _ s de tie buileiing,ammating to £6B 18s 81 and £7 43 be ETC. accepted subject to the same approval.</head>
        <p>plans by the Lonal Government B3ard, and also that the NEW BOOKS MAGAZINES estima:es of Messrs. Merryweather for fire hydrants io- _ s de tie buileiing,ammating to £6B 18s 81 and £7 43 be ETC. accepted subject to the same approval.</p>
        <p>Mr. WILLIAM BOUGHTON in oied the confirmation of the minuteg,and said the work of building was going on as satisfactorily as could be expected. Mr. BILLINGE secondel. The Rev. J. H. Roe:earn said he was glad the com- mittee were making haste more leinorely with respeot to electric lighting. With regard to the appointment of an electrical engineer by Messrs. Owen he would like to know upon whom the responsibility would rest in the event of any mistake respecting the electric lig it in- stallation ? The CHAIRMAN said the Board would hold the archi- tects responsible if anything went wrong. The names of two eruinent electrical engineers one of Landon an 1 the other of Liverpool were mentioned to the committee, and it was left to the architects b decide which of the two it would be most convenient to enrage. Mr. OWEN proceeded to state the position of affairs in reference to the Infirmary. He oointed out that on the 10th of Novlmber. 1897. an estimate was put before the whole Boarl of £32,016 133. for the new infirmary. This was approved and sent to the Local Goveroment Board. On the same date the builders contract for the bailding of the infirmary and the boundary wall was passed amounting to £23,263 13s. The Board had also committed iPself to the architects CllTllliS3iol amount- ing to £1,163 making a total of £24,429 131 Toe I, )cil Government Board sanctioned a loan oi.! £25,003, and on the 25th December the Guardian; decided to obtain the loan in two sums. Mr. TEIORNTON : What is the rate of interest? Mr. OWN: Two and threeguitters per cent. reeay- able in 30 years. Continuing his remarks M:. Owen sail that by the reeolations that morning the Board would commit itself to a further expenditure of £1,865 23. 81. The ele :trio lighting would come before the Board in due course.</p>
        <p>Thi CLERK stated that as far as he could se s from the expenditure of the Board, the sixpenny rate which had be,sn leved, w3uld lawn them with a reasonable bilanc3 which woali enable them to furniih the new infirmary withoul borrowing.</p>
        <p>VIE PROPOSED UNION OFFICES. The Re?. J. H. RIDDETTE sad h 3 would like to ask whether anything wis t.eing done with reference to th proposed erection of offices for the Union. Re hoped the Botra would not b 3 comm ttal to new public offiles until they knew exactly where they were wt regard to the Infirmary. His opinion was that the B )ar I should not cool nit itself to anything of the kin I until they had thoroughly discussel the matter. Ile kanw that the rur ft district councillors were desirous of hay- ing new odices. and that the Clerk made reference to it in his last report. The CHMII3I trr said at the colnmencsmant of the new year it via proposed to foran a committee to go into the matter.</p>
        <p>The CLERT, said that w'oatev3r decision was come to it was not likely that he would occupy new offices. The Rev. J. H. RIDDETTE : I hope we shall have your assista,nce for many years to corns. The CE:TRIC said he would remind Mr. Riddette that at the preient momeot they were paying very heavy rents for the offices tle.y now held, and he supoo39l the Board could build new ones and the repaymln: of the Dan would b 3 something 11k3 the amount they were al- ready plyine, as rent. That would be a matter for the Board tn consider.</p>
        <p>Mr. BALL : The oFfieze would be our own in thirty pelts tin..</p>
        <p>fhe CLmax ; Yo havo been paying rent for the I tst 30 years and have nothing to show for it. Mr. BILLING?, called attention to the fact that Mr. boner Fast on the °elusion of his last visit asked nem to consider what shou'd be done with the old hospital. The work of erecting the new one was going on satis- factorily, but unforhmatelv the plasterers strike had hampered the builder somewhat. The mlnntes of the committee were then agreed to. THE R ATE ABLE VALUE OF TFIE UNION.— UNPRECEDEN INCREASE. The CH&amp;IRMAN said he had some pleasing information to gi7e.wi:h reference to the Assessment Committee. At the commencement of the year the net rateable value of the Union was £356.884. It was now £373,233,0r an increase of £22,406. The increase was without preced- ent, and he thought the committee of whi.lit he had the pleasure of being chairm In tor so many years, had a very good account to give of themselves. He hoped that by lood tra le during the next year, Us's/ would be able to keep the ra,e down to 6 i in the 2.</p>
        <p>VOTES Or THANKS. On no motion of Mr. A. J. JOLLEY. s3londei by Mr. WILLIVI BOUGHTON. and supoortei by Messrs. MALKIN, BILLINGE. and CE, a vote of Vranks was paszei to tho Gr)irmAn.</p>
        <p>Mr. PILLING in retarning thanks sairthat if what he had chop irEit with their approval he had been amply repaid. He thanked Mr. Sutton and the other officials for the great .ssiatance they had given to him. Ele had always been received by them with the greatest cour- tesy, and hal obtained with facility all information he required. A. vote of thanks was accorded to the Vice-Chair,inan (Mr. A. J. Jolley) on the motion of the Rev. T. R. Marrixsws, seconded by Mr. HOUGHTON and supplrted by Mr. ASHCROFT.</p>
        <p>bleCsertaLLN AN Co.—Admirers of Tennyson— and their name is legion—will welcome warmly the latest work bearing his name. And their gratifica- tion will be the greater when they apprehend the fact that the new volume of "Tennyson "is a me- moir of the late Laureate written by his son, the present Lord Tennysan. Who is there better fitted to undertake such a task as this than the son who has lived his life with he whom the book, to some slight extent, immortalises? None, we should answer unhesitatingly. And Lord Tennyson proves himself peculiarly adapted to the labour of love which he has undertaken and just completed. The volume is a large one, and contains practically everything worth knowing about Alfred, Lora Tennyson, front the days of his boyhood to the closing stages of his life. It is dedicated to Her Majesty, and the first item on its pages is &amp;short poem, " To the Queen," an unpublished version written in 1831. There are letters and reminis- cences, reflections by the author on notable inci- dents in the life of Tennyson' recollections by the late Earl of Salborne, the late Master of Balliol, F. T. Palgrave, the Duke of Argyll, and J. A. Froude. For the convenience of the reader there is included a chronology of works, appendix, and index. A fine photogravure reproduction of Tennyson's por- trait forms a fitting frontispiece to a book which cannot but COllle as a treasure te the lovers of the peet,who are grateful for even the smallest addition to their knowledge of his coaracter. The price of the book is 103.—Aessrs. Macmillan are also pub- lishing a sixpenny series of selections from Lord Tennyson's works, which will bring them within the reach of the most impecunious of his ad- mirer&amp; Tee numbers are very legibly printed and should command a ready stale.—A two shilling library has just been commenced by the same publishers, the programme for which comprises a number of books which have earned popularity—and since maintained it—long ago. Vile library is par excellence a giri's library, and the members of tee fair sex will assuredly take more than a passing interest in its completion and in- stallation on their own shelves. Rhoda Broughton's "Cometh up as a Flower "is the pionser volume of the series, and though a pathetic little story its merits are such as to recammsnd it to all. It is the sort of novel that impressionable young ladies are apt to weep over, but at the same time enjoy and appreciate with equal intensity of feeling. Tee books of this library are strongly and tastefully !sound in cloth . A noteworthy addition to the Golden Treasury Series is the Rubaiyat of Omar Khayyam, the astronomer- poet of Persia. Omar Khayyam was born at Naishapar in Ktioras,in in tee latter halt of otir eleventh century, and died within tne first quarter of our tWeltrth century, so that it is not, perhaps, unnecessary that his peetic works should from time to time be orought before the public. Even yet the thoughts ot this Persian of many nuedrei years ago make interesting matter for study, and we shall be surprised if contributors to the Golden Treasury Series are no:-,in the main,gratified by the Peruse' of the work—Of the many issues of Shakes- peare's works, none will surpass, for general use, the edition included in the Eversley Series. It is edited, with introductions and notes, by C. H. Her- ford, Litt, D., and will be complete in ten 5s volumes. Nos. 1 and 2, with which we have been favoured cover" Love's Labour's Lost," "Comedy of Errors," "Two Gentlemen of Verona,"" Midsummer Nighs's Dream," "The Taming of the Shrew," "Toe Merchant of Venice," ‘" The Merry Wives of Windsor," "Twelfth Night," and "As you like it." In his introduction the Editor of this fie, comprehensive edition, says its scope and character have been largely determined by tee general ieten- tion of toe Evershey series. "It is desiglied,in other words, rather for the cultivated but not learned reader, than for the professed Shakesperiau or the examiuee, though neither of tuese, it is hoped, will turn to it altogether in vain Toe bulk of the notes are intended to provide, in the briefest possible form, such information as may serve to smooth the reader's path without insulting his intelligence. The introduction oiler brief surveys of the literary d ita of the several plays and poems with some indication of the bearing of each upon the external problem of Shakespeare's mind and art.' Here we have in a nutshell the nature and purpose of this edition of the immortal bard's works. Nothing could be plainer and more con- cise, and nothing could be more certain than that Mr. Herford has succeeded in his aim. A more suitable "Stiakespeare "for he generality of readers could not have been prepared. CASSELL AND Co.—Publications from the office of this firm need little recommendation. They emerge from the press legibly stamped with the hall-mark of excellence. Tne naejsrity of them, if they are periodicals, pursue the even tenour of their way, always pleasing and always adding to their popu- larity. -" Cassell's Magazine " is unquestionably a type of this species of literature. For over 20 years it nee catered for the fiction-loving public, and is probably a greater favourite now than ever before. Max Petribeiton's serial" The Garden of Swords,"eucl "Stories of Monte Carlo," by William Is Queux are prominent features in the pages of the shagazine at present. This month's num ear also contains an enter- taining article. on" The Sorrows of a Cyclizeby John Foster Fraser, the intrepid wheeltnen who has just completed a tour round tad worki ; " Hew Phew Mcteinnis held the Pier," by Robert Barr; "Cheque! Mate !" by Headon Hill; and many other equally interesting stories and special articles. Melly readers of " Cassell's" will also have their interest aroused by tee award of prizes to tee solvers of the "Secret of Emu Plain," a weird and tragic story Iron the peas of L. T. sqeade and Hebert Eustece. —" Peril and Patriotism" is slowly bet surely in- gratiating itseif with the numerous lovers or true tales of heroic deeds and startling adventure. Part 6isno vv reached and completes the first volume, toe seconi volume commencing on April 27th wita part 7. The current number contains among =lay accounts of exciting oeeurrences, the adventures of Fred Burnaby, under toe title of "Warrior and Wanderer," Tee frontispiece depicts this gallant °dicer's last fight with tile fierce Dervishes of tu e Soudau.—The April issue of" rhe Quiver ' is styled the Easter number, and during the holiday week- end it must have provided pleasure in many a home. The Rev. A. R. Buckland, M.A. contributes an able and timely article on "Clete Centenary of the Church Missionary Society," weile an Eastertide address is written by the Dean of Norwich. Margaret Westrue is the autuor of " Toe Master- tut Young Man" a short story whica is full of interest frcca beginning to end. Next month a new serial story entitled " Celina's island," Sy Ethel F. He idle' will commence in the "Quiver."—There is a great demand for good fiction at a cheap rate, and Messrs. Cassell and Co. are en- deavouring to meet that demand by the publica- tiou of such novels as "Kidnapped," "Catrioue" and "Treasure lela,ud,"oy Robert Louis 6tevoasou,"Kiug S Aornou's Mides," by Rider Haggard, and " Used Man's Rock,' by Q. Treece is a great difference be- tween paying eixpeuce and six shillings tor suce masterpieces of fic;ion as these, but there is en equal amount of enjoyment 1,0 be got out of the cheap elition as out of the expensive one, and tile public will assuredly not fail to recognise so im- portant a fact.—" The New Penny Magazine." which has already " caugnt on "is being puonsfied also in sixpenny parts. These big bargains contain SOine 3eo pages cud 260 illustrations. Watt more coull be desired for the money ? W. J. Eefas.—We suppose there are as many people as ever who call be faseiaated by the mysteries and murders of the sensational novel. At any rats the sensational novel continues to be written and published for the delectation oi .somt- bedy. "Thu Garden Court Murder," by 13urtord lltlenuoy is an excellent sample of this class,aud as a detective story must rank nigh. Mr. Delaunoy ! knows how to unravel a tangled skein or evidence as well as anyone, and readers at his story oug•it to be enthralled by tee rapid developments of the plot an I its eurprising sequel. 'There is nothing of an ultru-sonsatio ial c erecter about "The Garden Court Murder" It is a cleverly conceived detective story written in a style someweeu above the average. Tee price is sixpence.</p>
        <p>GEollari NEWNIti AND Co.—" The great3st most popular no)k of travel ever published,' other- wise " Taroup th 3 Dark Continent " by EL it. Staul.oy is being placed b3fore tho public at Glie total cost oc 9. oniy. Messrs. Newcies are issuing it iu sixpeutiy weekly numbers and as it is</p>
        <p>well illustrated and printed in large type, on good paper, it should meet with a large sale. Mr. Stanley has written an entirely new introduction to this issue of is famous work.—We congratulate the "Strand Magazine" on the attainment of its hundredth issue, and we join with Sir George Newnes in the expressibn of gratification at this important incident which he gives vent to in his brief chat about the "Strand's "history. It should be remarked here that the present number of this widely-read magazine is indicative of the continu- ous improvement manifested in the contents since part 1. came from the machine room at Burleigh- street. Articles of a specially interesting character are " Letters of Burne-Jones to a Child„"and " Two Great Railway Sensations." Jr. Doyle's " Story of the Latin Tutor," too, is one of the best he has written in the series now occupying the premier pages of the "Strand." "Hilda Wede" bids fair to be- come as popular a character as Lois Cayley, and Mr Grant Allen certainly seems to have it the mark with this class of continued story. "Liquid Air," is the title of an article when treats of a substance strange to 999 out of every 1,000 human beings, and for that reason is all the more attractive. Numerous other items of distine- tive'merit help to fill the pages of a bulky and readable number.- For remarkable stories "The Wide World Magazine "still holds premier place in the field of fiction. The principal contents this month are "Da Rougemont," "Me Holy Week Pro- cession in Seville," "My Texan Elopement," "A Naturalist in Cannibal Land." "Through Pygmy Land,'" gound the World in a Home-made Boat,"and "Attacked by Le ches."—With characteristic enter- wise Messrs. Newnes and .Co. have started a new sixpenny magaznie called "The Captain," intended for the amusement of boys and "old boys." It is a healthy, attractive production from cover to cover and deserves support from the quarters it aims to reach. Copiously illustrated, and possessing 112 pages of useful and entertaining matter, it makes a very goad eixpsnnyworth. Thera are a couple of serials, one d !soteci to the charms and excitements of military life, and the other to school life. C. B. Fry and Eugene Sandow are among the contri- butors to Part 1., which contains about thirty separate items.</p>
        <p>SMITH, ELDER AND CO —The April number of " Cornhill "abounds in acceptable reading matter. Interesting information is given by Lieutenant Hopkinson, of the Slaforth Highlanders. with re- spect to "The Sirdar's Camel Corps," the ramifica- tions of this useful body of servitors being more elaborate than is generally realised by civilians. " Tne Shakespear3 First Folio: Some Notes and a Disc werv: by Sidney Lee, being part of a lecture delivered by the writlr in London in February, will be attractive reading tl students of the immortal dramatist. The other items in a full list of special articles and stories ara also instructive and read- able.</p>
        <p>J. WHITAKER AND SONS.—We have received " Whitaker's Naval and Military Directory ani Indian List " which is a recent and useful addition to the excellent hooks of reference that have fol- lowed in the wake of the famoos " Almanack." This edition for 1899 has been considerably enlarged and extended, and now contains almost everything of interest and general utility which is known about the cmposition of the two services. Price 5s</p>
        <p>LATEST NEWS.</p>
        <p>THE BUDGET, THE CHLNCELLOR OF TEIE EXCHEQUER'S SPEECH.</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons, yesterday, Sir Michael Hicks Beach, rose at five minutes past four to make his Budget statement,</p>
        <p>Tile Exchange Company says that the statement of revenue and expenditure to be laid before tie House of Commons bp the Chancellor of the Exchequer shows that the total expenditure for 1898-9 was £108,150,000. The revenue for the same period (Exchequer receipts) amounted to £108,336,000, and therefore the revenue exceeded the expenditure by £186,000. As to the esti- mated expenditure for 1899-1990, the Budget estimate is £112,627.000, as against £106,829,000 for 1898.9. The Budget estimate of expenditure for 1899 0 includes £20,617,000 for the Army, £26,595,000 for the Navy. and £3.638,000 for the telegraphic service. SUICIDE' THE DAY AFTER. MIRRIA.GE. Mr. Caudwell, for many years a leading man in a seed firm at Rtading, shot himself deal yesterday. He married his second wife, who had been his housekeeper for three years, only on Wednesday. ANOTHER FIRE IN NEW YORK.</p>
        <p>NEW YORE, THURSDAY. A fire brake out last night at the Customs Elouge hers The building was destroyed, and though the damage is no: great many 6f the old records are lost.</p>
        <p>-FATALITY AT THE SALT UNION WORKS.</p>
        <p>A fatal accident °enured at the Salt Union Works, at Winsford, yesterday morning. A man named Samuel Baker, aged 72 years, who had been Employed as horse driver for many years, was assisting to shunt waggons on the meadow works shortly before eight 0',1102k, when he was crushed bEtween a waggon and fence, and sue- tam rt severe injuries. He died within half an hour.</p>
        <p>LATEST LOCAL 'WEATHER FO-REd.:I.ST. The following forecast of the weather for the ens _ling twenty-lour hours in the north-west of Elgiand was issued at the Meceorological OffiC3 at three o'clock yes- terday afternoon :</p>
        <p>North-westerly and northerly winds, filer ; o)lder.</p>
        <p>FATAL RESULT OF A FALL—THE INQUEST. At the Warrington Borough Police station on Tuesday, Mr. Brighouse, coroner, held an inquiry touching the death of Elizabeth Hockenhull (67), a widow, who resided at 9,Orchard-street which took place at her residence on Saturday morning, at seven o'clock. On the Ist July the deceased slipped while shopping in the town,and fractured her right thigh. She was taken to the Infirmary, where she remained about eight weeks, after which she went home. Two months ago,while getting into bed,sho fell and injured the same limb, being again re- moved to the Infirmary for treatment. She returned home, but gradually grew worse, and died at the time stated.—William B.ockenhull (deceased's son) said his mother had complained about the leg which was injured, feeling weak. She had Come home from her sister's when she fell down and he assisted her into bed. She was afraid of stumbling. her leg being so weak. He was satisfied both the injuries were accidental.—Dr. Langdale said the deceased had died from the resul's of her injuries, and the jury returned a verdict of •• Accidental death."</p>
