        <head>ANNUAL SALE OF WORK..</head>
        <p>ANNUAL SALE OF WORK..</p>
        <p>ST._ PAUL'S CdU RC 11,</p>
        <p>_ The annual sale of work in cmnection with St. ?aura Church, Bewsey-r lad, was held on Monday afternoon in the K-sndric a-street school rooms. The collection of articles on the various stalls,whieh were most tastefully laid out, compared favourably with the sales of work .of previous years, and the attendance augured well for the Success of the present one. The stalls presided over as follows :—No 1 stall : Mesdames Gibson Smith, Buckton, Chorlay, and Lord. No. 2 stall : The Misses Geddes and Mills. Blanchet. No. 3 stall : The Misses and Master Arthur Piot (St. Paul's Vicarage), and Misses Pigot (Greenbank. ('irapnenhall).N stall : Mrs,and the Misses Firmer. No 5 stall:Vs-o.meg Richarlson,llfiss Atherton(Folly-la.ne), and Mrs. J. Atherton. No. 6 stall : Miss 1., vthgoe, Miss Craik, Mrs a inkirma, aod Miss Ashurst. Flower stall: Miss R 'alb me, Miss Wainwright, and Miss D. Reid. Refreshment stall : Miss Westwell. the Misses Beesley, Miss lioughtoi and Miss Piekton. Bran pie : The Misses Annie and Jeanie Chautry, and Miss Gladys Hankinson. There were present on the platform Mr. Rohert Davies, the Revs. H. V. Pot who presidsd,E.R.Tarbuck, and E. C. E. Carieton.Gibson Smith, and Messrs. D.W. Finney, Chantrv, and R. J. Chorley. _</p>
        <p>The Rev. H. V. PIGOT said they were repeating that day at their annual sal of work, munh *".h.l same pro• gramme that they went througa the first oicasion in which he was present at a sale of work th.re. That was in 1891. He wis fr.shly apeointed to the living than and was very pleasel to have the presence and support of Mr. Robert Davies at that tim..as be was the prin?.ipal tante of the palTonage ot the churei and was one of the best friends the ptrish of S. Paul's otv.r had.,Mr.Tarbuck o it upiel pretty much the same pasition as he (the speaker) dii eight yea.ra ago-_v l was about to take the entire charge of that great parish. The ob- ject,: for which they appealed to them wt-r..) necessary and he hoped tie money that would be taken would he ample The expense of getting up the sale of work was so amall that almost every penny they laid oat in buying beautiful things would go stright to the aid for which it was intended. (Applause.) They had to mike a small grant to Miss VVes- w -It far inission work in tea parish and that appealed to everybody who knew the work she had done. There was the Additional Curat. Fund. They only hid a small grant from the curat.s .ancilty an that the grant of £5O they had in vie the list 6 years was almos; a necesricy. They als, gave £lO every year to the day schools of which they were proud. There were other s nall-r amounts not ipeess try t rnia. Una, bat the parish was largely clependeat upon the Ellet;el4 of that sal of work. (Applarise.)</p>
        <p>Mr. RCHIERT DaviEs said he came before the n to nl.ad the good old cause that will I t,t to the end of tn world, and that w..s the want of moony. to carry 011 t different objects of the parish. He knew'mon.y was not every- thing hut what j had don. in the hisbry of. the world ha did know. It had ealancioated slaves, an sent missionsries to the dark c uni-ri of Or wirld, and h., bvirri they would do all th.v could to support Mr. Tarb ink on that onlision. He trilieve I Mr. Tirou.:k and his wife wMI do goo I work i ths parka and h hoped they w 0,11,1 stay as long as Pigot h 1 don'. (Ap- pla.us..) He had intv:h pleaiura in formally the sale open. Aldprman Surrog proposed a vo",e of thanks to Mr. Davies f r his kin loess in openisg the sale f work Mr. FINNEY seconded.</p>
        <p>The It,v. E. IL TAtinuck in supplrting the motion said they would not have Mr. Pigot there as an attraction Rex year, Anil he hoped they•were not giving their support to :no sa'e of work hec tuse of their friendship with Mr. Pigo*, hit because of toe work they saw gotta; on at St. Paht'e Churc 1. That was nit one of the rich parishes of the diocese, hot he felt sure they ha] given their hearty support hi-c Luse they approved of Mr. Pig'ot's Work. Might he ask that next year and in the years to come thoie who had heel kind enough to come fro n outside the narish would C mtinue to help them ? One of the object+ for which the sale was held was the schools. which did a most (xc Aleut work, aod desory d their heartiest support. Mr. Finney hid done a noble work at the schools, and he was glad to be associated with him in the vote a thanks. (Applatsse.) The vote having been cordially carriel,</p>
        <p>Mr. Davies returned thanks. He said he wag proud he w a Warrington man horn and bred, and wae very jealous of tie honour of his native town. He fAt almost indignant because of the epithets umetitnes ap- plied to the town. Lately he had seen the word " de- eradPd " us.d in connection with it on two platforms. He utterly denied that Warrin zton was a degrade town, an 1 trona he ooporunities he had when he had the honour to hold the &amp;Eel of clerk to the magir- 'trates and he went int) the subj-ct carefully, he said, es they ordinarily understood the word morality, Warring- ton was 4 n exceedingly mor4l town, and far from h'irg distinguished by drunkenness or any other vice. (Hear, hear.) The sale was then proceedel with, the takinqs in the lifterno^in and evening amounting to £155 2s. 9,1.</p>
