      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-04-14_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>NOTES ON GAIIIE3 AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAIIIE3 AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>RUGBY' FOOTBALL</p>
        <p>Warrington had no d,ffieulty in defeating Hull Kings. ton Rovers on Saturr•lay. This seems rather surprising on the face of it, but when one comes to make enquiries the performaoce of the home team was not such a start- ling one after all. You see hull only brought one first team p'aver, the other fourt'en being bulky but inex- i”rienced players out of the second team. With all their Internationals, such as Rhodes. Kemp, Morfitt, Lindsay. Starks and others, absent, Kingston Rovers are naturally not a very powerful combination. But they were nearly good eriough to make a close fight with the weak organisation Warrington placed in the field. Bate and Isherwood were tie only two regular first-tam backs playing, while Naylor and Taylor were absent from the forwards. From one cause or another however, it was nearly as good a team as Warrington could get toaetler. Things are in a parlous state and no mis- take.</p>
        <p>There was a gaol-sized gale cavorting round who, hostilities—l mean friendly operations—started in the presence of a couple of thousand spectators. Mr. Frank Turner took charze of the whistle and put the usual amount of wind into his work. Like other referees he amused himself for a time by enforcing the " play the ball" rule,but finally gave it up as a bad job. And about time too. If anything do"s annoy a chap it is to see one of the home players make a daring save and then caolly insert the ball b tween the feet of the opposing forwards. And all belsuse the rules say he must play the hall. I haws not heard whether it was enforced at the referee's match last week, but I sincerely hope it was. Some o the whistle-blowers must have been nur,- ing bruises last week end in consequence.</p>
        <p>But this is digresdng. The Rovers played with the wind to cornmeoc.o wVii, and by dint of much kicking awl floundering, about they managed to keep Warring- ton more or less on the defence. A penalty goal came rh,-ir way too, and at half-tima they were leading. On changir3g ends the home9ters shapei a bit better,Hocken- hull and Isherwood scoring floe tries, both of which ,-,ere really obtained by the last named. A victory of 6 points to 2 ultimately rested with the home lot.</p>
        <p>It ought really to have bean 20 points to 2. A more unscientific lot of lambkinb the Rover's could hardly hive brought. They were big enough for anything, but like the Spanipeo Armada thei, floundered, and ought to have heel well trounced by their smaller foes, only the latter combined so miscribly. Wilcox was a bit off,but he did not play so badly as some critics want to make out ani will yet be a useful half back in my opinion. I have seen him play two excellent games—at Leigh and Broymhton. His namesake on the Hull side was having a high time of it, and s,erced to quite enjoy dribbling about among his old club mates. He was certainly a useful substitute for the Rovers. Boscow, Bate, and Isherwood were the only other players worth mention- ing no the home &amp;de, Waters being prominent at centre for Hull.</p>
        <p>In c-molusion I mizht parody a popular quotation and say "1 win's a win for a' that"—and certainly they have been sca-c;, enou2l3 of late to inak3 Siturday's suc- cws a sweet one.</p>
        <p>This week's engaeoment is with the Wanderers at Birkenhead. 'Twill tv; a close earns, but not class enough for me. No! Igo to Brouthton to see O'dham and Leigh battle for final place; in the Cup Competition. Toe new "tin pot"—not the: old one—ought to come Lancashirs way this year. There is accommodation for it ,t 01,1 ham and I fancy it witl go there. ;If it does not, well there will be some water-sheddings</p>
        <p>I cannot say that I wss surprised to find the favour- ites, Hull, ,g me down on Saturd,y. They had a very stiff nut to crack at HUIRlet, aid ale which they cauld probably haws manage i all right on their own hearth- stone, but an Leeds it-. proved too hard for them. Huns- let have a reli tble set of backa,and forward they simply swamped the third-porters. Leigh dii splendidly in the same town, where the, whacked the Courchmen com- fortably. Towards the end of the game they were terribly unfortunste. as .firri Coleman broke his right leg in colliding with a brother player. Re will h., badly missed against Oldham. Widnes got screlched. Thor found Oldham in tip-top form,and had their line crossed half-a-iozea times More the day was over. Twenty rysiats to nil was the sum total at the eloie of play. and Jim Hughes was orderei off for foul charging. Rather bad for such a young player ! He has hem ord-red to take a rest tilt the alas, of the season.</p>
        <p>The ssmi-fiaala are Oldham and Leigh at Broughton ; linnalet and Salford at Bradford. My fanc i Oldham and Runslet for the fiva', which will probatAy be played in L.neashile. If these two meet it will be a great struggle between capable teams.</p>
      </div>
